\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK:Japanese Demand Likely To Tighten Oil Products Market\b0\par\par\uc2 1,913 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2011\par 07:46\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 0646 GMT [Dow Jones] Japanese demand for oil products will likely tighten fuel oil and middle distillates markets. As the country's refining sector was hit by Friday's earthquake, Petroleum Association of Japan says it would likely import 450,000 kiloliters of oil products while exports of 650,000 kiloliters up end-March were cancelled; the current supply shortage is mainly related to local logistic problems, and domestic production of light products may be excessive of demand, it says. Japan's total crude runs fall to 2.7 million bbls/day from the pre-quake level of around 4.0 million bbls/day, but will recover to 3.4 million bbl/day by end-March, the industry group adds. Japan is importing gasoline and diesel for prompt delivery; but in the long run, it will need fuel oil also to meet demand from power plants, with almost a quarter of its nuclear power generation capacity shut down after the quake. (max.lin@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0638 GMT [Dow Jones] Singapore's STI is down 1.1% at 2939.35, as regional bourses trade lower due to uncertainty surrounding Japan's damaged nuclear reactors. Though the market remains vulnerable to news flow from Japan, further declines should be limited today as U.S. futures are pointing higher while the Nikkei ends well off its lows, helped by visible efforts to cool the overehating reactors. \uc2 RBC Capital Markets\uc2  says Asian market sentiment "remains very fragile as the Japanese nuclear crisis continues." UOB KayHian says Wednesday's price action, which resulted in a 'doji' candlestick pattern for the STI, "suggests indecisiveness in the direction of market. However, the trend remains downward with several bearish indicators against the index." It says the index could decline to the psychological level of 2900, where it tips support, with resistance at 3100. Market volume is mild at 723 million shares with six fallers per gainer. STI names down over 2.0% include SIA Engineering (S59.SG), CityDev (C09.SG), Golden Agri (E5H.SG), \uc2 CapitaMall Trust\uc2  (C38U.SG), F&N (F99.SG), Hongkong Land (H78.SG). (matthew.allen@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0633 GMT [Dow Jones] ASIAN SUMMARY: Regional markets trimmed earlier losses on hopes of progress in bringing a key Japanese nuclear facility under control. The Nikkei is down 1.4%, the S&P/ASX is off 0.1%, the HSI is down 1.9%, the Kospi is +0.1%, the Taiex is down 0.5%, the Sensex is 0.3% lower, the Shanghai Composite is down 1.3% and the STI is down 1.1%. In \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, the USD/JPY bounced to 79.22 after falling to a record low of 76.25 in early Asia trade amid expectations \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  would \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support the USD. Japanese Finance Minister Yoshihiko Noda said he is closely watching the yen given "nervous movements" and "speculations" in the market. Other Japanese officials claimed the JPY's spike was driven by speculators, not repatriation flows. The USD/CHF hit an all-time low of 0.8922, but later rose to CHF0.9028. The EUR/USD is at 1.3927 vs 1.3896 late Wednesday in New York; the EUR/JPY is at 110.39 from 110.67. Minutes from the BOJ's Feb. 14-15 meeting show the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would continue to support the economy by "pursuing powerful monetary easing." China is likely to face more hot money inflows near term due to its large external surpluses and interest-rate gap with the U.S., says Deng Xianhong, a SAFE vice director. The New Zealand government plans to borrow some NZ$10 billion to rebuild its Christchurch after the February earthquake. Singapore's February non-oil exports rose 7.8% on year vs January's +20.7%, below the 11.2% forecast by a Dow Jones poll. New Zealand's 1Q Consumer Confidence Index fell 10.4 points to 97.4 points, its lowest since 1Q09. Spot gold is at $1,393.90/oz, down $7.60 from the NY close. April Nymex crude oil futures are up 60 cents at $98.58/bbl. (philip.vahn@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0629 GMT [Dow Jones] The USD/HKD is higher on continued demand as players seek safe-haven of U.S. greenback amid uncertainty surrounding Japanese nuclear crisis, says a senior trader at a local bank. He tips the pair to trade in a 7.8000-7.8050 range in the near term. "I've spotted a German bank buying more than US$50 million in total and other investment banks are following its path. There are still many buy orders in the pipeline," the trader says. The pair is at 7.8025 vs 7.7956 in late Asian trade Wednesday. (chester.yung@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0619 GMT [Dow Jones] Asian CDS are wider Thursday as a potent mix of a nuclear crisis in Japan and political tensions in the Middle East keeps investors on the edge of their seats. "There is a flight-to-quality bid for U.S. Treasuries and out of emerging markets because of the aftermath of the Japanese quake and tsunami, as well as escalating tensions in the Middle East," an Asia-based trader says. In Japan, efforts continue to stabilize conditions at the country's quake-crippled nuclear reactors; in Bahrain, several people were killed overnight as security forces crack down on protesters in Manama, and impose a curfew across much of the capital. The Markit iTraxx Asia ex-Japan investment-grade CDS index is at 115 bps, 4 bps wider than Wednesday. Spreads are likely to remain wide for the time being as tensions linger, the trader adds. (natasha.brereton@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0609 GMT [Dow Jones] Nikkei 225 June futures are down 1.0% at 8,910, but well above their intraday low of 8,400 as expectations grow that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  may \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market as Japanese Finance Minister Noda said earlier Thursday that G-7 nations will discuss via teleconference on Friday morning the impact of the massive earthquake on the Japanese economy and financial markets, says Yumi Nishimura, deputy general manager of investment strategy at Daiwa Securities Capital Markets. Overseas investors bought on dips today, preventing the contract from falling further, as efforts to cool down overheating nuclear reactors continues, she says. She tips resistance around 9,000 for the remainder of the week, provided negative investor sentiment continues to dissipate. (kosaku.narioka@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0600 GMT [Dow Jones] PREVIEW: Hong Kong's unemployment rate likely held steady at 3.8% in the December-February period, unchanged from the November-January period, according to median forecast of nine economists polled by Dow Jones. Economists say the city's solid economic growth continues to spur job growth, but the rising price pressures could squeeze the firms' profit margin and cloud the labor market outlook. "If oil and commodities prices continue to skyrocket for a sustained period of time, it could dampen global demand and pose downside risk to our growth and employment forecast," Hang Seng bank chief economist Joanne Yim says. The government is scheduled to issue the unemployment data at 0830 GMT. (chester.yung@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0553 GMT [Dow Jones] The HSI is down 1.5% at 22,372.60, but the index is off its early low of 22,123.26, tracking moves in Japan's Nikkei, supported by hopes of progress in bringing a key Japanese nuclear facility under control, says Prudential Brokerage's associate director Alvin Cheung. He says the local index may have around 22,000 as a near-term support. "For medium-to-long term investors, it might be good to adopt a contrarian view to accumulate at the current level as it is quite attractive." He says the HSI is trading at around 13.2X forward P/E, which is undemanding. Ahead of results later, \uc2 PetroChina\uc2  (0857.HK) is down 0.4% at HK$10.50, while Tencent (0700.HK) is down 9.1% at HK$197.10 after lower-than-expected 4Q results. Volume of the local bourse is brisk at HK$53.15 billion. (susanna.tai@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0549 GMT [Dow Jones] Taiwan shares end down 0.5% at 8282.69, recovering from an early 2.1% fall, as Japan's stock market has reversed most of its early losses, says Concord Securities manager Allan Lin. "The recovering Tokyo stocks helped boost the sentiment here, with most financial and construction shares rebounding sharply," he adds. He notes most-traded TSMC (2330.TW) was off its early low because of buying from government funds in late trade. He tips the Taiex in a 8200-8350 band Friday; "the index must stand above its five-day moving average (at 8350) to stage a solid rebound next week," he adds. TSMC ends down 1.6% at NT$67.40, off an early low of NT$65.70; its rival UMC (2303.TW) is up 2.5% at NT$14.45 while Hon Hai (2317.TW) is down 4.3% at NT$101.00. Chinatrust Financial Holding (2891.TW) is up 2.8% at NT$23.90 and Taishin Financial Holding (2887.TW) is up 2.3% at NT$15.75. \uc2 Farglory Land Development\uc2  (5522.TW) is up 2.8% at NT$59.70 and \uc2 Huang Hsiang Construction\uc2  (2545.TW) is up 3.5% at NT$75.40. (fanny.liu@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0546 GMT [Dow Jones] \uc2 JPMorgan\uc2  now expects the RBA's next rate hike in August, pushing back its forecast from the previous call for May, says Stephen Walters, chief economist at \uc2 JPMorgan\uc2 . He says the uncertainty over the impact of Japan's earthquake and nuclear crisis, and the resulting dislocation in financial markets, mean the probabilities have shifted in favor of a later hike. He adds one thing is clear: the RBA is not about to cut the cash rate, contrary to the assessment of the local futures market, where pricing now implies a 40% chance of an easing by June. He says Australia's upbeat medium-term story is intact. (james.glynn@dowjones.com) [ 03-17-11 0246ET ]\par \par \uc2 0543 GMT [Dow Jones] Thai shares are down 0.4% at 1003.68 at the midday break in moderate trade. The benchmark has been in the negative territory throughout the morning session, pressured by the weakness in regional bourses. Investors are watching developments when the teleconference of G-7 finance ministers and central bankers takes place later, says Capital Nomura Securities' strategist Chai Chirasevenupraphand. He tips psychological support at 1000, then 990. The resistance is put at 1005. Among the most active stocks, Banpu (BANPU.TH) is down 0.5% at THB738, PTT (PTT.TH) is down 0.6% at THB337, Thai Oil (TOP.TH) is down 0.3% at THB80.50, IRPC (IRPC.TH) is down 0.8% at THB5.95 and \uc2 Indorama Ventures\uc2  (IVL.TH) is flat at THB49.75. (piyarat.setthasiriphaiboon@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0538 GMT [Dow Jones] The Petroleum Association of Japan is painting sobering picture of the country's refining problems; it says while throughput is improving, it expects end-March runs to be 600,000 b/d below the 4 million b/d level seen just before the earthquake. (mari.iwata@dowjones.com) [ 03-17-11 0246ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020110317e73h0004a\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
