lar down below Y76 and possibly to Y75." Sahara says that even though JPY buying by Japanese firms to repatriate funds isn't clear, speculation that further nuclear problems could spark such flows would be enough to drive short-term positioning. While sharp USD/JPY falls would increase the chance of MOF \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , "It could be hard for them to do it on Monday during Asian hours with Japan on a holiday." (andrew.monahan@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0504 GMT [Dow Jones] Spot gold is at $1,392.50/oz, down $9 since the NY close, but well off its intraday low of $1,389.80 after heavy Japanese selling early on the back of the ongoing nuclear crisis. The Nikkei-225 is down 2.1% at 8900.09 but the yen is off its highs, taking some pressure off gold. "Although we have stabilized since this morning I still don't think we have seen the low on this move," says a Singapore based trader at an international bank. He tips support at $1,380 and says there may be a test of that with physical buying present, but not in enough force to push gold higher. (james.campbell@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0502 GMT [Dow Jones] Singapore's key non-oil exports rose 7.8% on year in February vs +20.7% in the previous month as shipments of key electronics products decline. February's export growth was below the 11.2% forecast by Dow Jones poll of economists but is unlikely to change MAS thinking on monetary policy as it's close to the expected range of 8%-10% for 2011. The MAS is currently focused on inflation, which is likely to be the key factor for its monetary policy stance at its April meeting. Measured on month, February non-oil exports rose 2.9% in seasonally adjusted terms vs +2.1% on month in January and better than the +1.0% predicted by the poll. Government data show electronics exports down 12.8% on year in February vs +5.8% in January, while non-electronics shipments were +19.7%, slowing from +30.3% in January. Pharmaceutical exports, which are typically volatile, were nearly flat at +1.3% on year vs +38.6% in January. (gaurav.raghuvanshi@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0457 GMT [Dow Jones] India's overnight indexed swaps are lower, tracking a fall in U.S. treasury yields and a decline in crude oil prices, but the market is keenly awaiting the RBI's rate decision, due 630 GMT, for further cues, says a trader at a state-run bank. One-year OIS are at 7.31%/7.34% from 7.36%/7.38% in late Asian trade Wednesday. Five-year OIS are at 7.80%/7.84% from 7.87%/7.90%. "The market has factored in a 25bp hike and most don't expect any hawkish statement from RBI that will hurt economic growth," the trader says. He adds, the OIS market has factored in an acute cash crunch in the banking system due to company's advance tax outflows, but traders expect the cash to flow back to the system in the last 10 days of the fiscal year in the form of government spending. But he cautions, the market can swing either way on any surprises at the RBI meeting. He tips the one-year OIS in a 7.30%-7.40% band for the session. (sourav.mishra@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0448 GMT [Dow Jones] UBS Securities strategist Atsushi Ito estimates the supplementary budget for post-quake reconstruction to be around Y5.8 trillion, funded by Y3.6 trillion in over-budget JGB issuance in FY10, Y1.2 trillion in reserves and Y1 trillion from reduced spending by scaling back the child allowance and shelving the plan to abolish expressway tolls. Despite oversupply fears, hopes additional BOJ easing is expected to support shorter-end JGBs, while Wednesday's 20-year sale confirms solid demand for superlong bonds with high yields. Ito says "we therefore do not anticipate any major rise in yields beyond their current levels when the first supplementary budget is compiled," but "the government will need to maintain its focus on fiscal reform by formally revising the (medium-term fiscal frameworks), since the current spending cap of Y71 trillion will be shattered."(megumi.