\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: USD/KRW Volatility Eases As Exporters Kick-In\b0\par\par\uc2 1,650 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2011\par 06:28\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 0528 GMT [Dow Jones] Investment sentiment towards USD/KRW is somewhat stabilizing now after South Korean \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  authorities warned against volatile moves and as Japan's nuclear plant-related problems appear to be coming under control, say traders. The USD/KRW is at 1,136.75 vs 1,130.8 late yesterday, and the year-high of 1,144.0 tapped earlier in the day. There was "explosive" volume of export settlements when the pair was around 1,141 and 1,142, which drew it from its year-high and prompted offshore players to cut long-USD positions, says local bank trader; he tips the pair in a 1,135-1,140 for the rest of session. A foreign bank trader says the market turned calm in the afternoon along with the suspected BOK \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  earlier and the Kospi's rebound from its day-low. "The overnight uneasiness over Japan's nuke crisis extended into the morning session, but as the pair reverses course after marking the year-high, the market's trimming its volatility." (kanga.kong@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0517 GMT [Dow Jones] Singapore non-oil exports are likely to remain volatile, but the full-year government target of 8%-10% growth can be achieved, CIMB economist Song Seng Wun tells Dow Jones after government data show February exports slowed to +7.8% on year from +20.7% in January as shipments of key electronics products declined. "To some extent, this is because of the Chinese New Year holidays and we should read the cumulative numbers, which are not too bad. The beginning of the year is typically a slower period and the Japanese earthquake may increase volatility in the second quarter because of supply disruptions in the electronics industry," Song says. Data show electronics exports down 12.8% on year in February from +5.8% in January, while non-electronics shipments are +19.7%, slowing from +30.3% in January. (gaurav.raghuvanshi@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0514 GMT [Dow Jones] Standard Chartered now expects the outcome of the MAS meeting in April to maintain its current policy, compared with the house's previous view for some form of tightening. "The key reason for our change in the call is that the developments in Japan have raised the growth risks to the region as confidence may be hit," Standard Chartered says in a note, adding the "impact on confidence will dominate domestic inflation concerns until we have a clear resolution to the situation in Japan." Standard Chartered previously expected MAS to recenter its policy band at the prevailing USD/SGD level and a narrowing of the NEER. It now expects the SGD to weaken further, both near-term and after the MAS meeting, as the market has been long the SGD on expectations of further tightening. The USD/SGD is at 1.2826. (samuel.holmes@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0514 GMT [Dow Jones] Indonesian shares are down 1.5% at 3477.399 in moderate volume, off an intraday low of 3465.614, led by foreign selling in most blue chips amid growing concerns over Japan's nuclear crisis, traders say. They expect the main index to bottom out at 3550. "Bargain buying by local funds in select bank blue chips has lifted the main index from its lows," says a trader with Reliance Securities. Foreigners are net sellers of IDR253 billion worth of shares. Among decliners: heavyweight Telkom (TLKM.JK) is down 3.6% at IDR6,800 on worries that stiffer competition could hurt company's earnings, \uc2 Bank Mandiri\uc2  (BMRI.JK) is down 2.5% at IDR5,950, while Bank Rakyat (BBRI.JK) is down 1.0% at IDR5,050. (edhi.pranasidhi@dowjones.com)\par