\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: Reactor Woes Could Send USD/JPY To 75 - Dealer\b0\par\par\uc2 1,755 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2011\par 06:11\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 0511 GMT [Dow Jones] Any substantial worsening of Japan's Fukushima Daiichi nuclear reactor problems over the coming days could send the USD/JPY down around 75.00 early next week, with a national holiday in \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Monday complicating any effort to conduct JPY-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  if such a fall forced the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 's hand, says Mitsuru Sahara, a senior \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealer at \uc2 Bank of Tokyo-Mitsubishi\uc2  UFJ. "The next few days will be crucial with the nuclear reactor situation. If current efforts to cool the reactors fail and there are meltdowns, there could be panic early next week that would drive the dollar down below Y76 and possibly to Y75." Sahara says that even though JPY buying by Japanese firms to repatriate funds isn't clear, speculation that further nuclear problems could spark such flows would be enough to drive short-term positioning. While sharp USD/JPY falls would increase the chance of MOF \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , "It could be hard for them to do it on Monday during Asian hours with Japan on a holiday." (andrew.monahan@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0504 GMT [Dow Jones] Spot gold is at $1,392.50/oz, down $9 since the NY close, but well off its intraday low of $1,389.80 after heavy Japanese selling early on the back of the ongoing nuclear crisis. The Nikkei-225 is down 2.1% at 8900.09 but the yen is off its highs, taking some pressure off gold. "Although we have stabilized since this morning I still don't think we have seen the low on this move," says a Singapore based trader at an international bank. He tips support at $1,380 and says there may be a test of that with physical buying present, but not in enough force to push gold higher. (james.campbell@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0502 GMT [Dow Jones] Singapore's key non-oil exports rose 7.8% on year in February vs +20.7% in the previous month as shipments of key electronics products decline. February's export growth was below the 11.2% forecast by Dow Jones poll of economists but is unlikely to change MAS thinking on monetary policy as it's close to the expected range of 8%-10% for 2011. The MAS is currently focused on inflation, which is likely to be the key factor for its monetary policy stance at its April meeting. Measured on month, February non-oil exports rose 2.9% in seasonally adjusted terms vs +2.1% on month in January and better than the +1.0% predicted by the poll. Government data show electronics exports down 12.8% on year in February vs +5.8% in January, while non-electronics shipments were +19.7%, slowing from +30.3% in January. Pharmaceutical exports, which are typically volatile, were nearly flat at +1.3% on year vs +38.6% in January. (gaurav.raghuvanshi@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0457 GMT [Dow Jones] India's overnight indexed swaps are lower, tracking a fall in U.S. treasury yields and a decline in crude oil prices, but the market is keenly awaiting the RBI's rate decision, due 630 GMT, for further cues, says a trader at a state-run bank. One-year OIS are at 7.31%/7.34% from 7.36%/7.38% in late Asian trade Wednesday. Five-year OIS are at 7.80%/7.84% from 7.87%/7.90%. "The market has factored in a 25bp hike and most don't expect any hawkish statement from RBI that will hurt economic growth," the trader says. He adds, the OIS market has factored in an acute cash crunch in the banking system due to company's advance tax outflows, but traders expect the cash to flow back to the system in the last 10 days of the fiscal year in the form of government spending. But he cautions, the market can swing either way on any surprises at the RBI meeting. He tips the one-year OIS in a 7.30%-7.40% band for the session. (sourav.mishra@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0448 GMT [Dow Jones] UBS Securities strategist Atsushi Ito estimates the supplementary budget for post-quake reconstruction to be around Y5.8 trillion, funded by Y3.6 trillion in over-budget JGB issuance in FY10, Y1.2 trillion in reserves and Y1 trillion from reduced spending by scaling back the child allowance and shelving the plan to abolish expressway tolls. Despite oversupply fears, hopes additional BOJ easing is expected to support shorter-end JGBs, while Wednesday's 20-year sale confirms solid demand for superlong bonds with high yields. Ito says "we therefore do not anticipate any major rise in yields beyond their current levels when the first supplementary budget is compiled," but "the government will need to maintain its focus on fiscal reform by formally revising the (medium-term fiscal frameworks), since the current spending cap of Y71 trillion will be shattered."(megumi.fujikawa@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0440 GMT [Dow Jones] India's interbank call rate hits an over four-month high of 7.75% as liquidity in the banking system tightens as companies make large withdrawals to pay advance tax, says a state-run bank trader. The overnight call rate is at 7.75% from 7.25% at Wednesday's close. The collateralized borrowing and lending obligation or CBLO rate is at 6.55%, unchanged from the open. The call and CBLO rates are both higher than the RBI's 6.50% lending rate. "Apart from tight availability of cash, the call rate is reflecting that banks have priced in a 25 basis point rate increase by the RBI today. Traders now hope that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will extend its liquidity easing measures beyond the April 8 deadline," she says. She tips the call rate in 7.75%-7.90% band. Indian banks' borrowing from the RBI's discount window crossed the INR1 trillion-mark this week. (khushita.vasant@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0439 GMT [Dow Jones] The KLCI is 0.4% lower at 1487.22 at midday, in line with weaker regional markets on growing concerns over the potential nuclear catastrophe facing Japan. "There is some talk of Japan being able to contain the disaster, so we are seeing some buyers coming in. But we should wait to get a clearer picture," a local dealer says. He says the index's immediate support remains the Nov. 2010 low at 1474. Market breadth is negative with 491 decliners vs 141 gainers. Gamuda (5398.KU) is down 1.6% at MYR3.63, CIMB Group (1023.KU) is down 1.1% at MYR7.91, \uc2 AMMB Holdings\uc2  (1015.KU) is down 1.1% at MYR6.22 and Malaysian Airline (3786.KU) is down 1.1% at MYR1.80. Public Bank (1295.KU) is up 0.8% at MYR16.94 and \uc2 MMC Corp\uc2 . (2194.KU) is up 0.8% at MYR2.66. (ankur.relia@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0438 GMT [Dow Jones] Singapore's STI is down 1.1% at 2939.33 midday as concerns over Japan's nuclear crisis continue to weigh on sentiment. Volume is moderate at 648 million shares worth S$751 million, with fallers trumping gainers by over 6 to 1. DMG says there needs to be clear evidence that the situation at Japan's damaged nuclear reactors is under control before stability returns to the market. However, it notes "the STI remains attractive on a 12-month timeframe and (the) recent correction is a chance to accumulate." The house notes thermal coal prices for April delivery have risen 10% since Friday and is positive in the short-term for stocks like \uc2 Straits Asia Resources\uc2  (AJ1.SG), rated Neutral with a S$2.49 target, and Noble (N21.SG), rated Buy with a S$2.58 target. The stocks are down 1.6% at S$2.45, and down 1.0% at S$2.03, respectively. Genting Singapore (G13.SG) and \uc2 Golden Agri-Resources\uc2  (E5H.SG) are down 2.1%-2.3% while defensive dividend play StarHub (CC3.SG) is up 0.8% at S$2.62. STI support tipped at 2932, then 2915. (matthew.allen@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0434 GMT [Dow Jones] The Nikkei is down 2.1% at 8900.09, vs its midday close of 8903.86, as USD/JPY recovers somewhat (now 78.98), while efforts to prevent further overheating at Japan's troubled nuclear power plants appear to be making progress, says Kazuhiro Takahashi, general manager of investment strategy at Daiwa Securities Capital Markets. "Investors are regaining calmness as panic-driven selling in dollar-yen eases while the nuclear situation looks less dire," he says. The Nikkei may rise to 9,000 later in the day, he adds, but says players will likely need to see more proof of concrete and lasting resolution to the nuke crisis. 27/33 Topix subindexes are lower, with some recovery-related and domestic demand shares higher. Drugmaker Shionogi (4507.TO) is up 5.1% at Y1,338, \uc2 Central Japan Railway\uc2  (9022.TO) is up 1.8% at Y625,000, \uc2 Yokogawa Electric\uc2  (6841.TO) is up 9.6% at Y583, \uc2 Japan Steel Works\uc2  (5631.TO) is up 4.9% at Y533. \uc2 Tokyo Electric Power\uc2  (9501.TO) is now well off its lows, albeit still down 13% at Y802 on more than 30 times normal volume. Other utilities are up strongly; \uc2 Tohoku Electric Power\uc2  (9506.TO) is up 7.0% at Y1,484.(kosaku.narioka@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0434 GMT [Dow Jones] Philippine shares end lower, extending their decline to five consecutive sessions following sharp declines on Wall Street overnight. The benchmark PSEi is down 1.6% at 3817.38, with all of the sub-indices posting declines. Market breadth is negative with 114 decliners compared to 22 advancers, while 31 issues are unchanged. Grace Cerdenia, research head with 2TradeAsia.com believes buyers will come out at the 3800 level should the index heads towards that support level Friday. "But the positioning would be gradual depending on the extent of news (abroad)," she says. She tips next resistance at 3900. PLDT (TEL.PH) ended down 2.7% at PHP2,014, SM Investments (SM.PH) ended off 1.0% at PHP510.50, and Alliance Global (AGI.PH) ended 2.8% lower at PHP11.56. (rhea.sandique-carlos@dowjones.com) [ 03-17-11 0111ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020110317e73h0003n\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
