e country's interest rates. The Shanghai Stock Exchange government bond index is at 127.21 vs 127.22 at its last close and it's likely to stay in a 127.15-127.21 band. (ming.wang@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0215 GMT [Dow Jones] New Zealand government bond prices are lower, while interest rate swaps are higher after comments by Finance Minister Bill English on debt ahead of the weekly Debt Management Office tender, a local bond trader says. English said the government would have to borrow the majority of the money needed to rebuild Christchurch and would increase borrowing to NZ$300 million a week. The trader says the weekly bond tender was "a mixed bag" after the comments with the successful yield spread reaching 5 bps. "Since the tender they've (bond yields and swaps) all moved higher to reflect where the average was in the tender." The NZ Debt Management Office sold NZ$75 million of bonds due March 15, 2019, at an average yield of 5.22% and NZ$175 million of May 15, 2021, bonds at a average yield of 5.43%. (lucy.craymer@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0215 GMT [Dow Jones] The USD/JPY's decline to the 76.25 low was mainly due to a lack of JPY-selling investors, says Satoshi Tate, a senior dealer at \uc2 Mizuho Corporate Bank\uc2 . "We no longer have active Japanese long-term investors who have been aggressively buying assets overseas"; he adds "this is the main cause of the dollar falling to a surprising level of Y76.25. Improving these investors' risk-taking appetite is a must for the dollar to recover further." He says \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  MOF \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is possible, "as it makes sense that the authority provides yen-selling liquidity instead of long-term investors." But speculation is mounting over a G-7 teleconference later in the day. "Japan's MOF is unusually quite so far, and since we have the G-7 thing coming up, this could be the calm before the storm. There could be a major surprise from the G-7