\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: Japan May Sell JPY To Push USD/JPY Above 80.00\b0\par\par\uc2 1,668 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2011\par 03:34\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0234 GMT [Dow Jones] A target of any \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  will be to push the USD/JPY above 80.0 and let it stabilize around that level, says Nomura Securities chief \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategist Taisuke Tanaka. Still, large amounts of funds are necessary for \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to fulfill that because hedge-related USD/JPY selling by Japanese exporters is likely to weigh on the pair at 80.00. He also notes joint \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  by \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , U.S. and EU is possible, given the Japan-led equities weakness around the world. He adds after that the crisis is settled, the USD/JPY may stabilize in a 80.00-85.00 band. (takashi.mochizuki@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0231 GMT [Dow Jones] Vietnam shares are down 0.4% at 461.48, with selling in select blue-chip stocks on continued concerns over the negative impact from Japan's earthquake. Dealers say sentiment is also low because a government economist predicts banks will continue to maintain high interest rates in the second quarter because inflation is likely to remain high. "Fears of a withdrawal of Japanese investors and high inflation are discouraging many players from entering the market these days," sayd a Hanoi-based brokerage trader. Among leading decliners, \uc2 Bao Viet Holdings\uc2  (BVH.VH) is down 4.9% at VND78,000, Vinpearl (VPL.VH) is down 1.6% at VND63,000 and \uc2 PetroVietnam\uc2  fertilizer (DPM.VH) is down 0.6% at VND36,300. The trader tips immediate support for the main index at 460. (phammuoi.nguyen@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0231 GMT [Dow Jones] Philippine shares are falling as investors continue to fret about the nuclear situation in Japan. The benchmark PSEi is off 1.4% at 3823.19 in light volume. Losers are swamping gainers 91 to 12. "We will probably see more of this weakness until the uncertainty in Japan recedes," says Bernard Avinante, analyst at Wealth Securities. Traders place immediate support at 3800 and resistance at 3900. Among heavyweight losers, SM Investments (SM.PH) is down 1.0% at PHP510.50, property developer Megaworld (MEG.PH) is down 1.4% at PHP2.13, PLDT (TEL.PH) is down 3.1% at PHP2,006, and Metropolitan Bank & Trust (MBT.PH) is down 0.9% to PHP61.20. (cris.larano@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0219 GMT [Dow Jones] Spot gold is at $1,389.20/oz, down $12.30 since the New York close. Continuing liquidation on Tocom after the USD/JPY lurched lower Wednesday is putting spot under pressure in a similar pattern to Tuesday's trade as Japan investors move to cash. Tocom February 2012 contract is at Y3,561 a gram, down Y85 and has fallen 6.0% since Friday's pre-earthquake levels, reflecting the stronger yen, while spot gold has fallen 1.6%. \uc2 Barclays Capital\uc2  says technically gold has important support at $1,376, a break of which would signal a deeper correction to $1,360 or even $1,325. "Strategically, we are bullish, but there is not enough in the overnight price action to buy for anything other than the short term, as we would like to keep open the option of buying a dip to $1,325," the bank says.(james.campbell@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0218 GMT [Dow Jones] China government bonds are lower after the PBOC raises the size on its routine three-month bills sold in the open market \b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2  to CNY50 billion (from CNY32 billion last week), the largest since late October. "The sizable bill sale amount indicates the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's long-term efforts to mop up flush liquidity," says a Shanghai-based trader with a local fund. Still, he reckons the unchanged yield on the three-month PBOC bills suggests that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  probably has no imminent plan to hike the country's interest rates. The Shanghai Stock Exchange government bond index is at 127.21 vs 127.22 at its last close and it's likely to stay in a 127.15-127.21 band. (ming.wang@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0215 GMT [Dow Jones] New Zealand government bond prices are lower, while interest rate swaps are higher after comments by Finance Minister Bill English on debt ahead of the weekly Debt Management Office tender, a local bond trader says. English said the government would have to borrow the majority of the money needed to rebuild Christchurch and would increase borrowing to NZ$300 million a week. The trader says the weekly bond tender was "a mixed bag" after the comments with the successful yield spread reaching 5 bps. "Since the tender they've (bond yields and swaps) all moved higher to reflect where the average was in the tender." The NZ Debt Management Office sold NZ$75 million of bonds due March 15, 2019, at an average yield of 5.22% and NZ$175 million of May 15, 2021, bonds at a average yield of 5.43%. (lucy.craymer@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0215 GMT [Dow Jones] The USD/JPY's decline to the 76.25 low was mainly due to a lack of JPY-selling investors, says Satoshi Tate, a senior dealer at \uc2 Mizuho Corporate Bank\uc2 . "We no longer have active Japanese long-term investors who have been aggressively buying assets overseas"; he adds "this is the main cause of the dollar falling to a surprising level of Y76.25. Improving these investors' risk-taking appetite is a must for the dollar to recover further." He says \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  MOF \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is possible, "as it makes sense that the authority provides yen-selling liquidity instead of long-term investors." But speculation is mounting over a G-7 teleconference later in the day. "Japan's MOF is unusually quite so far, and since we have the G-7 thing coming up, this could be the calm before the storm. There could be a major surprise from the G-7." He adds liquidity in the market is still thin, so the USD/JPY is likely to fall below 76.25 if there's no \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . (takashi.mochizuki@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0210 GMT [Dow Jones] The USD/CNY falls a tad on lower fixing at 6.5690 vs Wednesday's 6.5718. In OTC trade, the USD/CNY is at 6.5704 vs 6.5713 late Wednesday. A Beijing-based local bank trader tips it in a narrow 6.5690-6.5720 band; "we may see a series of sideways fixings around 6.5700 for the rest of the month," he adds. Offshore, the one-year USD/CNY NDFs are up at 6.4660/6.4700 vs 6.4550/6.4600 late Wednesday. (jean.yung@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0208 GMT [Dow Jones] The USD/HKD rises to a five-month high at 7.8018 vs 7.7956 late Wednesday as some foreign investment banks buy on risk aversion as concerns over Japan's nuclear crisis escalate. He expects the pair to trade in a 7.8000-7.8050 band in the near term. "The buying of the U.S. unit hasn't eased and could push up the pair later," the trader says.(chester.yung@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0207 GMT [Dow Jones] SHFE base metals are mixed, with copper switching between positive and negative territory on the back of declining domestic and regional equities which are dragged down by mounting worries that Japan's nuclear crisis is spiralling out of control. Analysts say copper's resilience during a time of natural disasters and geopolitical instability indicates investors look beyond short-term demand disruption and focus on the long-term demand prospects from Japanese reconstruction in the wake of the earthquake and tsunami. But Minmetals Futures analyst Zhuo Guiqiu says latent demand in physical trade may keep possible gains capped in the near term; Zhuo tips nearby support for copper at CNY68,000/ton. Benchmark June copper is up 0.2% at CNY69,970/ton; benchmark June zinc is down 1.5% at CNY17,975/ton(yue.li@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0156 GMT [Dow Jones] Taiwan shares are down 1.4% at 8211.78, recovering from an early low of 8150.73 due to bargain hunting and strength in chemicals stocks amid a sell-off in heavyweight techs, says Capital Securities assistant vice-president Diana Wu. She tips Taiex to find support at 8070 today as some investors are still willing to buy at lows; "but the Taiex will fall further to 7800 in the near term if Japan's nuclear crisis deteriorates." Electronics plays lead declines on concerns over end-demand and component supply after the Japan quake, with TSMC (2330.TW) down 3.1% at NT$66.40 and HTC (2498.TW) down 3.2% at NT$997.00. Chemicals stocks shine on strength in commodity prices, with \uc2 Asia Polymer\uc2  (1308.TW) limit-up 7% at NT$48.10 and Taita Chemicals (1309.TW) up 6.7% at NT$12.80. (lorraine.luk@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0156 GMT [Dow Jones] The HSI is down 2.1% at 22,217.28 after sliding to a fresh 2011 low of 22,123.26 earlier, tracking weakness in overseas markets on continued concerns over Japan's nuclear crisis. At this point, it's perhaps better for investors to "turn off the screen, and take a look at (their) portfolios to see if there will be a long-lasting impact on the earnings of the companies they hold," says a local fund manager. Quake impact aside, \uc2 Citigroup\uc2  advises buying into China stocks' weakness "as (the) liquidity stretch is near its end." 44 of 45 blue chips are down led by Tencent's (0700.HK) 6.8% tumble to HK$202.00 after its 4Q earnings misses view; China Mobile (0941.HK) falls 3.2% to HK$68.80 on dividend disappointment and higher capex. Market volume is strong at HK$13.23 billion. (robert.li@dowjones.com) [ 03-16-11 2234ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020110317e73h0001y\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
