\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: China Bonds Down;PBOC Mopping Up Liquidity - Trader\b0\par\par\uc2 1,628 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2011\par 03:18\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0218 GMT [Dow Jones] China government bonds are lower after the PBOC raises the size on its routine three-month bills sold in the open market \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operation\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to CNY50 billion (from CNY32 billion last week), the largest since late October. "The sizable bill sale amount indicates the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's long-term efforts to mop up flush liquidity," says a Shanghai-based trader with a local fund. Still, he reckons the unchanged yield on the three-month PBOC bills suggests that the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  probably has no imminent plan to hike the country's interest rates. The Shanghai Stock Exchange government bond index is at 127.21 vs 127.22 at its last close and it's likely to stay in a 127.15-127.21 band. (ming.wang@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0215 GMT [Dow Jones] New Zealand government bond prices are lower, while interest rate swaps are higher after comments by Finance Minister Bill English on debt ahead of the weekly Debt Management Office tender, a local bond trader says. English said the government would have to borrow the majority of the money needed to rebuild Christchurch and would increase borrowing to NZ$300 million a week. The trader says the weekly bond tender was "a mixed bag" after the comments with the successful yield spread reaching 5 bps. "Since the tender they've (bond yields and swaps) all moved higher to reflect where the average was in the tender." The NZ Debt Management Office sold NZ$75 million of bonds due March 15, 2019, at an average yield of 5.22% and NZ$175 million of May 15, 2021, bonds at a average yield of 5.43%. (lucy.craymer@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0215 GMT [Dow Jones] The USD/JPY's decline to the 76.25 low was mainly due to a lack of JPY-selling investors, says Satoshi Tate, a senior dealer at \uc2 Mizuho Corporate Bank\uc2 . "We no longer have active Japanese long-term investors who have been aggressively buying assets overseas"; he adds "this is the main cause of the dollar falling to a surprising level of Y76.25. Improving these investors' risk-taking appetite is a must for the dollar to recover further." He says \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  MOF \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is possible, "as it makes sense that the authority provides yen-selling liquidity instead of long-term investors." But speculation is mounting over a G-7 teleconference later in the day. "Japan's MOF is unusually quite so far, and since we have the G-7 thing coming up, this could be the calm before the storm. There could be a major surprise from the G-7." He adds liquidity in the market is still thin, so the USD/JPY is likely to fall below 76.25 if there's no \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . (takashi.mochizuki@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0210 GMT [Dow Jones] The USD/CNY falls a tad on lower fixing at 6.5690 vs Wednesday's 6.5718. In OTC trade, the USD/CNY is at 6.5704 vs 6.5713 late Wednesday. A Beijing-based local bank trader tips it in a narrow 6.5690-6.5720 band; "we may see a series of sideways fixings around 6.5700 for the rest of the month," he adds. Offshore, the one-year USD/CNY NDFs are up at 6.4660/6.4700 vs 6.4550/6.4600 late Wednesday. (jean.yung@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0208 GMT [Dow Jones] The USD/HKD rises to a five-month high at 7.8018 vs 7.7956 late Wednesday as some foreign investment banks buy on risk aversion as concerns over Japan's nuclear crisis escalate. He expects the pair to trade in a 7.8000-7.8050 band in the near term. "The buying of the U.S. unit hasn't eased and could push up the pair later," the trader says.(chester.yung@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0207 GMT [Dow Jones] SHFE base metals are mixed, with copper switching between positive and negative territory on the back of declining domestic and regional equities which are dragged down by mounting worries that Japan's nuclear crisis is spiralling out of control. Analysts say copper's resilience during a time of natural disasters and geopolitical instability indicates investors look beyond short-term demand disruption and focus on the long-term demand prospects from Japanese reconstruction in the wake of the earthquake and tsunami. But Minmetals Futures analyst Zhuo Guiqiu says latent demand in physical trade may keep possible gains capped in the near term; Zhuo tips nearby support for copper at CNY68,000/ton. Benchmark June copper is up 0.2% at CNY69,970/ton; benchmark June zinc is down 1.5% at CNY17,975/ton(yue.li@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0156 GMT [Dow Jones] Taiwan shares are down 1.4% at 8211.78, recovering from an early low of 8150.73 due to bargain hunting and strength in chemicals stocks amid a sell-off in heavyweight techs, says Capital Securities assistant vice-president Diana Wu. She tips Taiex to find support at 8070 today as some investors are still willing to buy at lows; "but the Taiex will fall further to 7800 in the near term if Japan's nuclear crisis deteriorates." Electronics plays lead declines on concerns over end-demand and component supply after the Japan quake, with TSMC (2330.TW) down 3.1% at NT$66.40 and HTC (2498.TW) down 3.2% at NT$997.00. Chemicals stocks shine on strength in commodity prices, with \uc2 Asia Polymer\uc2  (1308.TW) limit-up 7% at NT$48.10 and Taita Chemicals (1309.TW) up 6.7% at NT$12.80. (lorraine.luk@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0156 GMT [Dow Jones] The HSI is down 2.1% at 22,217.28 after sliding to a fresh 2011 low of 22,123.26 earlier, tracking weakness in overseas markets on continued concerns over Japan's nuclear crisis. At this point, it's perhaps better for investors to "turn off the screen, and take a look at (their) portfolios to see if there will be a long-lasting impact on the earnings of the companies they hold," says a local fund manager. Quake impact aside, \uc2 Citigroup\uc2  advises buying into China stocks' weakness "as (the) liquidity stretch is near its end." 44 of 45 blue chips are down led by Tencent's (0700.HK) 6.8% tumble to HK$202.00 after its 4Q earnings misses view; China Mobile (0941.HK) falls 3.2% to HK$68.80 on dividend disappointment and higher capex. Market volume is strong at HK$13.23 billion. (robert.li@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0154 GMT [Dow Jones] The USD/TWD is slightly higher at 29.6040 vs 29.5560 in late Asian trade Wednesday on weak local stocks, says a trader with a foreign bank. The Taiex is off 1.5% at 8199.19. "The pair is at the top of the recent trading range but exporters are selling near NT$29.6000," he says. He adds, the pair is likely to stay in a 29.4000-29.7000 range. (paul.mozur@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0152 GMT [Dow Jones] JPY-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  may be imminent following the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's dramatic surge earlier this morning, partly because last week's devastating earthquake and tsunami, as well as the nuclear reactor problems, mean that foreign countries that under normal circumstances would oppose such a move are unlikely to do so now, says Nomura Securities in a research report. "Given the extraordinary circumstances in \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 , there should be no real opposition to \b \highlight22\uc2 FX intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  from key global partners, including the U.S, and the eurozone." The house says that the dire circumstances in \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  also mean that coordinated \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  "with potential participation from the Fed, ECB and BOE" cannot be ruled out. "Finally, an announcement of \b \highlight22\uc2 FX intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  could potentially be combined with further monetary policy easing measures," despite the fact that the BOJ announced additional easing measures on Monday, Nomura says. The USD/JPY is now at 79.57, off its all-time low of 76.25 tapped earlier in the session. (andrew.monahan@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0151 GMT [Dow Jones] China's March inflation will likely breach 5.0% on-year if food prices do not fall over the next two weeks, Daiwa's Mingchun Sun and Kevin Lai say. Daiwa's weekly inflation tracker shows food prices rose 0.33% on-week last week after rising 0.27% the previous week, which is quite exceptional, they say, adding food prices increased only once on an on-month basis in March between 1995 and 2010. "This suggests that food-price inflation is stickier this time than we had forecast originally." The house still expects food prices to decline as weather warms up and China's administrative pricing measures take effect. China's February CPI rose 4.9% on-year. (jean.yung@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0150 GMT [Dow Jones] Indonesian shares may fall, tracking weakness across global markets amid growing concerns over the nuclear crisis in Japan, traders say. They expect the main index to find support at 3480 after it ended up 0.2% Wednesday at 3531.477. "The weaker rupiah should also inspire selling at a time when the market is running out of fresh leads," says a trader with Lautandhana Securities. \uc2 Bank Mandiri\uc2  (BMRI.JK), and car maker Astra (ASII.JK) may lead declines on profit-taking. Meanwhile Bank Negara (BBNI.JK) may rise after reporting its 2010 net profit rose 65% on-year due to higher net interest income. (edhi.pranasidhi@dowjones.com) [ 03-16-11 2218ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par