ng earthquake and tsunami, as well as the nuclear reactor problems, mean that foreign countries that under normal circumstances would oppose such a move are unlikely to do so now, says Nomura Securities in a research report. "Given the extraordinary circumstances in \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 , there should be no real opposition to \b \highlight22\uc2 FX intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  from key global partners, including the U.S, and the eurozone." The house says that the dire circumstances in \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  also mean that coordinated \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  "with potential participation from the Fed, ECB and BOE" cannot be ruled out. "Finally, an announcement of \b \highlight22\uc2 FX intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  could potentially be combined with further monetary policy easing measures," despite the fact that the BOJ announced additional easing measures on Monday, Nomura says. The USD/JPY is now at 79.57, off its all-time low of 76.25 tapped earlier in the session. (andrew.monahan@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0151 GMT [Dow Jones] China's March inflation will likely breach 5.0% on-year if food prices do not fall over the next two weeks, Daiwa's Mingchun Sun and Kevin Lai say. Daiwa's weekly inflation tracker shows food prices rose 0.33% on-week last week after rising 0.27% the previous week, which is quite exceptional, they say, adding food prices increased only once on an on-month basis in March between 1995 and 2010. "This suggests that food-price inflation is stickier this time than we had forecast originally." The house still expects food prices to decline as weather warms up and China's administrative pricing measures take effect. China's February CPI rose 4.9% on-year. (jean.yung@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0150 GMT [Dow Jones] Indonesian shares may fall, tracking weakness across global markets amid growing concerns over the nuclear crisis in Japan, traders say. They expect the main index to find support at 3480 after it ended up 0.2% Wednesday at 3531.477. "The weaker rupiah should also inspire selling at a time when the market is running out of fresh leads," says a trader with Lautandhana Securities. \uc2 Bank Mandiri\uc2  (BMRI.JK), and car maker Astra (ASII.JK) may lead declines on profit-taking. Meanwhile Bank Negara (BBNI.JK) may rise after reporting its 2010 net profit rose 65% on-year due to higher net interest income. (edhi.pranasidhi@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0150 GMT [Dow Jones] The Nikkei is off 2.3% at 8883.82, well off its intraday low of 8639.56, after Japan's Self Defense Forces started dropping water on overheated reactor units at the troubled Fukushima Daiichi nuclear power plant, and authorities said that power cables from an outside source would be available by early this afternoon. "There will be further relief for the markets if we can confirm that this measure will lead to an actual drop in temperatures (at the reactor)," says Cosmo Securities equity strategist Toshikazu Horiuchi, pegging today's Nikkei support at 8500. He adds that market sentiment could be further boosted if authorities \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to boost USD/JPY (now 79.55), which fell to a record low at 76.25 overnight, breaching its previous record low of 79.75 in 1995. (kana.inagaki@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0148 GMT [Dow Jones] Lead June JGB futures briefly dip into negative territory, hitting a low of 139.67, as the Nikkei recovers some of its early losses and the dollar/yen bounces off its lows; the contract is currently up 0.10 at 139.82 after touching an intraday high of 140.39 earlier. "As players are not sure which directions they should move, amid the high uncertainty, moves tend to be one-sided" when there's large selling or buying, says RBS Securities Japan's chief rates strategist Akito Fukunaga. But Fukunaga expects the JGB market to stabilize next week, with a solid bias. "If you think about fiscal and economic downside risks (from the earthquake), deterioration in the domestic economy is likely to be a more immediate concern," he adds.(megumi.fujikawa@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0146 GMT [Dow Jones] China shares are lower, taking their cues from weakness in the Nikkei this morning and declines on Wall Street Wednesday. The Shanghai Composite Index is down 1.3% at 2892.93, with analysts tipping immediate support at 2850. "The A-share market is pressured by the weakness in overseas markets and investors are watching the latest developments of Japan's nuclear crisis," says Jacky Zhang, an analyst from Capital Securities. Among losers gold miners fall following the retreat in gold prices; Zijin Mining (601899.SH) sheds 3.3% to CNY7.61, while Shandong Gold-Mining (600547.SH) eases 1.7% to CNY46.80. Steel makers drop on profit-taking after recent gains; \uc2 Wuhan Iron & Steel\uc2  (600005.SH) slides 2.8% to CNY4.91, and Baoshan Iron & Steel (600019.SH) is down 2.2% at CNY7.29. The Shenzhen Composite Index is 1.2% lower at 1291.71. (amy.li@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0146 GMT [Dow Jones] The USD/SGD looks to trade sideways after testing a six-week high of 1.2874 in early Asia trade. Trade has been choppy in the morning as Japan's nuclear plant worries continue to weigh on the SGD. But a pullback from USD highs may be driven by uncertainty about whether \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  will \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, as the yen soared against the dollar overnight, says a local bank trader. The USD/SGD is last at 1.2825 and the trader tips a 1.2785-1.2850 band for the day. (martin.vaughan@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0142 GMT [Dow Jones] The STI is down 1.0% at 2940.40, amid falls across the region as worries over Japan's stricken nuclear units continue to weigh on investor sentiment. "The risk off situation continues," says Phillip Securities, noting bonds, the CHF and USD all saw gains overnight, while commodities continued to decline and the JPY soared to a record high against the greenback. It notes, the STI's gains Wednesday came from the opening gap after the "exceptionally sharp" selldown Tuesday, and the intermediate term trend remains downward biased while visibility is not that clear in the short term. It tips support at 2932 (Tuesday's low) with key support at 2915; resistance is at 2988 and 3000. There are 9 fallers per gainer across the market with 221 million shares traded. 27 of the STI names are lower, led by SIA Engineering (S59.SG), down 3.4% at S$3.99. \uc2 Golden Agri-Resources\uc2  (E5H.SG) is the most active stock so far, off 1.5% at S$0.640. (matthew.allen@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0140 GMT [Dow Jones] Korea treasury bonds, bond futures erase their sharp opening gains as some take profits following the run-up in prices of late, say market participants. The 3-year bonds are quoted at 3.56% from 3.64% yesterday, the 5-year is at 3.96% from 4.00% yesterday, while the June futures are up 15 ticks at 103.35 after rising to as high as 103.51 earlier. "The Japan (nuke plant problem) news is still an issue, but we seem to have reached levels which make us hesitate before buying more" due to the expensive prices, says Taurus Investment & Securities analyst Kong Dong-rak; he tips 3-year note to trade in a 3.50%-3.65% band for the rest of the session. (kanga.kong@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0139 GMT [Dow Jones] The KLCI is down 0.5% at 1484.49 in early trade as worries mount over the possibility of radiation leaks from the quake-damaged nuclear power plants in Japan, a local dealer says. Selling is broad and is in line with regional markets, after the Dow Jones Industrial Average fell 2.0% overnight. "It remain unclear as to what will happen next. We think it will be best to stay away from the markets for now," the dealer adds. He tips immediate key support at 1474. Market breadth is negative with 317 decliners compared with 51 gainers. Malaysian Airline (3786.KU) is down 2.2% at MYR1.78, Gamuda (5398.KU) is down 1.9% at MYR3.62, \uc2 AMMB Holdings\uc2  (1015.KU) is down 1.6% at MYR6.19 and CIMB Group (1023.KU) is down 1.5% at MYR7.88. (ankur.relia@dowjones.com) [ 03-16-11 2156ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020110317e73h0001h\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
