 hesitate before buying more" due to the expensive prices, says Taurus Investment & Securities analyst Kong Dong-rak; he tips 3-year note to trade in a 3.50%-3.65% band for the rest of the session. (kanga.kong@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0139 GMT [Dow Jones] The KLCI is down 0.5% at 1484.49 in early trade as worries mount over the possibility of radiation leaks from the quake-damaged nuclear power plants in Japan, a local dealer says. Selling is broad and is in line with regional markets, after the Dow Jones Industrial Average fell 2.0% overnight. "It remain unclear as to what will happen next. We think it will be best to stay away from the markets for now," the dealer adds. He tips immediate key support at 1474. Market breadth is negative with 317 decliners compared with 51 gainers. Malaysian Airline (3786.KU) is down 2.2% at MYR1.78, Gamuda (5398.KU) is down 1.9% at MYR3.62, \uc2 AMMB Holdings\uc2  (1015.KU) is down 1.6% at MYR6.19 and CIMB Group (1023.KU) is down 1.5% at MYR7.88. (ankur.relia@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0133 GMT [Dow Jones] The USD recovers about Y3.5 from its early record low of Y76.25, and was last at Y79.75, helped by investors' "somewhat eased" concerns about Japan's nuclear plant crisis after Japan defense force helicopters dropped water on the troubled reactors, says \uc2 Daiwa SB Investments\uc2  senior fund manager Kenichiro Ikezawa. The nuclear agency also said that it was close to completing the connection of power cables from outside sources to restore power to at least two of the units, which would enable their vital cooling pumps to begin operating again. On the subject of market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by Japanese authorities, Ikezawa said: "I thought \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  would have done it by now, and I think the market sentiment got a bit difficult for Japan to go ahead, from an hour ago." Another dealer in Japan says market players will be watching if USD/JPY can rise above tough resistance at 80.00, where many exporters are interested in selling the pair.(takashi.mochizuki@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0132 GMT [Dow Jones] Philippine shares are expected to fall amid ongoing concerns about the Japanese nuclear reactor damaged by the recent earthquake. The bellwether PSEi may slip to 3850 after it ended 0.4% lower at 3878.44 Wednesday; traders peg support at 3800 and resistance at 3900. "The ghost that is Japan and the Middle East will continue to haunt us. The nuclear problem in Japan is a major concern," says Astro del Castillo, managing director at First Grade Holdings. Major blue chips PLDT (TEL.PH) and \uc2 Aboitiz Equity Ventures\uc2  (AEV.PH) are expected to lead today's decliners. (cris.larano@dowjones.com) [ 03-16-11 2152ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020110317e73h0001f\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
