\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: Thai Shares Likely Lower; Support Tipped At 995\b0\par\par\uc2 1,456 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2011\par 02:29\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0129 GMT [Dow Jones] Thai shares are expected to fall, tracking Wall Street's decline overnight, while losses in regional markets hurt sentiment. Investors continue to fret about the nuclear situation in Japan but the benchmark index is unlikely to fall sharply as Thai energy plays are expected to benefit from growing energy demand, says Far East Securities' analyst Parin Kitchatornpitak. However, he reckons if the benchmark index fails to close above 1000 today, investors may start to fear the market will enter a corrective phase. He tips support at 995 and resistance at 1010. The SET Index ended +0.5% at 1008.13 Wednesday. (piyarat.setthasiriphaiboon@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0123 GMT [Dow Jones] The USD/JPY rises to 79.54 as Japanese importers buy to address their daily settlement needs, says a senior dealer at a major bank in Tokyo. Many investors are staying on the sidelines, keeping a close eye on \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's MOF, as they think the authorities could \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to curb' the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's recent strength. Still, he says exporters are expected to sell later in the day. He adds, the pair is largely unmoved by news Japan's Self Defense Force started dropping ocean water on nuclear plants. (takashi.mochizuki@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0123 GMT [Dow Jones] Lead June JGB futures are trimming some of their earlier gains, briefly easing back to 139.78 from 140.39 marked right after the market opens; while short- to medium-term JGBs are likely to be supported by expectations for additional easing by the BOJ, players may want to decrease exposure in their long-term JGB holdings because normal theory--where economic downside risks and yen's rises are regarded as buying cues for government bonds--may not work amid high uncertainty over the crisis situation in Japan, says Mizuho Securities chief market analyst Tetsuya Miura. The 10-year cash JGB yield is last down 3 bps at 1.190%. (megumi.fujikawa@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0117 GMT [Dow Jones] The USD/THB is quoted lower at 30.36-30.39 from 30.41-30.42 late Wednesday in Asia in thin trade. But the pair is biased upward as Thai shares are expected to fall in line with Nikkei weakness as a result of mounting concerns over Japan's nuclear plant woes, says local dealer. He adds the decline in gold prices may prompt some gold traders to buy the dollar. Meanwhile, the ongoing Thai parliament censure debate is unlikely to have an impact on sentiment as most investors look ahead to a new election; PM Abhisit said last week he plans to dissolve parliament by the first week of May. The dealer tips a 30.30-30.50 band for the day. (piyarat.setthasiriphaiboon@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0116 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/IDR is higher at 8,795 vs 8,780 late yesterday as plummeting Japanese stocks trigger selloffs in other regional bourses including Indonesia, dealers say. Two dealers say Bank Indonesia was heard checking rate, but is unlikely to have \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  as the market remains calm and the pair was off the 8,800 intraday high. "Bank Indonesia could sell the dollar if it creeps higher to above IDR8,800," a dealer says; he tips a 8,775-8,805 range. (i-made.sentana@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0113 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/KRW gaining ground as expected, but off its high with some traders suspecting the BOK has sold small amounts of USD to slow KRW's slide above the 1,140 level early today following finance ministry's warning against volatility, say market participants. The pair is last at 1,138.4, off the 1,144.0 high. "Exporters are selling above KRW1,140 and the Kospi is doing better than expected today. There are also expectations that the government wouldn't be happy to see more gains in the pair due to inflation, which will probably be the reason for their (\b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ) earlier," says a local bank trader; he tips the pair to be in a 1,135-1,145 band for the rest of day. A foreign bank trader says external uneasiness linked to Japan's nuclear plant problems have escalated and traders are betting on more gains in pair, but such attempts are offset partly by export settlement. (kanga.kong@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0109 GMT [Dow Jones] Malaysia government bonds are likely to rise a tad as worries over the global economy in the aftermath of Japan's earthquake may deter any interest rate hikes in the near future, a local trader says. He tips the yields to move 1 to 2 bps lower during the day. On Wednesday, the yield on the August 2014 MGS ends unchanged at 3.38%, the September 2016 MGS yield also closes flat at 3.55%, the September 2017 MGS yield ends down 2 bps at 3.75%, and the July 2021 MGS yield ends 4 bps down at 4.01%. (ankur.relia@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0108 GMT [Dow Jones] The USD/MYR is higher at 3.0610 vs late Wednesday's 3.0490, tracking other Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  after U.S. stocks tumbled overnight as worries mounted that Japan's nuclear crisis would end in reactor meltdowns and a radiation catastrophe, says a local trader. "We are back to risk aversion again today," he adds. He tips the pair in a 3.0500-3.0700 range. (ankur.relia@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0106 GMT [Dow Jones] The USD/PHP may rise as local stocks are expected to fall after Wall Street's decline overnight, while investors continue to fret about the nuclear situation in Japan. The pair may rise to 43.80 after settling at 43.755 in late Asian trade Wednesday; traders peg resistance at 44.00 and support at 43.50. "It's still a 'buy-dollar and sell-Asia' story because of Japan. However, this is mostly sentiment-driven so the trend could quickly reverse. We're also watching out for BSP (\b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 ) because it could \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  if the dollar moves towards PHP44.00," says a Manila-based \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealer.(cris.larano@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0105 GMT [Dow Jones] The HSI is likely to fall taking a cue from Wall Street, but is not expected to test Monday's low of 22,253 which is also 2011's low, says a futures trader, because "the panic sentiment is unlikely to match that of Monday morning's." Tencent (0700.HK) may fall after below-view FY10 results, while China Mobile (0941.HK) is likely to underperform more in the short run on dividend disappointment and higher capex ahead. More blue-chip stocks such as Henderson Land (0012.HK), China Overseas Land (0688.HK), \uc2 PetroChina\uc2  (0857.HK) and \uc2 China Resources Power\uc2  (0836.HK) are expected to report results later Thursday. The HSI ended +0.1% at 22,700.88. (robert.li@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0105 GMT [Dow Jones] While bargain hunting has buoyed the USD/JPY up from the post World War II low it hit earlier this morning at 76.25, selling by Japan exporters could weigh the pair down to around 75.00 in the near term, says Osamu Takashima, the chief \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Citibank Japan. "Buying back the dollar may temporarily take precedence" after the sharp fall earlier, Takashima adds. "But given the morning's drop, there are many exporter options that were knocked out, so the exporters' dollar-selling, yen-buying pressure will likely increase further from here." Takashima says for the rest of the global day, the pair's topside will be heavy around its previous record low of 79.75, while it could fall back as low as 78.00, compared with last 79.54. (andrew.monahan@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0100 GMT [Dow Jones] China shares may extend gains, but any upside will likely be capped on concerns over further interest rate hikes in the short term, analysts say. They expect the Shanghai Composite Index to test 2950 resistance after it ends up 1.2% at 2930.80 yesterday. "With fears about Japan's radiation leak fading, Chinese investors will shift focus back to the domestic economic situation," Shanxi Securities says in a note. Analysts tip negative sentiment towards companies that are involved in the nuclear power industry, such as Dongfang Electric (600875.SH) and Sufa Technology Industry (000777.SZ), after China says late yesterday that it halted approvals for new nuclear power plants pending changes to safety standards. The Shenzhen Composite Index ends up 1.1% at 1307.96. (rose.yu@dowjones.com) [ 03-16-11 2129ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020110317e73h00012\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
