0941.HK) is likely to underperform more in the short run on dividend disappointment and higher capex ahead. More blue-chip stocks such as Henderson Land (0012.HK), China Overseas Land (0688.HK), \uc2 PetroChina\uc2  (0857.HK) and \uc2 China Resources Power\uc2  (0836.HK) are expected to report results later Thursday. The HSI ended +0.1% at 22,700.88. (robert.li@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0105 GMT [Dow Jones] While bargain hunting has buoyed the USD/JPY up from the post World War II low it hit earlier this morning at 76.25, selling by Japan exporters could weigh the pair down to around 75.00 in the near term, says Osamu Takashima, the chief \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Citibank Japan. "Buying back the dollar may temporarily take precedence" after the sharp fall earlier, Takashima adds. "But given the morning's drop, there are many exporter options that were knocked out, so the exporters' dollar-selling, yen-buying pressure will likely increase further from here." Takashima says for the rest of the global day, the pair's topside will be heavy around its previous record low of 79.75, while it could fall back as low as 78.00, compared with last 79.54. (andrew.monahan@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0100 GMT [Dow Jones] China shares may extend gains, but any upside will likely be capped on concerns over further interest rate hikes in the short term, analysts say. They expect the Shanghai Composite Index to test 2950 resistance after it ends up 1.2% at 2930.80 yesterday. "With fears about Japan's radiation leak fading, Chinese investors will shift focus back to the domestic economic situation," Shanxi Securities says in a note. Analysts tip negative sentiment towards companies that are involved in the nuclear power industry, such as Dongfang Electric (600875.SH) and Sufa Technology Industry (000777.SZ), after China says late yesterday that it halted approvals for new nuclear power plants pending changes to safety standards. The Shenzhen Composite Index ends up 1.1% at 1307.96. (rose.yu@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0100 GMT [Dow Jones] PREVIEW: India's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may continue its monetary tightening as inflation continues to defy attempts to tame it, economists say. The RBI may raise the key lending and borrowing rates by 25 bps each to 6.75% and 5.75% respectively, according to 14 of the 15 economists polled by Dow Jones. "Since crippling growth is not on the agenda, the RBI will likely adopt a 'go-slow' approach from here on"--raising rates only gradually--"unless sharply higher global commodity prices mess up everything," a CLSA research note says. Only one expects a sharper 50bp increase in both rates as inflation increasingly becomes generalized and the RBI "simply cannot afford to remain behind the curve any longer," Religare Capital Markets says. RBI's rate decision is due 0630 GMT Thursday. (khushita.vasant@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0059 GMT [Dow Jones] The KLCI may open lower tracking weak U.S. stocks overnight as worries mounted about Japan's nuclear crisis, a local dealer says. "Yesterday's rebound was in really low volumes, and we are most likely to see another day of broad selling today," the dealer adds. He tips immediate support at 1485, then the Nov. 2010 low of 1474. Wednesday KLCI ended 0.6% higher at 1492.44. \uc2 Malaysia Airports\uc2  (5014.KU), or MAHB, may rise strong traffic growth. (ankur.relia@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0056 GMT [Dow Jones] The Kospi is down 1.1% at 1937.20, tracking the overnight declines in U.S. stocks. Though the market is fearing over the nuclear crisis in neighboring Japan, the recent corrections also provide good buying opportunities considering the fundamentals of local stocks, says \uc2 Samsung Securities\uc2  analyst You Seung-min, who tips 1900 support. Most heavyweight stocks are down, with \uc2 Samsung Electronics\uc2  (005930.SE) down 1.5% at KRW874,000, \uc2 Hyundai Motor\uc2  (005380.SE) off 1.1% at KRW180,000 and \uc2 LG Chem\uc2  (051910.SE) down 1.3% at KRW407,000. But shipbuilders ourperform on new order hopes after \uc2 Samsung Heavy Industries\uc2  (010140.SE) says late Wednesday it received a KRW1.25 trillion order to build two drilling ships for an unidentified U.S. firm; Samsung Heavy is up 0.3% at KRW36,900, \uc2 Daewoo Shipbuilding & Marine Engineering\uc2  (042660.SE) is up 2.2% at KRW32,400 and \uc2 Hyundai Heavy Industries\uc2  (009540.SE) rises 1.6% at KRW460,000. (jieun.shin@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0053 GMT [Dow Jones] Longer-dated Singapore government bonds may rise, tracking gains in U.S. Treasurys overnight, flattening the yield curve, as concerns over Japan's nuclear crisis and unrest in the Middle East continue driving investors toward safer assets, a trader with a foreign bank says. "Bonds will likely rise with a flatter curve, given the current global situation. (But) I don't think there will be much movement on the short-end, maybe 2 basis points at most." She tips the 10-year bond yield to slide 5-7 bps. On Wednesday, the two-year bond yield fell 1 bp at 0.37%, while the 10-year yield slid 1 bp at 2.30%. (chunhan.wong@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0050 GMT [Dow Jones] The USD/TWD is biased up after U.S. stocks tumble on worries that Japan's nuclear crisis would end in reactor meltdowns and a radiation catastrophe, says a foreign bank trader. "Any panic selling in stock markets will push the greenback higher today. Many investors want to cut positions and park funds in USD." He tips that the pair may breach 29.650 resistance as the Taiex is expected to drop sharply. The USD/TWD was steady at 29.556 late Wednesday. (lorraine.luk@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0047 GMT [Dow Jones] The USD/CNY is likely to continue to consolidate around 6.5700 from 6.5713 late Wednesday in light of the intensifying nuclear crisis in Japan. Thursday's central parity will likely be set around 6.5700, compared to Wednesday's 6.5718, says a Shanghai-based Asian bank trader