\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: USD/JPY May Fall To Around 75.00 In N/T - Citi\b0\par\par\uc2 1,681 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2011\par 02:05\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0105 GMT [Dow Jones] While bargain hunting has buoyed the USD/JPY up from the post World War II low it hit earlier this morning at 76.25, selling by Japan exporters could weigh the pair down to around 75.00 in the near term, says Osamu Takashima, the chief \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategist at Citibank Japan. "Buying back the dollar may temporarily take precedence" after the sharp fall earlier, Takashima adds. "But given the morning's drop, there are many exporter options that were knocked out, so the exporters' dollar-selling, yen-buying pressure will likely increase further from here." Takashima says for the rest of the global day, the pair's topside will be heavy around its previous record low of 79.75, while it could fall back as low as 78.00, compared with last 79.54. (andrew.monahan@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0100 GMT [Dow Jones] China shares may extend gains, but any upside will likely be capped on concerns over further interest rate hikes in the short term, analysts say. They expect the Shanghai Composite Index to test 2950 resistance after it ends up 1.2% at 2930.80 yesterday. "With fears about Japan's radiation leak fading, Chinese investors will shift focus back to the domestic economic situation," Shanxi Securities says in a note. Analysts tip negative sentiment towards companies that are involved in the nuclear power industry, such as Dongfang Electric (600875.SH) and Sufa Technology Industry (000777.SZ), after China says late yesterday that it halted approvals for new nuclear power plants pending changes to safety standards. The Shenzhen Composite Index ends up 1.1% at 1307.96. (rose.yu@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0100 GMT [Dow Jones] PREVIEW: India's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may continue its monetary tightening as inflation continues to defy attempts to tame it, economists say. The RBI may raise the key lending and borrowing rates by 25 bps each to 6.75% and 5.75% respectively, according to 14 of the 15 economists polled by Dow Jones. "Since crippling growth is not on the agenda, the RBI will likely adopt a 'go-slow' approach from here on"--raising rates only gradually--"unless sharply higher global commodity prices mess up everything," a CLSA research note says. Only one expects a sharper 50bp increase in both rates as inflation increasingly becomes generalized and the RBI "simply cannot afford to remain behind the curve any longer," Religare Capital Markets says. RBI's rate decision is due 0630 GMT Thursday. (khushita.vasant@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0059 GMT [Dow Jones] The KLCI may open lower tracking weak U.S. stocks overnight as worries mounted about Japan's nuclear crisis, a local dealer says. "Yesterday's rebound was in really low volumes, and we are most likely to see another day of broad selling today," the dealer adds. He tips immediate support at 1485, then the Nov. 2010 low of 1474. Wednesday KLCI ended 0.6% higher at 1492.44. \uc2 Malaysia Airports\uc2  (5014.KU), or MAHB, may rise strong traffic growth. (ankur.relia@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0056 GMT [Dow Jones] The Kospi is down 1.1% at 1937.20, tracking the overnight declines in U.S. stocks. Though the market is fearing over the nuclear crisis in neighboring Japan, the recent corrections also provide good buying opportunities considering the fundamentals of local stocks, says \uc2 Samsung Securities\uc2  analyst You Seung-min, who tips 1900 support. Most heavyweight stocks are down, with \uc2 Samsung Electronics\uc2  (005930.SE) down 1.5% at KRW874,000, \uc2 Hyundai Motor\uc2  (005380.SE) off 1.1% at KRW180,000 and \uc2 LG Chem\uc2  (051910.SE) down 1.3% at KRW407,000. But shipbuilders ourperform on new order hopes after \uc2 Samsung Heavy Industries\uc2  (010140.SE) says late Wednesday it received a KRW1.25 trillion order to build two drilling ships for an unidentified U.S. firm; Samsung Heavy is up 0.3% at KRW36,900, \uc2 Daewoo Shipbuilding & Marine Engineering\uc2  (042660.SE) is up 2.2% at KRW32,400 and \uc2 Hyundai Heavy Industries\uc2  (009540.SE) rises 1.6% at KRW460,000. (jieun.shin@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0053 GMT [Dow Jones] Longer-dated Singapore government bonds may rise, tracking gains in U.S. Treasurys overnight, flattening the yield curve, as concerns over Japan's nuclear crisis and unrest in the Middle East continue driving investors toward safer assets, a trader with a foreign bank says. "Bonds will likely rise with a flatter curve, given the current global situation. (But) I don't think there will be much movement on the short-end, maybe 2 basis points at most." She tips the 10-year bond yield to slide 5-7 bps. On Wednesday, the two-year bond yield fell 1 bp at 0.37%, while the 10-year yield slid 1 bp at 2.30%. (chunhan.wong@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0050 GMT [Dow Jones] The USD/TWD is biased up after U.S. stocks tumble on worries that Japan's nuclear crisis would end in reactor meltdowns and a radiation catastrophe, says a foreign bank trader. "Any panic selling in stock markets will push the greenback higher today. Many investors want to cut positions and park funds in USD." He tips that the pair may breach 29.650 resistance as the Taiex is expected to drop sharply. The USD/TWD was steady at 29.556 late Wednesday. (lorraine.luk@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0047 GMT [Dow Jones] The USD/CNY is likely to continue to consolidate around 6.5700 from 6.5713 late Wednesday in light of the intensifying nuclear crisis in Japan. Thursday's central parity will likely be set around 6.5700, compared to Wednesday's 6.5718, says a Shanghai-based Asian bank trader. "We expect Beijing to keep the yuan largely insulated from the bumpy markets overseas. The central parity may be frozen around 6.5700 for the foreseeable future," he says. (jean.yung@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0046 GMT [Dow Jones] Short- and medium-term JGB yields are likely to fall, "perhaps due in part to expectations for additional easing from the BOJ," says \uc2 Barclays Capital\uc2  chief strategist Chotaro Morita. The cash JGB market is still relatively calm, with the 5-year yield trading down 2 bps at 0.490%. 2-year JGBs are untraded and the yield closed at 0.225% Wednesday. He adds, "the prospect of (\b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  market) \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  seems likely and G-7 leaders are planning to issue a statement on aid for \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  tonight, which could contain remarks in support of such \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ." The USD/JPY briefly fell overnight as far as 76.25, breaching its previous post-war record low of 79.75. (megumi.fujikawa@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0040 GMT [Dow Jones] Singapore shares are likely to fall after steep falls on Wall Street, with ongoing worries over a potential nuclear disaster in Japan, escalating Middle East violence, and some weak U.S. data combining to ensure markets are firmly risk-off. On Wednesday, the STI ends up 0.8% at 2971.00, reversing five straight days of falls. AmFraser technical analyst Najeeb Jarhom says the STI's weak rebound, and its close proximity to the February 24 low of 2965 "imply the STI has yet to mark its first major bottom." He notes major double bottoms, such as at the end of late-2007 to the early 2009 crash from 3831 to 1455, as well as in 1H10, are usually spaced out some three months apart, "thus the February 24 intraday low of 2965 which is barely two weeks ahead of yesterday's 2932 low cannot be regarded as a major double bottom formation which ushered in the great recovery from 1455 in March 2009 to 2950/3038 in January-April last year." He tips resistance at 2998-3007, then in the 3040-3065 zone. (matthew.allen@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0040 GMT [Dow Jones] The Nikkei is off 3.6% at 8767.61 after USD/JPY fell to a record low of 76.25 overnight, breaching its previous record low of 79.75 in 1995. "The stronger yen concerns are clouding the outlook for Japanese corporate earnings," says Fumiyuki Takahashi, equity strategist at \uc2 Barclays Capital\uc2  Japan, pegging today's index support at 8300. "If the nuclear power problems continue to drag on, foreign investors will begin to withhold their investment in Japanese shares," he adds. All 33 Topix subindexes are lower, with bellwether exporters off sharply; Canon (7751.TO) is down 6.3% at Y3,365, \uc2 Toyota Motor\uc2  (7203.TO) is down 4.9% at Y3,180, and \uc2 Sony\uc2  (6758.TO) is down 4.9% at Y2,404. \uc2 Tokyo Electric Power\uc2  (9501.TO), or "Tepco", operator of the troubled nuclear power facility, is ask/only at Y861. Nikkei 225 June futures are off 3.7% at 8680,(kana.inagaki@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0038 GMT [Dow Jones] The USD/JPY is rebounding off its lows of the day as a few long-term players look at 'cheap levels,' while more short-term players expect the BOJ/G-7 support in the next few hours to help create a base, says \uc2 Deutsche Bank\uc2 's global head of \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategy Alan Ruskin. "With the G-7 scheduled to hold a conference call on Japan late Thursday, it seems inevitable that the yen will be part of a broader discussion on stabilizing global markets. It would be nothing short of amazing if they fail to at least make some veiled threat of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," he says. The most important point is to watch for any clear signal that if the BOJ \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  the action will be coordinated with other major central banks, notably the Fed and the ECB, Ruskin says. The pair is now at 79.27, off its all-time low of 76.25 hit early in the Asian day, which Ruskin attributes to complete absence of bids 'matched' against an avalanche of barrier related stop-loss selling, and retail margin calls. (shri.navaratnam@dowjones.com) [ 03-16-11 2105ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020110317e73h0000m\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
