 He notes major double bottoms, such as at the end of late-2007 to the early 2009 crash from 3831 to 1455, as well as in 1H10, are usually spaced out some three months apart, "thus the February 24 intraday low of 2965 which is barely two weeks ahead of yesterday's 2932 low cannot be regarded as a major double bottom formation which ushered in the great recovery from 1455 in March 2009 to 2950/3038 in January-April last year." He tips resistance at 2998-3007, then in the 3040-3065 zone. (matthew.allen@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0040 GMT [Dow Jones] The Nikkei is off 3.6% at 8767.61 after USD/JPY fell to a record low of 76.25 overnight, breaching its previous record low of 79.75 in 1995. "The stronger yen concerns are clouding the outlook for Japanese corporate earnings," says Fumiyuki Takahashi, equity strategist at \uc2 Barclays Capital\uc2  Japan, pegging today's index support at 8300. "If the nuclear power problems continue to drag on, foreign investors will begin to withhold their investment in Japanese shares," he adds. All 33 Topix subindexes are lower, with bellwether exporters off sharply; Canon (7751.TO) is down 6.3% at Y3,365, \uc2 Toyota Motor\uc2  (7203.TO) is down 4.9% at Y3,180, and \uc2 Sony\uc2  (6758.TO) is down 4.9% at Y2,404. \uc2 Tokyo Electric Power\uc2  (9501.TO), or "Tepco", operator of the troubled nuclear power facility, is ask/only at Y861. Nikkei 225 June futures are off 3.7% at 8680,(kana.inagaki@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0038 GMT [Dow Jones] The USD/JPY is rebounding off its lows of the day as a few long-term players look at 'cheap levels,' while more short-term players expect the BOJ/G-7 support in the next few hours to help create a base, says \uc2 Deutsche Bank\uc2 's global head of \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategy Alan Ruskin. "With the G-7 scheduled to hold a conference call on Japan late Thursday, it seems inevitable that the yen will be part of a broader discussion on stabilizing global markets. It would be nothing short of amazing if they fail to at least make some veiled threat of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," he says. The most important point is to watch for any clear signal that if the BOJ \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  the action will be coordinated with other major central banks, notably the Fed and the ECB, Ruskin says. The pair is now at 79.27, off its all-time low of 76.25 hit early in the Asian day, which Ruskin attributes to complete absence of bids 'matched' against an avalanche of barrier related stop-loss selling, and retail margin calls. (shri.navaratnam@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0026 GMT [Dow Jones] Japan MOF Tamaki's comment that "it's wrong to think that the yen's sharp rise has been driven by Japanese institutional investors repatriating funds to Japan", sounds a bit odd because there's repatriation by Japanese institutions that want cash in hand, says \uc2 Okasan Securities\uc2  senior dealer Tsutomu Soma. For today, investors will stand on the sidelines for a while to see how and when Japan will take action; if Japan fails to do it, the USD/JPY may start falling to the record-low of 76.25 on investors' disappointment, he adds. "\b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  has no choice. \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  should conduct \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the BOJ must leave it unsterilized. Otherwise, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's recent efforts of pumping liquidity will become meaningless." The pair is last at 79.50. (takashi.mochizuki@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0019 GMT [Dow Jones] Lead June JGB futures open higher on the yen's steep rise overnight and the Nikkei's fall (last down 3.6%); the contract is now up 0.50 at 140.22. The JGB market is expected to "remain directionless, moving in a volatile range during the day, due to increasing uncertainties over the investment environment and confusion in the market," says Mitsubishi UFJ Morgan Stanley Securities chief strategist Jun Ishii. 10-year cash bonds are yet to change hands; a 1.170%-1.230% range is tipped for the day vs yesterday's 1.220%. (megumi.fujikawa@dowjones.com) [ 03-16-11 2100ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020110317e73h0000k\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
