\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: Kospi Down 1.1%; 1900 Support Tipped\b0\par\par\uc2 1,745 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2011\par 01:56\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 0056 GMT [Dow Jones] The Kospi is down 1.1% at 1937.20, tracking the overnight declines in U.S. stocks. Though the market is fearing over the nuclear crisis in neighboring Japan, the recent corrections also provide good buying opportunities considering the fundamentals of local stocks, says \uc2 Samsung Securities\uc2  analyst You Seung-min, who tips 1900 support. Most heavyweight stocks are down, with \uc2 Samsung Electronics\uc2  (005930.SE) down 1.5% at KRW874,000, \uc2 Hyundai Motor\uc2  (005380.SE) off 1.1% at KRW180,000 and \uc2 LG Chem\uc2  (051910.SE) down 1.3% at KRW407,000. But shipbuilders ourperform on new order hopes after \uc2 Samsung Heavy Industries\uc2  (010140.SE) says late Wednesday it received a KRW1.25 trillion order to build two drilling ships for an unidentified U.S. firm; Samsung Heavy is up 0.3% at KRW36,900, \uc2 Daewoo Shipbuilding & Marine Engineering\uc2  (042660.SE) is up 2.2% at KRW32,400 and \uc2 Hyundai Heavy Industries\uc2  (009540.SE) rises 1.6% at KRW460,000. (jieun.shin@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0053 GMT [Dow Jones] Longer-dated Singapore government bonds may rise, tracking gains in U.S. Treasurys overnight, flattening the yield curve, as concerns over Japan's nuclear crisis and unrest in the Middle East continue driving investors toward safer assets, a trader with a foreign bank says. "Bonds will likely rise with a flatter curve, given the current global situation. (But) I don't think there will be much movement on the short-end, maybe 2 basis points at most." She tips the 10-year bond yield to slide 5-7 bps. On Wednesday, the two-year bond yield fell 1 bp at 0.37%, while the 10-year yield slid 1 bp at 2.30%. (chunhan.wong@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0050 GMT [Dow Jones] The USD/TWD is biased up after U.S. stocks tumble on worries that Japan's nuclear crisis would end in reactor meltdowns and a radiation catastrophe, says a foreign bank trader. "Any panic selling in stock markets will push the greenback higher today. Many investors want to cut positions and park funds in USD." He tips that the pair may breach 29.650 resistance as the Taiex is expected to drop sharply. The USD/TWD was steady at 29.556 late Wednesday. (lorraine.luk@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0047 GMT [Dow Jones] The USD/CNY is likely to continue to consolidate around 6.5700 from 6.5713 late Wednesday in light of the intensifying nuclear crisis in Japan. Thursday's central parity will likely be set around 6.5700, compared to Wednesday's 6.5718, says a Shanghai-based Asian bank trader. "We expect Beijing to keep the yuan largely insulated from the bumpy markets overseas. The central parity may be frozen around 6.5700 for the foreseeable future," he says. (jean.yung@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0046 GMT [Dow Jones] Short- and medium-term JGB yields are likely to fall, "perhaps due in part to expectations for additional easing from the BOJ," says \uc2 Barclays Capital\uc2  chief strategist Chotaro Morita. The cash JGB market is still relatively calm, with the 5-year yield trading down 2 bps at 0.490%. 2-year JGBs are untraded and the yield closed at 0.225% Wednesday. He adds, "the prospect of (\b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  market) \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  seems likely and G-7 leaders are planning to issue a statement on aid for \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  tonight, which could contain remarks in support of such \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ." The USD/JPY briefly fell overnight as far as 76.25, breaching its previous post-war record low of 79.75. (megumi.fujika