\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: S'pore Bonds May Rise; 10-Yr Yield Support At 2.23%\b0\par\par\uc2 1,719 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2011\par 01:53\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0053 GMT [Dow Jones] Longer-dated Singapore government bonds may rise, tracking gains in U.S. Treasurys overnight, flattening the yield curve, as concerns over Japan's nuclear crisis and unrest in the Middle East continue driving investors toward safer assets, a trader with a foreign bank says. "Bonds will likely rise with a flatter curve, given the current global situation. (But) I don't think there will be much movement on the short-end, maybe 2 basis points at most." She tips the 10-year bond yield to slide 5-7 bps. On Wednesday, the two-year bond yield fell 1 bp at 0.37%, while the 10-year yield slid 1 bp at 2.30%. (chunhan.wong@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0050 GMT [Dow Jones] The USD/TWD is biased up after U.S. stocks tumble on worries that Japan's nuclear crisis would end in reactor meltdowns and a radiation catastrophe, says a foreign bank trader. "Any panic selling in stock markets will push the greenback higher today. Many investors want to cut positions and park funds in USD." He tips that the pair may breach 29.650 resistance as the Taiex is expected to drop sharply. The USD/TWD was steady at 29.556 late Wednesday. (lorraine.luk@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0047 GMT [Dow Jones] The USD/CNY is likely to continue to consolidate around 6.5700 from 6.5713 late Wednesday in light of the intensifying nuclear crisis in Japan. Thursday's central parity will likely be set around 6.5700, compared to Wednesday's 6.5718, says a Shanghai-based Asian bank trader. "We expect Beijing to keep the yuan largely insulated from the bumpy markets overseas. The central parity may be frozen around 6.5700 for the foreseeable future," he says. (jean.yung@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0046 GMT [Dow Jones] Short- and medium-term JGB yields are likely to fall, "perhaps due in part to expectations for additional easing from the BOJ," says \uc2 Barclays Capital\uc2  chief strategist Chotaro Morita. The cash JGB market is still relatively calm, with the 5-year yield trading down 2 bps at 0.490%. 2-year JGBs are untraded and the yield closed at 0.225% Wednesday. He adds, "the prospect of (\b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  market) \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  seems likely and G-7 leaders are planning to issue a statement on aid for \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  tonight, which could contain remarks in support of such \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ." The USD/JPY briefly fell overnight as far as 76.25, breaching its previous post-war record low of 79.75. (megumi.fujikawa@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0040 GMT [Dow Jones] Singapore shares are likely to fall after steep falls on Wall Street, with ongoing worries over a potential nuclear disaster in Japan, escalating Middle East violence, and some weak U.S. data combining to ensure markets are firmly risk-off. On Wednesday, the STI ends up 0.8% at 2971.00, reversing five straight days of falls. AmFraser technical analyst Najeeb Jarhom says the STI's weak rebound, and its close proximity to the February 24 low of 2965 "imply the STI has yet to mark its first major bottom." He notes major double bottoms, such as at the end of late-2007 to the early 2009 crash from 3831 to 1455, as well as in 1H10, are usually spaced out some three months apart, "thus the February 24 intraday low of 2965 which is barely two weeks ahead of yesterday's 2932 low cannot be regarded as a major double bottom formation which ushered in the great recovery from 1455 in March 2009 to 2950/3038 in January-April last year." He tips resistance at 2998-3007, then in the 3040-3065 zone. (matthew.allen@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0040 GMT [Dow Jones] The Nikkei is off 3.6% at 8767.61 after USD/JPY fell to a record low of 76.25 overnight, breaching its previous record low of 79.75 in 1995. "The stronger yen concerns are clouding the outlook for Japanese corporate earnings," says Fumiyuki Takahashi, equity strategist at \uc2 Barclays Capital\uc2  Japan, pegging today's index support at 8300. "If the nuclear power problems continue to drag on, foreign investors will begin to withhold their investment in Japanese shares," he adds. All 33 Topix subindexes are lower, with bellwether exporters off sharply; Canon (7751.TO) is down 6.3% at Y3,365, \uc2 Toyota Motor\uc2  (7203.TO) is down 4.9% at Y3,180, and \uc2 Sony\uc2  (6758.TO) is down 4.9% at Y2,404. \uc2 Tokyo Electric Power\uc2  (9501.TO), or "Tepco", operator of the troubled nuclear power facility, is ask/only at Y861. Nikkei 225 June futures are off 3.7% at 8680,(kana.inagaki@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0038 GMT [Dow Jones] The USD/JPY is rebounding off its lows of the day as a few long-term players look at 'cheap levels,' while more short-term players expect the BOJ/G-7 support in the next few hours to help create a base, says \uc2 Deutsche Bank\uc2 's global head of \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategy Alan Ruskin. "With the G-7 scheduled to hold a conference call on Japan late Thursday, it seems inevitable that the yen will be part of a broader discussion on stabilizing global markets. It would be nothing short of amazing if they fail to at least make some veiled threat of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," he says. The most important point is to watch for any clear signal that if the BOJ \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  the action will be coordinated with other major central banks, notably the Fed and the ECB, Ruskin says. The pair is now at 79.27, off its all-time low of 76.25 hit early in the Asian day, which Ruskin attributes to complete absence of bids 'matched' against an avalanche of barrier related stop-loss selling, and retail margin calls. (shri.navaratnam@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0026 GMT [Dow Jones] Japan MOF Tamaki's comment that "it's wrong to think that the yen's sharp rise has been driven by Japanese institutional investors repatriating funds to Japan", sounds a bit odd because there's repatriation by Japanese institutions that want cash in hand, says \uc2 Okasan Securities\uc2  senior dealer Tsutomu Soma. For today, investors will stand on the sidelines for a while to see how and when Japan will take action; if Japan fails to do it, the USD/JPY may start falling to the record-low of 76.25 on investors' disappointment, he adds. "\b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  has no choice. \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  should conduct \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the BOJ must leave it unsterilized. Otherwise, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's recent efforts of pumping liquidity will become meaningless." The pair is last at 79.50. (takashi.mochizuki@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0019 GMT [Dow Jones] Lead June JGB futures open higher on the yen's steep rise overnight and the Nikkei's fall (last down 3.6%); the contract is now up 0.50 at 140.22. The JGB market is expected to "remain directionless, moving in a volatile range during the day, due to increasing uncertainties over the investment environment and confusion in the market," says Mitsubishi UFJ Morgan Stanley Securities chief strategist Jun Ishii. 10-year cash bonds are yet to change hands; a 1.170%-1.230% range is tipped for the day vs yesterday's 1.220%. (megumi.fujikawa@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0018 GMT [Dow Jones] Taiwan shares are biased down on steep falls in U.S. stocks overnight, says Grand Cathay Securities analyst Mars Hsu. He adds as \uc2 Apple Inc\uc2 . (AAPL) dropped sharply overnight on concerns over a shortage of component supplies from Japan, Taiwan's Apple-related stocks are likely to fall. "There is no good news for now; the benchmark index is likely to drop more than 150 points at the open, but it may also rebound later on bargain hunting." He tips the Taiex to trade in a 8200-8300 band for the session, and pegs a major support level at 8148, the 12-month moving average. Taiex ends up 1.1% at 8324.58 Wednesday, with HTC (2498.TW) limit-up 7% at NT$1,030.00 on media reports that the company's CEO said there won't be a shortage of component supplies by May, while Hon Hai(2317.TW) ends down 3.2% at NT$105.50. (fanny.liu@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0012 GMT [Dow Jones] Conditions are set for \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  to conduct \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to stop JPY's rise because the strengthening local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  would weigh on corporate sentiment and hurt share prices, says \uc2 Barclays Capital\uc2  chief \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist Masafumi Yamamoto; getting the go-ahead for \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  from global counterparts should also be easy because \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's nuclear plant issues are hurting global equity prices, he adds. The key for \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  is to pick a time to surprise the market, which he says isn't "an easy task." Japan may want to conduct massive JPY-selling that would greatly exceed September's Y2 trillion, or may want to seek joint \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  with the U.S. and Europe, or even ask G-7 or G-20 to put out an statement expressing worries. As for the timing, it's possible Japan will step into the market after Tokyo trading hours in order to surprise traders. The pair is now at 79.19. (takashi.mochizuki@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2357 GMT [Dow Jones] The USD/KRW may rise vs 1,130.8 in late Seoul trade Wednesday, tracking higher NDFs overnight in New York trade as growing worries over Japan's ability to avert nuclear meltdowns hurt risk-sensitive Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , say market participants. Amid conditions of increasing uncertainty, investors are seeking safer assets, says Samsung Futures analyst Jeon Seung-ji; "The dollar/won may rise sharply due to a possible sell-off in the local stock market by foreign investors and we may also see players selling the won against the yen." She adds, players will keep a close eye on the \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  authorities as they could step in to slow the KRW's slide. A foreign bank trader tips the pair to trade in a 1,135-1,145 band. (kanga.kong@dowjones.com) [ 03-16-11 2053ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020110317e73h0000g\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
