\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK:  \highlight22\uc2 Japan \highlight\uc2  Has No Choice But To  \highlight22\uc2 Intervene \highlight\uc2  - Dealer\b0\par\par\uc2 1,808 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2011\par 01:26\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0026 GMT [Dow Jones] Japan MOF Tamaki's comment that "it's wrong to think that the yen's sharp rise has been driven by Japanese institutional investors repatriating funds to Japan", sounds a bit odd because there's repatriation by Japanese institutions that want cash in hand, says \f28 \uc2 \f2 Okasan Securities\f28 \uc2 \f2  senior dealer Tsutomu Soma. For today, investors will stand on the sidelines for a while to see how and when Japan will take action; if Japan fails to do it, the USD/JPY may start falling to the record-low of 76.25 on investors' disappointment, he adds. "\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has no choice. \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  should conduct \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and the BOJ must leave it unsterilized. Otherwise, the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's recent efforts of pumping liquidity will become meaningless." The pair is last at 79.50. (takashi.mochizuki@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0019 GMT [Dow Jones] Lead June JGB futures open higher on the yen's steep rise overnight and the Nikkei's fall (last down 3.6%); the contract is now up 0.50 at 140.22. The JGB market is expected to "remain directionless, moving in a volatile range during the day, due to increasing uncertainties over the investment environment and confusion in the market," says Mitsubishi UFJ Morgan Stanley Securities chief strategist Jun Ishii. 10-year cash bonds are yet to change hands; a 1.170%-1.230% range is tipped for the day vs yesterday's 1.220%. (megumi.fujikawa@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0018 GMT [Dow Jones] Taiwan shares are biased down on steep falls in U.S. stocks overnight, says Grand Cathay Securities analyst Mars Hsu. He adds as \uc2 Apple Inc\uc2 . (AAPL) dropped sharply overnight on concerns over a shortage of component supplies from Japan, Taiwan's Apple-related stocks are likely to fall. "There is no good news for now; the benchmark index is likely to drop more than 150 points at the open, but it may also rebound later on bargain hunting." He tips the Taiex to trade in a 8200-8300 band for the session, and pegs a major support level at 8148, the 12-month moving average. Taiex ends up 1.1% at 8324.58 Wednesday, with HTC (2498.TW) limit-up 7% at NT$1,030.00 on media reports that the company's CEO said there won't be a shortage of component supplies by May, while Hon Hai(2317.TW) ends down 3.2% at NT$105.50. (fanny.liu@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0012 GMT [Dow Jones] Conditions are set for \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  to conduct \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to stop JPY's rise because the strengthening local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  would weigh on corporate sentiment and hurt share prices, says \uc2 Barclays Capital\uc2  chief \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist Masafumi Yamamoto; getting the go-ahead for \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  from global counterparts should also be easy because \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's nuclear plant issues are hurting global equity prices, he adds. The key for \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  is to pick a time to surprise the market, which he says isn't "an easy task." Japan may want to conduct massive JPY-selling that would greatly exceed September's Y2 trillion, or may want to seek joint \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  with the U.S. and Europe, or even ask G-7 or G-20 to put out an statement expressing worries. As for the timing, it's possible Japan will step into the market after Tokyo trading hours in order to surprise traders. The pair is now at 79.19. (takashi.mochizuki@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2357 GMT [Dow Jones] The USD/KRW may rise vs 1,130.8 in late Seoul trade Wednesday, tracking higher NDFs overnight in New York trade as growing worries over Japan's ability to avert nuclear meltdowns hurt risk-sensitive Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , say market participants. Amid conditions of increasing uncertainty, investors are seeking safer assets, says Samsung Futures analyst Jeon Seung-ji; "The dollar/won may rise sharply due to a possible sell-off in the local stock market by foreign investors and we may also see players selling the won against the yen." She adds, players will keep a close eye on the \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  authorities as they could step in to slow the KRW's slide. A foreign bank trader tips the pair to trade in a 1,135-1,145 band. (kanga.kong@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2355 GMT [Dow Jones] The NZX-50 is down 0.6% at 3307.47 as investors continue to worry about global issues, says Hamilton Hindin Greene broker Adrian Vance. "There is certainly a good round of profit-taking and there are a number of themes at play; you have the Middle East with things going on in Bahrain, you've obviously got the Japanese earthquake and nuclear issues playing on investors minds." This is weighing on big stocks, with Telecom (TEL.NZ) down 1.0% at NZ$1.97 and Fletcher Building (FBU.NZ) down 0.5% at NZ$8.62. Retailer Kathmandu (KMD.NZ) is up 2.6% at NZ$1.99 after the company said its 1H net profit rose to NZ$10.5 million from NZ$4.4 million on solid same-store sales growth and improving profit margins. Ryman is up 0.9% at NZ$2.33 after the company said it would build another retirement village in New Zealand. (lucy.craymer@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2350 GMT [Dow Jones] The Kospi is likely to fall after U.S. stocks fall steeply on growing worries over an escalating nuclear crisis in Japan. Analysts say the local stock market is likely to continue to experience highly volatile sessions due to uncertainties over the impact of the Japan quake and nuclear plant damage. "It was a massive quake at first and the tsunami right after, and then the nuclear crisis. The most scary thing for the market is no one knows what's coming next," says KB Investment & Securities analyst Kim Soo-young. He tips a 1920-1970 range for the session. Analysts expect oil refiners and machinery makers will likely to be the main beneficiaries for a short while, with Japanese industry peers having supply problems. The Kospi ends up 1.8% at 1957.97 Wednesday. (jieun.shin@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2343 GMT [Dow Jones] Even though \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's MOF would likely prefer to conduct any \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  only when it could point to a large and steady buildup of speculative JPY long positioning, which it cannot do now as the JPY's strengthening has owed largely to repatriation flows, it may still \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  if USD/JPY again drops sharply, says Tohru Sasaki, chief \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at \uc2 JPMorgan\uc2  in Japan. "It's possible that the MOF and the BOJ would prefer to have those conditions for any \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but if the dollar-yen goes lower and tries to break Y76, the market will be panicked, so I think they may move to prevent that panic from spreading." Any \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  would likely send the USD/JPY to around 80.00, where it would stay for a while, Sasaki says. But \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  would not mean the USD/JPY could not head even lower than the 76.25 low hit this morning in coming weeks "as looking at its moves lower following the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  last September suggests," Sasaki adds. (andrew.monahan@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2338 GMT [Dow Jones] The USD/JPY is at 78.68, off its record low of 76.25 earlier but there's still a good chance that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  MOF will conduct JPY-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  soon, says a senior dealer at major bank in Tokyo. "Don't get relaxed just because Japan didn't step into the market at Y76.25 (against the U.S. dollar). The greenback is now higher than that, but Tokyo may step into the market during the Asian session because they have better staffing in Asia than they do in New York." He adds that markets are watching the G-7; French Finance Minister Lagarde said Wednesday a telephone conference of G-7 finance ministers and central bankers is scheduled for Thursday and would discuss support for disaster-stricken Japan, including the potential purchase of Japanese bonds. When asked to what range he expected the USD/JPY to trade in for the rest of the session, he refused and said "it's nonsense" to try predicting how the pair would move. (takashi.mochizuki@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2337 GMT [Dow Jones] Nymex crude is likely to consolidate with a downward bias after hitting a 13-day low of $96.22/bbl Wednesday, Dow Jones technical analysis shows. The daily continuation chart is negative-biased as the MACD & stochastic indicators are bearish; meanwhile, the 5-day moving average is below the 15-day MA and falling. A drop below the $96.22 support would target $95.62 (Feb. 24 low), then $95.14 (Feb. 23 low), $92.84 (previous cap set Jan. 31) and $92.58 (55-day moving average). Resistance is at $99.60 (Wednesday's high); a breach would temper the near-term negative outlook, exposing the upside to $101.87 (Tuesday's high), then to $103.00 (Friday's high), $105.06 (March 10 high), $105.92 (March 9 high) and $106.95 (29-month high hit March 7). April crude is down 97 cents at $97.01/bbl on Globex. (jerry.tan@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2333 GMT [Dow Jones] The number of Japanese investors who expect rises in 10-year U.S. Treasury yields declines sharply this week to 30.3% from 44.7% in the previous week, \uc2 Barclays Capital\uc2 's latest survey shows; results "presumably reflect a 'flight-to-quality' linked to the earthquake rather than factors on the U.S. side," says strategist Yoshio Takahashi. 10-year U.S. Treasurys rose to 103 16/32 yielding 3.208% at the NY close, while the 2-year notes are also slightly higher at 100 4/32 to yield 0.553%. The survey also shows steep declines in expectations for a weaker yen; USD/JPY (now around 78.85) briefly fell as far as 76.25, breaching its previous record low of 79.75. (megumi.fujikawa@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2328 GMT [Dow Jones] Stop-loss selling orders from just below 80.00, combined with ultra-thin liquidity, sent USD/JPY sliding earlier--it was not due to large speculative selling, says Tohru Sasaki, chief \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at \uc2 J.P.Morgan\uc2  in Japan. "Until early this morning basically the major driver of the yen's strengthening has been the repatriation movements of Japanese firms, not speculative moves," Sasaki says. "What happened earlier this morning was basically stop-loss selling orders being triggered" in USD/JPY. The pair is now at 78.84.(andrew.monahan@dowjones.com) [ 03-16-11 2026ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020110317e73h00009\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
