\par\par \b \uc2 DJ  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2  FOCUS: The Bank Of Japan Has A Major Battle Ahead -2-\b0\par\par\uc2 753 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2011\par 15:31\par \uc2 Dow Jones Chinese Financial Wire\par \uc2 DJCFWE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright (c) 2011, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 -- Strength of yen is unwarranted; it has long been overvalued\par \par \uc2 -- BOJ's largest \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in September essentially failed\par \par \uc2 -- Earthquake increases chance of coordinated \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 -- Uncertainty whether repatriation or speculation is responsible By Nicholas Hastings\par \par \uc2 A DOW JONES NEWSWIRES COLUMN\par \par \uc2 LONDON (Dow Jones)--The Bank of Japan had better be braced for a big battle as it tries to stop the yen from rising to new record highs against the dollar.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  only works at the best of times and these are not the best of times for the yen.\par \par \uc2 There is little doubt that the strength of the yen is unwarranted.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was overvalued by as much as 6% even before the earthquake in Japan last Friday prompted buying, as the market began to anticipate the repatriation of funds.\par \par \uc2 Also, with Japanese interest rates down at virtually zero and the Bank of Japan pouring in an additional Y31 trillion of liquidity in the last four days alone, yields on the yen can hardly be described as attractive.\par \par \uc2 Nonetheless, as the Bank of Japan found to its cost last September, international support for the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  remains strong.\par \par \uc2 Back then, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  launched its largest ever solo \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  exercise, spending Y2.13 trillion in a futile attempt to stop the dollar from falling under Y85.\par \par \uc2 Needless to say, the dollar kept on falling and a strong yen remained a problem for Japanese exporters as the country struggled to stop slipping back into recession. By the time the earthquake - and then the tsunami - hit Japan's north-east coast nearly a week ago, the dollar was still down at Y83.\par \par \uc2 If anything, the need for a weaker yen now has become even more acute than it was last September. Since the earthquake, the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has rallied another 8%, breaking to a new record high of Y76.25 against the dollar late on Wednesday. And the growth prospects for the Japanese economy have deteriorated rapidly.\par \par \uc2 For the moment, though, the Bank of Japan is nowhere to be seen.\par \par \uc2 This could be because the bank is choosing to play a cat-and-mouse game that leaves the market guessing over just what it plans to do.\par \par \uc2 Or, it could be because the Japanese are hoping to get international support as the ongoing threat of nuclear contamination from the damaged nuclear reactors at Fukushima still poses a risk for financial markets in general.\par \par \uc2 A weaker yen will then be in the self-interest of other major economies as much as it is now for \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The need for a coordinated \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  exercise, which would have a much better chance of pushing the yen lower, will more than likely be part of the G-7 discussions on Japan.\par \par \uc2 But, there is another reason why the Bank of Japan may be reluctant to rush into the market just yet--uncertainty over just what is driving the yen higher.\par \par \uc2 If flows into the yen represent a longer-term shift of investment back into Japan from overseas, these could prove difficult to reverse.\par \par \uc2 But, if they only represent speculative interest as market players look for more yen gains in the future, then the Bank of Japan may have a better chance of turning market sentiment around.\par \par \uc2 At the moment, no one seems to be sure, although the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Tokyo has been keen to point to speculators as the main problem.\par \par \uc2 A study by \uc2 Citigroup\uc2  also suggests that repatriation flows may be not be anywhere as large as the market has been anticipating as the amount of funds that insurance companies, pension funds and even Japanese banks will need to bring home aren't that big after all.\par \par \uc2 If that is the case, then the Bank of Japan has a better chance of halting the yen's advance. If not, the bank could find itself entering a prolonged \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  battle that could cost it considerably more than its efforts to get the yen down back in September.\par \par \uc2 Bloomberg TNI FRX POV\par \par \uc2 Reuters USD/DJ\par \par \uc2 Thomson P/1066 or P/1074\par \par \uc2 (Nicholas Hastings is a Senior Correspondent in London for Dow Jones Newswires who has written about \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  for more than 20 years. He previously covered a variety of markets, including equities, fixed income, commodities and energy. He can be contacted on +44-20-7842-9493 or by email: nick.hastings@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 -0-\par \par \uc2 Copyright (c) 2011 \uc2 Dow Jones & Company, Inc\uc2 . \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJCFWE0020110318e73h00e76\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
