\par\par \b \uc2 DJ UPDATE: Asian Shares Trim Losses On Modest Japan Nuclear Relief\b0\par\par\uc2 1,150 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2011\par 06:46\par \uc2 Dow Jones Chinese Financial Wire\par \uc2 DJCFWE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright (c) 2011, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 (Adds information, quotes, updates/adds market levels) By Colin Ng and Puja Rajeev Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES\par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE (Dow Jones)--Asian stock markets, led by Tokyo, trimmed their losses on Thursday on hopes of progress in bringing a key Japanese nuclear facility under control.\par \par \uc2 In the latest attempt to cool down reactors at the stricken Fukushima Daiichi nuclear power plant, Japan's Self Defense Force dropped ocean water on the overheated units Thursday morning. The nation's nuclear safety agency said that power cables from an outside source could be available by the afternoon, a significant development which if successful would provide engineers a better chance of getting the smouldering reactors under control.\par \par \uc2 The U.S. dollar, which tumbled to a record low of Y76.25 against the yen in very early in Asian trade, recovered to trade above Y79.00, and a modest ebbing in risk-aversion allowed equities to pare their morning losses\par \par \uc2 Toshikazu Horiuchi, an equity strategist at Cosmo Securities, said: 'There will be further relief for the markets if we can confirm this measure will lead to an actual drop in temperatures' at the reactor.\par \par \uc2 Japan's Nikkei Stock Average was down 2.0%, Australia's S&P/ASX 200 was down 0.1% at 4555, South Korea's Kospi Composite was down 0.7% Hong Kong's Hang Seng Index fell 1.8% and China's Shanghai Composite fell 0.5%.\par \par \uc2 Equity, \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  and commodity markets were roiled earlier after Greg Jaczko, chairman of the Nuclear Regulatory Commission indicated the Japanese atomic crisis was even graver than Japanese officials had outlined in public, and recommended a 'much larger radius' for evacuations than Tokyo had imposed.\par \par \uc2 That sent the Dow Jones Industrial Average down 2.0% Wednesday, but after the latest containment efforts by Japan, the DJIA futures were 26 points higher in screen trade.\par \par \uc2 In Tokyo, the yen's pullback from early record highs and the easing of concerns over a worst-case nuclear disaster scenario staunched the morning session's across-the-board selling.\par \par \uc2 'Investors are regaining calmness as panic-driven selling in dollar-yen eases while the nuclear situation looks less dire,' said Kazuhiro Takahashi, general manager of investment strategy at Daiwa Securities Capital Markets.\par \par \uc2 Twenty four of the 33 Topix subindexes were down with bellwether exporters lower, but off their early morning lows.\par \par \uc2 Canon was down 3.1%, \uc2 Toyota Motor\uc2  was 3.6% lower and \uc2 Sony\uc2  shed 2.2%.\par \par \uc2 Tokyo Electric Power\uc2 , operator of the troubled nuclear power facility, was down 9.5% though well off the 22% slide suffered earlier.\par \par \uc2 Australia's headline stock index slipped to a fresh six-month low of 4477.4 in early trade but steadily pared its losses and even managed to briefly enter positive territory.\par \par \uc2 'It's been a pretty rough week so maybe there's some short covering near Tuesday's low,' said RBS head of sales, Justin Gallagher. 'Japan's nuclear crisis is clearly the main issue. But there's also the weak U.S. housing data and concern about inflationary pressures in the U.S.'\par \par \uc2 Uranium stocks were leading the losers, with \uc2 Paladin Energy\uc2  down 6.8% and \uc2 Extract Resources\uc2  down 21.3% after China halted approvals for new nuclear power plants pending changes to safety standards.\par \par \uc2 Building materials stocks were hit by the slump in U.S. housing starts, with James Hardie down 2.6%.\par \par \uc2 U.S. data Wednesday showed home construction in February fell 22.5%, the steepest monthly plunge in nearly 27 years and new building permits set a record low.\par \par \uc2 China's suspension of approvals for new nuclear power plants prompted short-term funds to buy traditional energy and clear energy-related shares.\par \par \uc2 China \uc2 Yangtze Power\uc2  rose 5.2% and Huaneng Power International climbed 2.2%.\par \par \uc2 Steel makers fell on profit-taking after recent gains, with \uc2 Wuhan Iron & Steel\uc2  down 2.6%.\par \par \uc2 In Korea most market heavyweights were lower with \uc2 Samsung Electronics\uc2  down 1.5%, \uc2 Hyundai Motor\uc2  down 1.1% and \uc2 LG Chem\uc2  1.3% lower.\par \par \uc2 Still, shipbuilders were outperforming on hopes for new orders after \uc2 Samsung Heavy Industries\uc2  late Wednesday said it received a KRW1.25 trillion order to build two drilling ships for an unidentified U.S. firm. Samsung Heavy was up 1.7%, \uc2 Daewoo Shipbuilding & Marine Engineering\uc2  gained 5.6% and \uc2 Hyundai Heavy Industries\uc2  added 3.6%.\par \par \uc2 Among other markets, New Zealand's NZX-50 was 0.3% higher, Singapore's Straits Times Index shed 1.0%, Malaysia's KLCI fell 0.4%, Taiwan's Taiex slipped 0.9%, Indonesian shares were down 1.4% and Philippine shares slipped 1.4%. India's Sensex fell 0.2% and Thailand's SET fell 0.9%.\par \par \uc2 In \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, the U.S. dollar clawed back toward Y79.070 against the yen after its record-setting fall Thursday morning. Investors' expectation that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  would \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  appeared to support the dollar.\par \par \uc2 'The focus now is on whether the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  will \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market,' said Fumiyuki Nakanishi, general manager at SMBC Friend Securities. 'Firms are now closing books for the fiscal year. The dollar below Y80 is not desirable.'\par \par \uc2 Japanese Finance Minister Yoshihiko Noda said he is closely watching the yen given 'nervous movements' and 'speculations' in the market. Other Japanese officials also claimed the overnight spike in the yen was driven by speculators, not by repatriation flows in the wake of last Friday's devastating earthquake.\par \par \uc2 'At the moment, the market is front-running a possible \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ' by the Finance Ministry, said David Forrester, a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at \uc2 Barclays Capital\uc2 . 'We think the yen is in a place where such \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is warranted.'\par \par \uc2 The dollar also hit an all-time low against the Swiss franc, dropping below CHF0.9000 to hit CHF0.8922. It was recently at CHF0.8984.\par \par \uc2 Lead June Japanese government bond futures were up 0.24 at 139.96 points, lifted by the Nikkei's fall and the yen's gains. The 10-year cash JGB was down two-and-a-half basis points at 1.195%.\par \par \uc2 Spot gold was at $1,391.30 per troy ounce, down $10.20 from its New York settlement Wednesday as markets focussed on the Japanese crisis.\par \par \uc2 April Nymex crude oil futures were down 37 cents at $97.61 per barrel on Globex.\par \par \uc2 -Colin Ng, Dow Jones Newswires; +65-6415-4142; colin.ng@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 TALK BACK: We invite readers to send us comments on this or other financial news topics. Please email us at TalkbackAsia@dowjones.com. Readers should include their full names, work or home addresses and telephone numbers for verification purposes. We reserve the right to edit and publish your comments along with your name; we reserve the right not to publish reader comments.\par \par \uc2 -0-\par \par \uc2 Copyright (c) 2011 \uc2 Dow Jones & Company, Inc\uc2 . \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJCFWE0020110318e73h00566\par }\pag