\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: Baxter Sees 'Manageable' Japan Impact\b0\par\par\uc2 1,134 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2011\par 14:37\par \uc2 Dow Jones News Service\par \uc2 DJ\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 9:37 (Dow Jones) Medical-products maker Baxter (BAX) has about 875 employees in Japan and a plant making dialysis solutions, but the plant is in the southern part of the country, far from the main disaster site. CFO Robert Hombach, speaking at Barclay's health-care conference, says quake's impact should be "manageable." BAX got 4% of sales from Japan last year, Piper Jaffray estimates, which is less than the 10% level for the overall medical-devices market. BAX up 2.5% to $51.47. (jon.kamp@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 9:35 (Dow Jones) Speculation about \uc2 Pfizer\uc2  (PFE) spin-offs continue to abound. Janney's Jody Lurie thinks PFE may spin-off its nutrition business. The unit had $1.9B in 2010 sales, and PFE "is looking to reap benefits from current market conditions, as many drug patents expire over the next few years," the analyst notes. And while PFE may be looking to unwind some prior deals via spins, it also may be looking for fresh ones, Lurie says, with $28B in cash on hand for funding. "Potential future targets may include biotech companies, in order to solve for expiring patents," analyst adds. PFE up 1.1% at $19.52. (kellie.geressy@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 9:34 (Dow Jones) While the levels of utilization during the winter months were the highest in this economic recovery, the rates--76.3% in February--remain well below the 80%-plus that would suggest factories are running into bottlenecks that could allow for prices to be marked up. (kathleen.madigan@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 9:26 (Dow Jones) Speculative investors aren't nearly as long yen as before September's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , according to weekly CFTC trading data. Traders held a $2.51B net long position in the yen against the dollar as of March 8. On Sept. 14, the day before \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , speculative traders held a $7.17B net long position. By the following week it was slashed to $3.39B. The CFTC's next report, for the period as of Tuesday, will be released Friday. (stephen.bernard@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 9:18 (Dow Jones) \uc2 Stifel Nicolaus\uc2 ' phones have been ringing with enough questions about \uc2 Bank of America\uc2  (BAC) the firm decided to explain why it's not more bullish. Firm knows investors got excited at BAC Day when a "normalized" earnings picture finally got painted. But that painting might take until 2014 to dry. That's when Stifel thinks "normal" gets achieved (and the later end of management range.) So while the firm's price target shows 20% upside, it doesn't expect the market to have three-year patience. BAC rose 4.7% during investor day and fell 6.8% since. Any big rally may be a bit away still. (david.benoit@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 9:12 (Dow Jones) The retail outlook is getting "cloudier" for brokers such as \uc2 Charles Schwab\uc2  (SCHW), Stifel (SF) and Raymond James (RJF), Ticonderoga says. There's been two straight weeks of domestic equity mutual funds getting money pulled from them amid the crude-oil surge, after the first extended period of inflows in some time, while debt markets have begun to see more deals pulled. Ticonderoga calls the debt development a "discouraging sign" for the strong rally in retail brokerages this year. And there's the likelihood of more equity outflows near-term amid the slumping stock market in recent days. (brett.philbin@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 9:09 (Dow Jones) \uc2 Credit Suisse\uc2  raises target on \uc2 Petrobras\uc2  (PBR PETR4.BR) ADRs to $48 from $43 on expectations for higher oil prices near term. Firm boosts outlook for North Sea Brent (PBR's reference crude) to $105.80 and $100.50/barrel in 2011 and 2012, respectively, up from $84.50 forecast. Despite higher target, firm says PBR's investments and policy of stable domestic fuel prices will likely offset recent spike in oil prices. In NY, PBR up 1.5% premarket at $39.20. (jeff.fick@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 8:54 (Dow Jones) Not much left that's deflationary, or disinflationary, in February CPI. Only one negative category last month, a 0.9% monthly decline in apparel costs. And considering cotton's rise, it's likely apparel's decline won't last. The biggest gains came in energy, unsurprisingly. On an annualized basis, fuel oil was up 27.1%. From a year ago, however, utility gas prices are down 5.9%. Food is up 2.3% from a year ago. (michael.derby@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 8:53 (Dow Jones) Oversold conditions can lead to a short-term bounce, but they don't necessarily mean the selloff is over. "Most short term momentum indicators have moved to their biggest oversold readings since the April-August 2010 market correction," said Phil Roth, chief technical market analyst at \uc2 Miller Tabak\uc2 . "Such action sets the stage for a reflex rally." He cautioned, however, that the oversold condition is an indication of great selling pressure developing just a month from a market peak, which is actually a "negative message" for the medium term. S&P 500 futures up 18. (tomi.kilgore@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 8:47 (Dow Jones) That suggests inflation is slipping beyond just energy and food and into other items. In today's report, the new-vehicles index rose 1% in February, its largest increase since October 2009. The medical-care index rose 0.4%, with medical-care commodities up 0.7%. The index for airline fares increased 2.1% in February, its fourth consecutive monthly increase of over 2%. (kathleen.madigan@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 8:44 (Dow Jones) Although jobless claims drop indicate some recovery in labor markets, those with jobs are getting squeezed by rising prices. Real average weekly earnings fell 0.5% over the month in February as the average workweek remained unchanged and inflation-adjusted average hourly earnings dropped, the Labor Department says. (kathleen.madigan@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 8:42 (Dow Jones) Worries about Japan may have pushed US data off the radar screen and occluded the Fed story, but even so, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is unlikely to have been surprised by the print of the CPI data. Core inflation trends remain modest at a 0.2% monthly gain. Overall CPI might be up 2.1% from February 2010, from a 1.1% annual gain as recently as November, but recall the Fed does want to see a touch more inflation relatively to what it has been getting. (michael.derby@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par To contact the Equities Market Talk Editors, \par John Shipman, 212 416-2181,john.shipman@dowjones.com \par Paul Vigna, 212 416-2184,paul.vigna@dowjones.com \par Visit the Market Talk website at \f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.djnmarkettalk.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://www.djnmarkettalk.com}}}\par \par \uc2 \f2 [ 03-17-11 0937ET ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 4035   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJ00000020110317e73h0006t\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
