\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: NZX-50 +0.7% On Bargain Hunting -For Barr\b0\par\par\uc2 1,885 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2011\par 00:32\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 2332 GMT [Dow Jones] The NZX-50 is up 0.7% at 3352.81 and is likely to remain stronger on bargain hunting following falls earlier in the week, says \f28 \uc2 \f2 Forsyth Barr\f28 \uc2 \f2  broker Dave Schaper. "There is buying into weakness; a lot of our companies aren't affected by woes in Japan so it makes sense really." This is helping large-cap stocks Fletcher Building (FBU.NZ), +0.5% at NZ$8.72, and Telecom (TEL.NZ), +0.3% at NZ$2.00. Kathmandu (KMD.NZ) is +5.9% at NZ$2.15 after it announced 1H results Thursday that beat previous guidance. (lucy.craymer@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 2322 GMT [Dow Jones] Offshore accounts at nine foreign brokerages placed net buy orders for 25.3 million Japanese shares before the market open, according to traders. The fourth consecutive day of premarket buying may be positive for the market at the open, although the yen value of the figures is unknown. Buy orders totaled 42.1 million shares, with sell orders amounting to 16.8 million shares.(juro.osawa@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2321 GMT [Dow Jones] The Nikkei may remain volatile and may move in a 8,700-9,100 range today, as traders continue to closely monitor news from the stricken Fukushima Daiichi nuclear plant, says Kenichi Hirano, operating officer at Tachibana Securities. "The market is very vulnerable to speculative selling," he says. "Any worsening in the nuclear plant situation may trigger a new round of selling, and there are few offsetting buy incentives." He says that traders also await G-7 statements and possible cooperative \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market. Brokerage dealers may pick up selective stocks that have fallen sharply but remain fundamentally sound. Still, "As we don't know what may happen at the nuclear plant over the weekend, it's hard to buy stocks today," he said. The Nikkei dropped 131.05 points, or 1.4%, to 8962.67 yesterday. CME Nikkei futures finished at 8875 overnight vs the Osaka close of 8930. (juro.osawa@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2314 GMT [Dow Jones] The USD/JPY and EUR/USD "are quite stable and a lot of that reflects the fact that no news is good news from Japan and we haven't seen a deterioration or change in what we are hearing from that part of the world," says \uc2 HiFX\uc2  Head of Trading Mike Hollows. He says the U.N. vote authorizing military force against Libya poses some event risk and "if anything I think people will be reducing risk today." He says market focused on the ongoing G-7 meeting but that's unlikely to shake things up too much; "There will certainly be a unified voice but I don't know if that is going to necessarily change the fundamental issues and clearly stabilizing these reactors is everything right now." The USD/JPY is at 79.17. He tips resistance to hold at 79.75 with support at 78.30. (rebecca.howard@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2309 GMT [Dow Jones] Verbal support for USD/JPY from the G-7 is likely, and there is a case to be made for joint \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , says \uc2 Barclays Capital\uc2  in a report. It says with risk aversion spreading from Japan, and with market dynamics, rather than fundamentals, the most likely culprit, joint \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  seems like a low-risk way to help \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  turn sentiment. It adds, on the other hand, few would argue that \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  are fundamentally misaligned, requiring a long commitment of joint \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and also says most central banks are likely to judge that the direct effects from Japan on their economies will likely be limited. Therefore, any joint action would probably be designed as a temporary action. The USD/JPY is at 79.20 as markets watch for the outcome of an ongoing teleconference of G-7 finance ministers and central bankers. (james.glynn@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2302 GMT [Dow Jones] Australia's S&P/ASX 200 is likely to be range bound in cautious trading Friday despite a 1.3% rise in the S&P 500. Early morning developments, including the U.N. Security Council's resolution to endorse a no-fly zone in Libya, and reports of steam coming from the Fukushima Daichi No. 2 reactor, could mean another dip in equities, although weekend short covering may prevent a retest of major support from the Fibonacci retracement at 4469.0. Some technical support has formed at 4530.0, according to Dow Jones Newswires technical analysis, while Wednesday's high at 4582.2 is key resistance ahead of the 200-day moving average at 4637.0. Nymex April crude oil futures have spiked up as much as US$1.50 to US$102.90 while the Australian dollar has fallen to 0.9788 from 0.9803 in NY. Still, with a G-7 finance minister's meeting underway sellers will be wary of any \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken the Yen, which could temporarily support equities. Oil and gas stocks should outperform gain while uranium stocks could remain out of favor. Index last 4555.3. (david.rogers1@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2259 GMT [Dow Jones] AUD/USD is slightly weaker after the United Nations Security Council voted 10 to zero in support of the use of "all necessary measures" including the use of a no-fly zone to protect civilians and rebel forces in Libya from forces loyal to Col. Moammar Gadhafi. "It is getting uglier and it is worrying," says \uc2 ANZ Bank\uc2  Senior Dealer Alex Sinton. The AUD/USD is at 0.9797 after dipping to 0.9783. He says support will still hold at 0.9732 while resistance is at 0.9830 "but with the Aussie right now it all depends on what happens with the crosses," with the market waiting for any statement out of the G-7. He says both the NZD and the AUD have demand from exporters and therefore "any dips are going to find a fair amount of demand." (rebecca.howard@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 TALK BACK: We invite readers to send us comments and share their views on financial news topics. Please email us at:\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    TalkBackAsia@dowjones.com \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Readers should include their full names, work or home addresses and telephone numbers for verification purposes. We reserve the right to edit and publish your comments along with your name; we reserve the right not to publish reader comments.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 2250 GMT [Dow Jones] ASIA OUTLOOK: Regional stock markets could move higher, bouncing from their recent losses and taking their cue from Thursday's gains on Wall Street; still, markets remain vulnerable to any negative headlines from Japan, as the country struggles with its nuclear crisis. In \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets, investors are watching for the outcome of an ongoing meeting of G-7 finance ministers and \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  governors for indications about Japan's intentions in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets; a person familiar with the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  told Dow Jones that Japanese authorities were "prepared for battle" against emboldened traders determined to buy yen. The USD/JPY is at 79.19, from 78.93 in late NY trade Thursday, the EUR/JPY is at 110.96, from 110.58 and the EUR/USD is at 1.4010, from 1.4020. The EUR/USD slips on news Egypt's military has begun shipping arms over the border to Libyan rebels with Washington's knowledge, according to U.S. and Libyan rebel officials, and news that the U.N. endorsed a no-fly zone over Libya. Not much on the data calendar, with Japan's revisions of January's indexes of business conditions at 0500 GMT and its monthly economic report. Elsewhere there's U.K.'s Nationwide consumer confidence index and the E.U.'s balance of payments and trade balance. (colin.ng@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 2246 GMT [Dow Jones] JGBs are likely to open lower, following weakness in U.S. Treasurys, says \f28 \uc2 \f2 Tokai Tokyo Securities\f28 \uc2 \f2  chief strategist Kazuhiko Sano; while overall market liquidity is expected to remain low, "movements in futures could be big, depending on how much stocks rebound," he adds. Sano expects the yield curve to flatten. Market payers are also focused on whether G-7 nations are willing to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in coordinated fashion in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market to weaken the yen; G-7 finance ministers and central bankers are having a teleconference now. Lead June JGB futures are tipped in a 139.08-139.69 band vs the 139.70 last close, while the 10-year yield is eyed in a 1.205%-1.245% range vs 1.200%. (megumi.fujikawa@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 2223 GMT [Dow Jones] Spot gold is trading in the same $5 band it has held since the PM London gold fix Thursday shortly after the start of trade in Asia. The day's sharp lurch down as low as $1,386/oz, amid a surge in the yen as investors repatriated money and funds hit stop-loss orders on carry trades, was largely effaced over the course of the day, with an informal close at 2200 GMT at $1,402.79/oz a little above the previous day's $1,399.35/oz. Still, \f28 \uc2 \f2 Commerzbank\f28 \uc2 \f2  argues that the metal is showing a similar pattern to that seen during the financial crisis, when it hit a recent low of $681.75/oz. "Calculated in euros, the yellow metal is still hovering around the EUR1,000/oz mark. This means gold is not serving as a safe haven...investors currently appear to be selling gold to raise cash and cover margin calls." Spot gold was $1,403.70/oz at 2220 GMT, pushing at the upper end of the $1,399.50/oz-$1,404.95/oz band held since the PM gold fix at 1530 GMT. (david.fickling@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 2221 GMT [Dow Jones] S&P/ASX 200 offshore leads are bullish, with overnight SPI 200 futures up 0.4% at 4575.0. Wall Street's S&P 500 rose 1.3% to 1273.72 on stronger-than-expected U.S. economic data, some hope of stabilization in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's nuclear crisis, and expectations of G7 \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to weaken the yen, with markets awaiting a G7 finance minister's teleconference from 2200 GMT. U.S. weekly jobless claims fell more than expected and the Philadelphia Fed's manufacturing survey beat expectations. \f28 \uc2 \f2 FedEx\f28 \uc2 \f2  rose 5.0% on stronger-than-expected third quarter revenue and fiscal 2011 earnings guidance. S&P 500 Financials rose 1.4% and Materials rose 1.7%, with BHP Billiton (BHP.AU) ADRs rising to equivalent A$44.58 vs A$43.70 locally. Energy rose 3.1%, with Nymex April crude oil up 3.5% to US$101.42 as Japan claimed progress in controlling the nuclear crisis, while Western countries appeared closer to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervening\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in Libya. LME base metals rose, with copper and nickel up more than 3.0%. Spot gold rose to US$1,403.95 vs US$1,395.60 this time Thursday. S&P/ASX 200 last 4555.3. [ 03-17-11 1932ET ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (david.rogers1@dowjones.com) [ 03-17-11 1932ET ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020110317e73h000dt\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
