\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: Sensex Extends Losses, Off 0.5%; 18,170 Support\b0\par\par\uc2 2,021 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2011\par 08:18\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 0718 GMT [Dow Jones] The Sensex extends losses after the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  raises the key lending and borrowing rates by 25 bps each; the benchmark index is down 0.5% at 18,272.20. "The market had already factored in rate hikes of 25 bps so this does not come as a surprise," a local analyst says; he adds, "what is concerning is the RBI's view on further upside risks to inflation due to surging crude oil prices." The RBI has raised its March-end inflation estimate to 8% from 7% previously. "The RBI said that high energy and commodity prices is a threat to the country's growth trajectory which is also concerning," he adds. The rate sensitive sectors like banking, auto and realty remain weak after the policy review. A Dow Jones technical analyst tips the Sensex to trade in a 18,170-18,500 range for the rest of the day. ICICI Bank (532174.BY) is down 0.9% at INR1,017.95, \uc2 Maruti Suzuki India\uc2 (532500.BY) is down 2.6% at INR1,191.30 and DLF (532868.BY) is down 0.8% at INR224.75. (swagata.gupta@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0712 GMT [Dow Jones] The \uc2 Reserve Bank of India\uc2  is likely to continue with its measured monetary tightening to ensure that India's growth trajectory and investment momentum do not suffer even as it continues its battle against stubbornly high prices in the economy, says Anubhuti Sahay, economist at Standard Chartered Bank. She says, the RBI still has 50 bps worth of hikes to implement in both key rates through 2011. "With the RBI highlighting that energy and commodity prices pose a threat to growth, we think it will stick to its calibrated rate tightening," Sahay says. (khushita.vasant@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0710 GMT [Dow Jones] While the EUR/USD looks fairly prone to bouts of selling on headline-driven risk aversion at present, the broader underlying theme of potential ECB tightening is likely to kick in once the situation in Japan stabilizes, a trader says. Pending some stability in Japan and \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets in general, he expects the pair could test 1.40 over next few days, and then 1.41 in next three weeks. However, given the volatility in markets, there could be scope for the pair to pull back to the 1.37 area if Japan-related fears grip the market again. The EUR/USD is at 1.3920. (samuel.holmes@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0705 GMT [Dow Jones] RBI is likely to continue with its calibrated monetary tightening policy to cool demand side inflationary pressure, says Namrata Padhye, an economist at IDBI Gilts. "The RBI has revised its current fiscal year-end inflation target to 8% from 7%, which indicates they accept that inflation is here to stay," Padhye says. She adds, "The RBI however will remain cautious and will not hurt growth by tightening aggressively." She reckons the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will closely monitor global commodity prices, especially crude oil. The RBI increased its repurchase--or overnight lending--rate by 25 bps to 6.75%, and the reverse repurchase--or borrowing--rate also by 25 bps to 5.75%. (sourav.mishra@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0658 GMT [Dow Jones] The Kospi ends flat at 1959.03 Thursday, recovering earlier losses after it was down as much as 2.0%. Analysts say the index received positive cues from news about Japan's efforts to cool down the troubled reactor units at the Fukushima Daiichi nuclear plant, which also helped the Japanese stock market to trim its earlier losses. "The index seems to have checked its firm support at the lower-1900 levels, but some more volatile sessions are exepected," says Shinyoung Securities analyst Im Tae-geun. He tips a 1920-1980 range for the near term. Some heavyweight stocks recovered from earlier falls; \uc2 Samsung Electronics\uc2  (005930.SE) ended up 0.5% at KRW890,000, Posco (005490.SE) ended up 0.9% at KRW486,500 and \uc2 LG Chem\uc2  (051910.SE) ended up 1.3% at KRW418,000. Oil refiners were higher as they were viewed as beneficiaries of any supply troubles of Japanese industry peers; \uc2 SK Innovation\uc2  (096770.SE) ended up 4.0% to KRW207,500 and \uc2 S-Oil Corp\uc2  (010950.SE) ended up 1.1% at KRW139,500. (jieun.shin@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0655 GMT [Dow Jones] India government bonds are down after the RBI raised its year-end inflation estimate while hiking its key interest rates by a widely-expected 25 bps. The most-traded 8.08% 2022 bond swooned to a low of 99.95 vs 100.10 before the policy release and 99.99 at Wednesday's close. The bond had a positive knee-jerk reaction to in-line hike in rates but concerns about higher inflation weighed on sentiment, says a dealer with a state-run bank. "The inflationary pressures are now real and evident. Instead of surprising the market, RBI has stuck to its gradual tightening course and more hikes are on the cards" he says. The RBI raised its year-end inflation forecast to 8% from 7%. Inflation was 8.31% in February from a year earlier, quicker than January's provisional 8.23%. The government also revised up December's inflation reading to 9.41% from a provisional 8.43% reported earlier. The dealer expects the 8.08% 2022 bond yield to remain in a 8.05%-8.11% band in the rest of the session. (nupur.acharya@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0654 GMT [Dow Jones] India's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , in a widely anticipated move, raises its key rates by 25 bps for the eighth time in 12 months as it keeps up efforts to clamp down on raging price pressures in the economy. The key lending or repo rate now stands at 6.75% and the key borrowing or reverse repo rate is at 5.75%. "Based on the current and evolving growth and inflation scenario, the Reserve Bank is likely to persist with the current anti-inflationary stance," the RBI says. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  raises its forecast for inflation for the current fiscal year ending March to 8% from 7%. The RBI expects the economy to expand 8.6% in FY11. Fourteen of the 15 economists polled by Dow Jones expected the RBI to lift rates by 25 basis points--only one called for a sharper 50 basis point hike. The RBI keeps cash reserve ratio, statutory liquidity ratio and bank rate unchanged. (khushita.vasant@dowjones.com; subhadip.sircar@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0653 GMT [Dow Jones] The RBI is likely to hike the repo and reverse repo rates by another 50 bps each through 2011 as it continues to battle rising prices in a fast expanding economy, says Manoranjan Sharma, chief economist at state-run \uc2 Canara Bank\uc2 ; in an expected move, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  raised the two key policy rates by 25 bps a short while ago. "The RBI will act according to the data points, its actions will be primarily driven by inflation readings," says Sharma; he adds the rate hikes may be spaced if inflation shows signs of easing sharply. (abhrajit.gangopadhyay@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0652 GMT [Dow Jones] The Swiss franc's gains against the euro and dollar overnight are "over-extended and exhaustive" and likely to be filled in when London enters the market, according to \uc2 Commerzbank\uc2 . "In order to alleviate extreme downside pressure these markets will need to stabilize above these time zone gaps (these occur when there has been a sharp move overnight that London hasn't seen), while below them further spikes down cannot be ruled out," says technical analyst Karen Jones. The EUR/CHF is at 1.2540 and the USD/CHF is at 0.9006. (neil.maclucas@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0646 GMT [Dow Jones] Japanese demand for oil products will likely tighten fuel oil and middle distillates markets. As the country's refining sector was hit by Friday's earthquake, Petroleum Association of Japan says it would likely import 450,000 kiloliters of oil products while exports of 650,000 kiloliters up end-March were cancelled; the current supply shortage is mainly related to local logistic problems, and domestic production of light products may be excessive of demand, it says. Japan's total crude runs fall to 2.7 million bbls/day from the pre-quake level of around 4.0 million bbls/day, but will recover to 3.4 million bbl/day by end-March, the industry group adds. Japan is importing gasoline and diesel for prompt delivery; but in the long run, it will need fuel oil also to meet demand from power plants, with almost a quarter of its nuclear power generation capacity shut down after the quake. (max.lin@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0638 GMT [Dow Jones] Singapore's STI is down 1.1% at 2939.35, as regional bourses trade lower due to uncertainty surrounding Japan's damaged nuclear reactors. Though the market remains vulnerable to news flow from Japan, further declines should be limited today as U.S. futures are pointing higher while the Nikkei ends well off its lows, helped by visible efforts to cool the overehating reactors. \uc2 RBC Capital Markets\uc2  says Asian market sentiment "remains very fragile as the Japanese nuclear crisis continues." UOB KayHian says Wednesday's price action, which resulted in a 'doji' candlestick pattern for the STI, "suggests indecisiveness in the direction of market. However, the trend remains downward with several bearish indicators against the index." It says the index could decline to the psychological level of 2900, where it tips support, with resistance at 3100. Market volume is mild at 723 million shares with six fallers per gainer. STI names down over 2.0% include SIA Engineering (S59.SG), CityDev (C09.SG), Golden Agri (E5H.SG), \uc2 CapitaMall T