n protesters in Manama, and impose a curfew across much of the capital. The Markit iTraxx Asia ex-Japan investment-grade CDS index is at 115 bps, 4 bps wider than Wednesday. Spreads are likely to remain wide for the time being as tensions linger, the trader adds. (natasha.brereton@dowjones.com) [ 03-17-11 0258ET ]\par \par \uc2 0609 GMT [Dow Jones] Nikkei 225 June futures are down 1.0% at 8,910, but well above their intraday low of 8,400 as expectations grow that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  may \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market as Japanese Finance Minister Noda said earlier Thursday that G-7 nations will discuss via teleconference on Friday morning the impact of the massive earthquake on the Japanese economy and financial markets, says Yumi Nishimura, deputy general manager of investment strategy at Daiwa Securities Capital Markets. Overseas investors bought on dips today, preventing the contract from falling further, as efforts to cool down overheating nuclear reactors continues, she says. She tips resistance around 9,000 for the remainder of the week, provided negative investor sentiment continues to dissipate. (kosaku.narioka@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0600 GMT [Dow Jones] PREVIEW: Hong Kong's unemployment rate likely held steady at 3.8% in the December-February period, unchanged from the November-January period, according to median forecast of nine economists polled by Dow Jones. Economists say the city's solid economic growth continues to spur job growth, but the rising price pressures could squeeze the firms' profit margin and cloud the labor market outlook. "If oil and commodities prices continue to skyrocket for a sustained period of time, it could dampen global demand and pose downside risk to our growth and employment forecast," Hang Seng bank chief economist Joanne Yim says. The government is scheduled to issue the unemployment data at 0830 GMT. (chester