\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: RBI Lifts Key Rates By 25 Bps;Ups Inflation Forecast\b0\par\par\uc2 1,989 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2011\par 07:54\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 0654 GMT [Dow Jones] India's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , in a widely anticipated move, raises its key rates by 25 bps for the eighth time in 12 months as it keeps up efforts to clamp down on raging price pressures in the economy. The key lending or repo rate now stands at 6.75% and the key borrowing or reverse repo rate is at 5.75%. "Based on the current and evolving growth and inflation scenario, the Reserve Bank is likely to persist with the current anti-inflationary stance," the RBI says. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  raises its forecast for inflation for the current fiscal year ending March to 8% from 7%. The RBI expects the economy to expand 8.6% in FY11. Fourteen of the 15 economists polled by Dow Jones expected the RBI to lift rates by 25 basis points--only one called for a sharper 50 basis point hike. The RBI keeps cash reserve ratio, statutory liquidity ratio and bank rate unchanged. (khushita.vasant@dowjones.com; subhadip.sircar@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0653 GMT [Dow Jones] The RBI is likely to hike the repo and reverse repo rates by another 50 bps each through 2011 as it continues to battle rising prices in a fast expanding economy, says Manoranjan Sharma, chief economist at state-run \uc2 Canara Bank\uc2 ; in an expected move, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  raised the two key policy rates by 25 bps a short while ago. "The RBI will act according to the data points, its actions will be primarily driven by inflation readings," says Sharma; he adds the rate hikes may be spaced if inflation shows signs of easing sharply. (abhrajit.gangopadhyay@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0652 GMT [Dow Jones] The Swiss franc's gains against the euro and dollar overnight are "over-extended and exhaustive" and likely to be filled in when London enters the market, according to \uc2 Commerzbank\uc2 . "In order to alleviate extreme downside pressure these markets will need to stabilize above these time zone gaps (these occur when there has been a sharp move overnight that London hasn't seen), while below them further spikes down cannot be ruled out," says technical analyst Karen Jones. The EUR/CHF is at 1.2540 and the USD/CHF is at 0.9006. (neil.maclucas@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0646 GMT [Dow Jones] Japanese demand for oil products will likely tighten fuel oil and middle distillates markets. As the country's refining sector was hit by Friday's earthquake, Petroleum Association of Japan says it would likely import 450,000 kiloliters of oil products while exports of 650,000 kiloliters up end-March were cancelled; the current supply shortage is mainly related to local logistic problems, and domestic production of light products may be excessive of demand, it says. Japan's total crude runs fall to 2.7 million bbls/day from the pre-quake level of around 4.0 million bbls/day, but will recover to 3.4 million bbl/day by end-March, the industry group adds. Japan is importing gasoline and diesel for prompt delivery; but in the long run, it will need fuel oil also to meet demand from power plants, with almost a quarter of its nuclear power generation capacity shut down after the quake. (max.lin@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0638 GMT [Dow Jones] Singapore's STI is down 1.1% at 2939.35, as regional bourses trade lower due to uncertainty surrounding Japan's damaged nuclear reactors. Though the market remains vulnerable to news flow from Japan, further declines should be limited today as U.S. futures are pointing higher while the Nikkei ends well off its lows, helped by visible efforts to cool the overehating reactors. \uc2 RBC Capital Markets\uc2  says Asian market sentiment "remains very fragile as the Japanese nuclear crisis continues." UOB KayHian says Wednesday's price action, which resulted in a 'doji' candlestick pattern for the STI, "suggests indecisiveness in the direction of market. However, the trend remains downward with several bearish indicators against the index." It says the index could decline to the psychological level of 2900, where it tips support, with resistance at 3100. Market volume is mild at 723 million shares with six fallers per gainer. STI names down over 2.0% include SIA Engineering (S59.SG), CityDev (C09.SG), Golden Agri (E5H.SG), \uc2 CapitaMall Trust\uc2  (C38U.SG), F&N (F99.SG), Hongkong Land (H78.SG). (matthew.allen@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0633 GMT [Dow Jones] ASIAN SUMMARY: Regional markets trimmed earlier losses on hopes of progress in bringing a key Japanese nuclear facility under control. The Nikkei is down 1.4%, the S&P/ASX is off 0.1%, the HSI is down 1.9%, the Kospi is +0.1%, the Taiex is down 0.5%, the Sensex is 0.3% lower, the Shanghai Composite is down 1.3% and the STI is down 1.1%. In \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, the USD/JPY bounced to 79.22 after falling to a record low of 76.25 in early Asia trade amid expectations \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  would \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support the USD. Japanese Finance Minister Yoshihiko Noda said he is closely watching the yen given "nervous movements" and "speculations" in the market. Other Japanese officials claimed the JPY's spike was driven by speculators, not repatriation flows. The USD/CHF hit an all-time low of 0.8922, but later rose to CHF0.9028. The EUR/USD is at 1.3927 vs 1.3896 late Wednesday in New York; the EUR/JPY is at 110.39 from 110.67. Minutes from the BOJ's Feb. 14-15 meeting show the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would continue to support the economy by "pursuing powerful monetary easing." China is likely to face more hot money inflows near term due to its large external surpluses and interest-rate gap with the U.S., says Deng Xianhong, a SAFE vice director. The New Zealand government plans to borrow some NZ$10 billion to rebuild its Christchurch after the February earthquake. Singapore's February non-oil exports rose 7.8% on year vs January's +20.7%, below the 11.2% forecast by a Dow Jones poll. New Zealand's 1Q Consumer Confidence Index fell 10.4 points to 97.4 points, its lowest since 1Q09. Spot gold is at $1,393.90/oz, down $7.60 from the NY close. April Nymex crude oil futures are up 60 cents at $98.58/bbl. (philip.vahn@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0629 GMT [Dow Jones] The USD/HKD is higher on continued demand as players seek safe-haven of U.S. greenback amid uncertainty surrounding Japanese nuclear crisis, says a senior trader at a local bank. He tips the pair to trade in a 7.8000-7.8050 range in the near term. "I've spotted a German bank buying more than US$50 million in total and other investment banks are following its path. There are still many buy orders in the pipeline," the trader says. The pair is at 7.8025 vs 7.7956 in late Asian trade Wednesday. (chester.yung@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0619 GMT [Dow Jones] Asian CDS are wider Thursday as a potent mix of a nuclear crisis in Japan and political tensions in the Middle East keeps investors on the edge of their seats. "There is a flight-to-quality bid for U.S. Treasuries and out of emerging markets because of the aftermath of the Japanese quake and tsunami, as well as escalating tensions in the Middle East," an Asia-based trader says. In Japan, efforts continue to stabilize conditions at the country's quake-crippled nuclear reactors; in Bahrain, several people were killed overnight as security forces crack down on protesters in Manama, and impose a curfew across much of the capital. The Markit iTraxx Asia ex-Japan investment-grade CDS in