 moves in Japan's Nikkei, supported by hopes of progress in bringing a key Japanese nuclear facility under control, says Prudential Brokerage's associate director Alvin Cheung. He says the local index may have around 22,000 as a near-term support. "For medium-to-long term investors, it might be good to adopt a contrarian view to accumulate at the current level as it is quite attractive." He says the HSI is trading at around 13.2X forward P/E, which is undemanding. Ahead of results later, \uc2 PetroChina\uc2  (0857.HK) is down 0.4% at HK$10.50, while Tencent (0700.HK) is down 9.1% at HK$197.10 after lower-than-expected 4Q results. Volume of the local bourse is brisk at HK$53.15 billion. (susanna.tai@dowjones.com) [ 03-17-11 0254ET ]\par \par \uc2 0549 GMT [Dow Jones] Taiwan shares end down 0.5% at 8282.69, recovering from an early 2.1% fall, as Japan's stock market has reversed most of its early losses, says Concord Securities manager Allan Lin. "The recovering Tokyo stocks helped boost the sentiment here, with most financial and construction shares rebounding sharply," he adds. He notes most-traded TSMC (2330.TW) was off its early low because of buying from government funds in late trade. He tips the Taiex in a 8200-8350 band Friday; "the index must stand above its five-day moving average (at 8350) to stage a solid rebound next week," he adds. TSMC ends down 1.6% at NT$67.40, off an early low of NT$65.70; its rival UMC (2303.TW) is up 2.5% at NT$14.45 while Hon Hai (2317.TW) is down 4.3% at NT$101.00. Chinatrust Financial Holding (2891.TW) is up 2.8% at NT$23.90 and Taishin Financial Holding (2887.TW) is up 2.3% at NT$15.75. \uc2 Farglory Land Development\uc2  (5522.TW) is up 2.8% at NT$59.70 and \uc2 Huang Hsiang Construction\uc2  (2545.TW) is up 3.5% at NT$75.40. (fanny.liu@dowjones.com) [ 03-17-11 0254ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020110317e73h0004d\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\