\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: Thai Shares Down 0.4% Midday; Support At 1000\b0\par\par\uc2 1,834 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2011\par 06:43\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0543 GMT [Dow Jones] Thai shares are down 0.4% at 1003.68 at the midday break in moderate trade. The benchmark has been in the negative territory throughout the morning session, pressured by the weakness in regional bourses. Investors are watching developments when the teleconference of G-7 finance ministers and central bankers takes place later, says Capital Nomura Securities' strategist Chai Chirasevenupraphand. He tips psychological support at 1000, then 990. The resistance is put at 1005. Among the most active stocks, Banpu (BANPU.TH) is down 0.5% at THB738, PTT (PTT.TH) is down 0.6% at THB337, Thai Oil (TOP.TH) is down 0.3% at THB80.50, IRPC (IRPC.TH) is down 0.8% at THB5.95 and \f28 \uc2 \f2 Indorama Ventures\f28 \uc2 \f2  (IVL.TH) is flat at THB49.75. (piyarat.setthasiriphaiboon@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0538 GMT [Dow Jones] The Petroleum Association of Japan is painting sobering picture of the country's refining problems; it says while throughput is improving, it expects end-March runs to be 600,000 b/d below the 4 million b/d level seen just before the earthquake. (mari.iwata@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0535 GMT [Dow Jones] \uc2 BNP Paribas\uc2  says the 9.0 magnitude earthquake in northern Japan and the nuclear crisis at the Fukushima power plant has led it to "sharply revise down" its growth forecast for Japan for 2011. It now expects GDP to contract by 0.9% vs its earlier forecast for 1.6% growth on a fiscal year basis, and to contract 1.2% vs a growth of 1.6% on a calendar year basis. But it marks up its FY12 growth forecast to +2.9% (it doesn't provide its earlier forecast), to factor the impact of expected reconstruction; it adds, "we expect the economy will contract significantly in the first two quarters of the year. The economy should, then, return to positive growth from 3Q." It notes that these forecasts are predicated on the Fukushima crisis being contained and the rolling blackouts by Tepco ending as planned at end-April. (puja.rajeev@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0528 GMT [Dow Jones] Investment sentiment towards USD/KRW is somewhat stabilizing now after South Korean \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  authorities warned against volatile moves and as Japan's nuclear plant-related problems appear to be coming under control, say traders. The USD/KRW is at 1,136.75 vs 1,130.8 late yesterday, and the year-high of 1,144.0 tapped earlier in the day. There was "explosive" volume of export settlements when the pair was around 1,141 and 1,142, which drew it from its year-high and prompted offshore players to cut long-USD positions, says local bank trader; he tips the pair in a 1,135-1,140 for the rest of session. A foreign bank trader says the market turned calm in the afternoon along with the suspected BOK \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  earlier and the Kospi's rebound from its day-low. "The overnight uneasiness over Japan's nuke crisis extended into the morning session, but as the pair reverses course after marking the year-high, the market's trimming its volatility." (kanga.kong@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0517 GMT [Dow Jones] Singapore non-oil exports are likely to remain volatile, but the full-year government target of 8%-10% growth can be achieved, CIMB economist Song Seng Wun tells Dow Jones after government data show February exports slowed to +7.8% on year from +20.7% in January as shipments of key electronics products declined. "To some extent, this is because of the Chinese New Year holidays and we should read the cumulative numbers, which are not too bad. The beginning of the year is typically a slower period and the Japanese earthquake may increase volatility in the second quarter because of supply disruptions in the electronics industry," Song says. Data show electronics exports down 12.8% on year in February from +5.8% in January, while non-electronics shipments are +19.7%, slowing from +30.3% in January. (gaurav.raghuvanshi@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0514 GMT [Dow Jones] Standard Chartered now expects the outcome of the MAS meeting in April to maintain its current policy, compared with the house's previous view for some form of tightening. "The key reason for our change in the call is that the developments in Japan have raised the growth risks to the region as confidence may be hit," Standard Chartered says in a note, adding the "impact on confidence will dominate domestic inflation concerns until we have a clear resolution to the situation in Japan." Standard Chartered previously expected MAS to recenter its policy band at the prevailing USD/SGD level and a narrowing of the NEER. It now expects the SGD to weaken further, both near-term and after the MAS meeting, as the market has been long the SGD on expectations of further tightening. The USD/SGD is at 1.2826. (samuel.holmes@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0514 GMT [Dow Jones] Indonesian shares are down 1.5% at 3477.399 in moderate volume, off an intraday low of 3465.614, led by foreign selling in most blue chips amid growing concerns over Japan's nuclear crisis, traders say. They expect the main index to bottom out at 3550. "Bargain buying by local funds in select bank blue chips has lifted the main index from its lows," says a trader with Reliance Securities. Foreigners are net sellers of IDR253 billion worth of shares. Among decliners: heavyweight Telkom (TLKM.JK) is down 3.6% at IDR6,800 on worries that stiffer competition could hurt company's earnings, \uc2 Bank Mandiri\uc2  (BMRI.JK) is down 2.5% at IDR5,950, while Bank Rakyat (BBRI.JK) is down 1.0% at IDR5,050. (edhi.pranasidhi@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0511 GMT [Dow Jones] Any substantial worsening of Japan's Fukushima Daiichi nuclear reactor problems over the coming days could send the USD/JPY down around 75.00 early next week, with a national holiday in \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Monday complicating any effort to conduct JPY-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  if such a fall forced the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 's hand, says Mitsuru Sahara, a senior \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealer at \uc2 Bank of Tokyo-Mitsubishi\uc2  UFJ. "The next few days will be crucial with the nuclear reactor situation. If current efforts to cool the reactors fail and there are meltdowns, there could be panic early next week that would drive the dollar down below Y76 and possibly to Y75." Sahara says that even though JPY buying by Japanese firms to repatriate funds isn't clear, speculation that further nuclear problems could spark such flows would be enough to drive short-term positioning. While sharp USD/JPY falls would increase the chance of MOF \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , "It could be hard for them to do it on Monday during Asian hours with Japan on a holiday." (andrew.monahan@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0504 GMT [Dow Jones] Spot gold is at $1,392.50/oz, down $9 since the NY close, but well off its intraday low of $1,389.80 after heavy Japanese selling early on the back of the ongoing nuclear crisis. The Nikkei-225 is down 2.1% at 8900.09 but the yen is off its highs, taking some pressure off gold. "Although we have stabilized since this morning I still don't think we have seen the low on this move," says a Singapore based trader at an international bank. He tips support at $1,380 and says there may be a test of that with physical buying present, but not in enough force to push gold higher. (james.campbell@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0502 GMT [Dow Jones] Singapore's key non-oil exports rose 7.8% on year in February vs +20.7% in the previous month as shipments of key electronics products decline. February's export growth was below the 11.2% forecast by Dow Jones poll of economists but is unlikely to change MAS thinking on monetary policy as it's close to the expected range of 8%-10% for 2011. The MAS is currently focused on inflation, which is likely to be the key factor for its monetary policy stance at its April meeting. Measured on month, February non-oil exports rose 2.9% in seasonally adjusted terms vs +2.1% on month in January and better than the +1.0% predicted by the poll. Government data show electronics exports down 12.8% on year in February vs +5.8% in January, while non-electronics shipments were +19.7%, slowing from +30.3% in January. Pharmaceutical exports, which are typically volatile, were nearly flat at +1.3% on year vs +38.6% in January. (gaurav.raghuvanshi@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0457 GMT [Dow Jones] India's overnight indexed swaps are lower, tracking a fall in U.S. treasury yields and a decline in crude oil prices, but the market is keenly awaiting the RBI's rate decision, due 630 GMT, for further cues, says a trader at a state-run bank. One-year OIS are at 7.31%/7.34% from 7.36%/7.38% in late Asian trade Wednesday. Five-year OIS are at 7.80%/7.84% from 7.87%/7.90%. "The market has factored in a 25bp hike and most don't expect any hawkish statement from RBI that will hurt economic growth," the trader says. He adds, the OIS market has factored in an acute cash crunch in the banking system due to company's advance tax outflows, but traders expect the cash to flow back to the system in the last 10 days of the fiscal year in the form of government spending. But he cautions, the market can swing either way on any surprises at the RBI meeting. He tips the one-year OIS in a 7.30%-7.40% band for the session. (sourav.mishra@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0448 GMT [Dow Jones] UBS Securities strategist Atsushi Ito estimates the supplementary budget for post-quake reconstruction to be around Y5.8 trillion, funded by Y3.6 trillion in over-budget JGB issuance in FY10, Y1.2 trillion in reserves and Y1 trillion from reduced spending by scaling back the child allowance and shelving the plan to abolish expressway tolls. Despite oversupply fears, hopes additional BOJ easing is expected to support shorter-end JGBs, while Wednesday's 20-year sale confirms solid demand for superlong bonds with high yields. Ito says "we therefore do not anticipate any major rise in yields beyond their current levels when the first supplementary budget is compiled," but "the government will need to maintain its focus on fiscal reform by formally revising the (medium-term fiscal frameworks), since the current spending cap of Y71 trillion will be shattered."(megumi.fujikawa@dowjones.com) [ 03-17-11 0143ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020110317e73h0003w\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
