\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: Indonesian Shares Down 1.4%; Telkom Leads\b0\par\par\uc2 1,689 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2011\par 04:17\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0317 GMT [Dow Jones] Indonesian shares are down 1.4% at 3481.554 in moderate volume, tracking weakness across global markets amid growing concerns over Japan's nuclear crisis, traders say. They expect the main index to find support at 3470. "Selling in most bank and telecommunication blue chips has driven the main index lower amid generally very weak sentiment," says a trader with a local securities firm. Heavyweight Telkom (TLKM.JK) is down 2.8% at IDR6,850 on worries stiffer competition could hurt company's earnings, \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank Mandiri\f28 \uc2 \f2  (BMRI.JK) is down 2.5% at IDR5,950 and Bank Rakyat (BBRI.JK) is down 2.0% at IDR5,000. (edhi.pranasidhi@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0317 GMT [Dow Jones] Taiwan government bonds are higher as local stocks decline and investors flock toward safe-haven investments due to the growing uncertainty over Japan's nuclear troubles; the Taiex is down 0.9% at 8249.93. "We saw a bit of profit-taking after Wednesday's rebound but weak stocks will continue to lend support," a local trader says. The yield of the most-traded 10-year government bonds falls to 1.3100% vs 1.3522% in late Asian trade Wednesday; he tips the 10-year yield to trade in a 1.3000%-1.3200% range. The 5-year yield is at 1.0390% vs 1.0624% and it's tipped to trade in a 1.0300 to 1.0500 range. (paul.mozur@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0306 GMT [Dow Jones] The USD/INR is expected to open higher on souring risk sentiment as Japan struggles to control its nuclear crisis and mirroring losses on Wall Street overnight, says a senior trader with a privately-run bank. The pair may open around 45.20 from 45.12 late Wednesday and is tipped in a 45.15-45.25 range until the RBI's rate decision, due at 0630 GMT, provides fresh cues. "The rupee isn't likely to see any sharp movements as a direct impact of any rate action. But it will closely mirror stock market's reaction and you may see some profit-booking on forward premia if the RBI hikes by 25 basis points today," he says. Foreign funds were net sellers of a provisional INR1.35 billion worth of Indian stocks on Wednesday; 1-month NDFs are quoting at 45.50/45.54. (khushita.vasant@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0304 GMT [Dow Jones] LME metals are mostly higher with only copper and tin slightly lower, reflecting the still long-term bullish sentiment in the market says, Ben Westmore, economist at National Australia bank in Melbourne. "Every time you get a significant drop on the Japanese news there are more forward looking partcipants coming in to buy," he says. Despite the gloom hanging over financial markets and concern over growth implications of the disaster, it is clear that Japan reconstruction efforts will be "metals-intensive", says Westmore and that's keeping the market supported. LME 3-month copper is at $9,245/ton, down $15 with 1,905 lots done. LME 3-month aluminum was at $2,480/ton, up $23 with 1,055 lots done. (james.campbell@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 TALK BACK: We invite readers to send us comments and share their views on financial news topics. Please email us at:\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    TalkBackAsia@dowjones.com \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Readers should include their full names, work or home addresses and telephone numbers for verification purposes. We reserve the right to edit and publish your comments along with your name; we reserve the right not to publish reader comments.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0252 GMT [Dow Jones] Indian government bonds are expected to open mildly higher, tracking Wednesday's gains for U.S. Treasurys as the nuclear crisis in Japan unfolds, says a senior dealer with a primary dealership. But gains will be kept in check as market participants tread cautiously ahead of the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Reserve Bank of India\f28 \uc2 \f2 's crucial policy review, with the rate decision due at 0630 GMT later. The most-traded 8.08% 2022 bond may open around 100.05 vs its 99.99 last close. "If there is a 25-basis-point rate hike, as expected by a section of the market, there may be a mild sell-off in bonds. It's difficult to say what direction yields may take because the market is quite divided over the likelihood and quantum of a rate action today," he says. The RBI may raise the key lending and borrowing rates by 25 bps each to 6.75% and 5.75% respectively, according to 14 of the 15 economists polled by Dow Jones. (khushita.vasant@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0252 GMT [Dow Jones] China shares trim earlier losses as short-term funds rush into traditional energy and clear energy related shares after the government suspends its new approvals of nuclear power plants. The Shanghai Composite Index is down 0.1% at 2926.86, off its intraday low of 2886.69; analysts tip the index to consolidate around 2900. "Lingering tightening concerns and uncertainties over Japan's radiation leak are likely to weigh on sentiment," says Li Xin, an analyst from China Development Bank Securities. Among leading decliners, steel makers fall on profit-taking after recent gains; \f28 \uc2 \f2 Wuhan Iron & Steel\f28 \uc2 \f2  (600005.SH) slides 2.6% to CNY4.92, and Baoshan Iron & Steel (600019.SH) eases 2.2% to CNY7.29. Offsetting decliners, thermal and hydro power plants are rising on optimism about the industry's outlook after Beijing suspends its approval of new nuclear power plants Wednesday; China \f28 \uc2 \f2 Yangtze Power\f28 \uc2 \f2  (600900.SH) adds 5.2% to CNY8.12, and Huaneng Power International (600011.SH) climbs 2.2% to CNY5.54. The Shenzhen Composite Index is 0.7% lower at 1299.00. (amy.li@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0250 GMT [Dow Jones] The USD/PHP rises to a seven-week high as weak stocks and the growing uncertainty over Japan's natural disaster and nuclear troubles hurt the local \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . The pair is at 43.955 in moderate volume after hitting 44.02 earlier (the highest level since early February) vs 43.755 in late Asian trade. Traders place resistance at 44.20 and support at 43.70. "We've yet to see the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . They're probably allowing the dollar to appreciate ahead of a possible rate hike next week," says a local bank trader. (cris.larano@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0244 GMT [Dow Jones] Japan's earthquake will cause minimal disruption to trade flows in the near term and demand for Australia's exports are poised to grow in the aftermath of the disaster, says \f28 \uc2 \f2 Moody's\f28 \uc2 \f2  Analytics economist Matthew Circosta. He says Japan's reconstruction will require more steel-making material and demand for agricultural shipments will also grow, while strong fundamentals will continue to support commodity prices. Circosta says the shutdown of the Fukushima nuclear plant has severely reduced Japan's electricity generation capacity. He expects thermal coal and liquefied natural gas, which make up the bulk of Japan's fuel imports from Australia, to fill the void. Japan's demand for iron ore and coking coal is also tipped to rise as reconstruction gets underway, while Japan's fresh food needs are expected to ensure continued strong markets for Australia's meat, dairy and grain exports. (david.rogers1@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0244 GMT [Dow Jones] The Nikkei is off 2.1% at 8903.86 midday, but well off its intraday low of 8639.56, after Japan's Self Defense Force helicopters started dropping water on overheated reactor units at the troubled Fukushima Daiichi nuclear power plant. Over 1.8 billion shares were traded on the TSE 1st section by the midday break--a robust figure, but well down from the record-breaking levels seen in the prior three session. "The massive trading volume seen over the past few sessions indicates that the market is not lacking buyers," says Yutaka Yoshii, general manager at \f28 \uc2 \f2 Mito Securities\f28 \uc2 \f2 , adding some domestic investors seem to be snapping up undervalued shares. Yoshii pegs index support for the afternoon at 8530. All but two of 33 Topix subindexes are lower with \f28 \uc2 \f2 Tokyo Electric Power\f28 \uc2 \f2  (9501.TO), operator of the troubled nuclear power facility, off 18% at Y759. General contractors are up on demand expectations from reconstruction efforts; Shimizu (1803.TO) is up 2.9% at Y359, \f28 \uc2 \f2 Kajima\f28 \uc2 \f2  (1812.TO) is up 3.9% at Y239. (kana.inagaki@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0234 GMT [Dow Jones] Japan's earthquake will cause minimal disruption to trade flows in the near term and demand for Australia's exports are poised to grow in the aftermath, says \f28 \uc2 \f2 Moody's\f28 \uc2 \f2  Analytics economist Matthew Circosta. He says Japan's reconstruction will require more steel and demand for agricultural shipments will also grow, while strong fundamentals will continue to support commodity prices. Circosta says the shutdown of the Fukoshima nuclear plant has severely reduced Japan's electricity generation capacity. He expects thermal coal and liquefied natural gas, which make up the bulk of Japan's fuel imports from Australia, to fill the void. Japan's demand for iron ore and coking coal is also tipped to rise as reconstruction gets underway, while Japan's fresh food needs are expected to ensure continued strong markets for Australia's meat, dairy and grain exports. (david.rogers1@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0234 GMT [Dow Jones] A target of any \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  will be to push the USD/JPY above 80.0 and let it stabilize around that level, says Nomura Securities chief \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategist Taisuke Tanaka. Still, large amounts of funds are necessary for \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to fulfill that because hedge-related USD/JPY selling by Japanese exporters is likely to weigh on the pair at 80.00. He also notes joint \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  by \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , U.S. and EU is possible, given the Japan-led equities weakness around the world. He adds after that the crisis is settled, the USD/JPY may stabilize in a 80.00-85.00 band. (takashi.mochizuki@dowjones.com) [ 03-16-11 2317ET ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020110317e73h0002f\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
