m reconstruction efforts; Shimizu (1803.TO) is up 2.9% at Y359, \f28 \uc2 \f2 Kajima\f28 \uc2 \f2  (1812.TO) is up 3.9% at Y239. (kana.inagaki@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0234 GMT [Dow Jones] Japan's earthquake will cause minimal disruption to trade flows in the near term and demand for Australia's exports are poised to grow in the aftermath, says \f28 \uc2 \f2 Moody's\f28 \uc2 \f2  Analytics economist Matthew Circosta. He says Japan's reconstruction will require more steel and demand for agricultural shipments will also grow, while strong fundamentals will continue to support commodity prices. Circosta says the shutdown of the Fukoshima nuclear plant has severely reduced Japan's electricity generation capacity. He expects thermal coal and liquefied natural gas, which make up the bulk of Japan's fuel imports from Australia, to fill the void. Japan's demand for iron ore and coking coal is also tipped to rise as reconstruction gets underway, while Japan's fresh food needs are expected to ensure continued strong markets for Australia's meat, dairy and grain exports. (david.rogers1@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0234 GMT [Dow Jones] A target of any \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  will be to push the USD/JPY above 80.0 and let it stabilize around that level, says Nomura Securities chief \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategist Taisuke Tanaka. Still, large amounts of funds are necessary for \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to fulfill that because hedge-related USD/JPY selling by Japanese exporters is likely to weigh on the pair at 80.00. He also notes joint \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  by \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , U.S. and EU is possible, given the Japan-led equities weakness around the world. He adds after that the crisis is settled, the USD/JPY may stabilize in a 80.00-85.00 band. (takashi.mochizuki@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0231 GMT [Dow Jones] Vietnam shares are down 0.4% at 461.48, with selling in select blue-chip stocks on continued concerns over the negative impact from Japan's earthquake. Dealers say sentiment is also low because a government economist predicts banks will continue to maintain high interest rates in the second quarter because inflation is likely to remain high. "Fears of a withdrawal of Japanese investors and high inflation are discouraging many players from entering the market these days," sayd a Hanoi-based brokerage trader. Among leading decliners, \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bao Viet Holdings\f28 \uc2 \f2  (BVH.VH) is down 4.9% at VND78,000, Vinpearl (VPL.VH) is down 1.6% at VND63,000 and \f28 \uc2 \f2 PetroVietnam\f28 \uc2 \f2  fertilizer (DPM.VH) is down 0.6% at VND36,300. The trader tips immediate support for the main index at 460. (phammuoi.nguyen@dowjones.com) [ 03-16-11 2317ET ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020110317e73h0002e\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
