\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: LME Metals Mostly Higher; Dips Bought - NAB\b0\par\par\uc2 1,712 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2011\par 04:04\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0304 GMT [Dow Jones] LME metals are mostly higher with only copper and tin slightly lower, reflecting the still long-term bullish sentiment in the market says, Ben Westmore, economist at National Australia bank in Melbourne. "Every time you get a significant drop on the Japanese news there are more forward looking partcipants coming in to buy," he says. Despite the gloom hanging over financial markets and concern over growth implications of the disaster, it is clear that Japan reconstruction efforts will be "metals-intensive", says Westmore and that's keeping the market supported. LME 3-month copper is at $9,245/ton, down $15 with 1,905 lots done. LME 3-month aluminum was at $2,480/ton, up $23 with 1,055 lots done. (james.campbell@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 TALK BACK: We invite readers to send us comments and share their views on financial news topics. Please email us at:\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    TalkBackAsia@dowjones.com \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Readers should include their full names, work or home addresses and telephone numbers for verification purposes. We reserve the right to edit and publish your comments along with your name; we reserve the right not to publish reader comments.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0252 GMT [Dow Jones] Indian government bonds are expected to open mildly higher, tracking Wednesday's gains for U.S. Treasurys as the nuclear crisis in Japan unfolds, says a senior dealer with a primary dealership. But gains will be kept in check as market participants tread cautiously ahead of the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Reserve Bank of India\f28 \uc2 \f2 's crucial policy review, with the rate decision due at 0630 GMT later. The most-traded 8.08% 2022 bond may open around 100.05 vs its 99.99 last close. "If there is a 25-basis-point rate hike, as expected by a section of the market, there may be a mild sell-off in bonds. It's difficult to say what direction yields may take because the market is quite divided over the likelihood and quantum of a rate action today," he says. The RBI may raise the key lending and borrowing rates by 25 bps each to 6.75% and 5.75% respectively, according to 14 of the 15 economists polled by Dow Jones. (khushita.vasant@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0252 GMT [Dow Jones] China shares trim earlier losses as short-term funds rush into traditional energy and clear energy related shares after the government suspends its new approvals of nuclear power plants. The Shanghai Composite Index is down 0.1% at 2926.86, off its intraday low of 2886.69; analysts tip the index to consolidate around 2900. "Lingering tightening concerns and uncertainties over Japan's radiation leak are likely to weigh on sentiment," says Li Xin, an analyst from China Development Bank Securities. Among leading decliners, steel makers fall on profit-taking after recent gains; \f28 \uc2 \f2 Wuhan Iron & Steel\f28 \uc2 \f2  (600005.SH) slides 2.6% to CNY4.92, and Baoshan Iron & Steel (600019.SH) eases 2.2% to CNY7.29. Offsetting decliners, thermal and hydro power plants are rising on optimism about the industry's outlook after Beijing suspends its approval of new nuclear power plants Wednesday; China \f28 \uc2 \f2 Yangtze Power\f28 \uc2 \f2  (600900.SH) adds 5.2% to CNY8.12, and Huaneng Power International (600011.SH) climbs 2.2% to CNY5.54. The Shenzhen Composite Index is 0.7% lower at 1299.00. (amy.li@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0250 GMT [Dow Jones] The USD/PHP rises to a seven-week high as weak stocks and the growing uncertainty over Japan's natural disaster and nuclear troubles hurt the local \f28 \b \hi