\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: China Shares Off 0.1%; Power Plays Rise\b0\par\par\uc2 1,814 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2011\par 03:52\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 0252 GMT [Dow Jones] China shares trim earlier losses as short-term funds rush into traditional energy and clear energy related shares after the government suspends its new approvals of nuclear power plants. The Shanghai Composite Index is down 0.1% at 2926.86, off its intraday low of 2886.69; analysts tip the index to consolidate around 2900. "Lingering tightening concerns and uncertainties over Japan's radiation leak are likely to weigh on sentiment," says Li Xin, an analyst from China Development Bank Securities. Among leading decliners, steel makers fall on profit-taking after recent gains; \uc2 Wuhan Iron & Steel\uc2  (600005.SH) slides 2.6% to CNY4.92, and Baoshan Iron & Steel (600019.SH) eases 2.2% to CNY7.29. Offsetting decliners, thermal and hydro power plants are rising on optimism about the industry's outlook after Beijing suspends its approval of new nuclear power plants Wednesday; China \uc2 Yangtze Power\uc2  (600900.SH) adds 5.2% to CNY8.12, and Huaneng Power International (600011.SH) climbs 2.2% to CNY5.54. The Shenzhen Composite Index is 0.7% lower at 1299.00. (amy.li@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0250 GMT [Dow Jones] The USD/PHP rises to a seven-week high as weak stocks and the growing uncertainty over Japan's natural disaster and nuclear troubles hurt the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The pair is at 43.955 in moderate volume after hitting 44.02 earlier (the highest level since early February) vs 43.755 in late Asian trade. Traders place resistance at 44.20 and support at 43.70. "We've yet to see the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 . They're probably allowing the dollar to appreciate ahead of a possible rate hike next week," says a local bank trader. (cris.larano@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0244 GMT [Dow Jones] Japan's earthquake will cause minimal disruption to trade flows in the near term and demand for Australia's exports are poised to grow in the aftermath of the disaster, says \uc2 Moody's\uc2  Analytics economist Matthew Circosta. He says Japan's reconstruction will require more steel-making material and demand for agricultural shipments will also grow, while strong fundamentals will continue to support commodity prices. Circosta says the shutdown of the Fukushima nuclear plant has severely reduced Japan's electricity generation capacity. He expects thermal coal and liquefied natural gas, which make up the bulk of Japan's fuel imports from Australia, to fill the void. Japan's demand for iron ore and coking coal is also tipped to rise as reconstruction gets underway, while Japan's fresh food needs are expected to ensure continued strong markets for Australia's meat, dairy and grain exports. (david.rogers1@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0244 GMT [Dow Jones] The Nikkei is off 2.1% at 8903.86 midday, but well off its intraday low of 8639.56, after Japan's Self Defense Force helicopters started dropping water on overheated reactor units at the troubled Fukushima Daiichi nuclear power plant. Over 1.8 billion shares were traded on the TSE 1st section by the midday break--a robust figure, but well down from the record-breaking levels seen in the prior three session. "The massive trading volume seen over the past few sessions indicates that the market is not lacking buyers," says Yutaka Yoshii, general manager at \uc2 Mito Securities\uc2 , adding some domestic investors seem to be snapping up undervalued shares. Yoshii pegs index support for the afternoon at 8530. All but two of 33 Topix subindexes are lower with \uc2 Tokyo Electric Power\uc2  (9501.TO), operator of the troubled nuclear power facility, off 18% at Y759. General contractors are up on demand expectations from reconstruction efforts; Shimizu (1803.TO) is up 2.9% at Y359, \uc2 Kajima\uc2  (1812.TO) is up 3.9% at Y239. (kana.inagaki@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0234 GMT [Dow Jones] Japan's earthquake will cause minimal disruption to trade flows in the near term and demand for Australia's exports are poised to grow in the aftermath, says \uc2 Moody's\uc2  Analytics economist Matthew Circosta. He says Japan's reconstruction will require more steel and demand for agricultural shipments will also grow, while strong fundamentals will continue to support commodity prices. Circosta says the shutdown of the Fukoshima nuclear plant has severely reduced Japan's electricity generation capacity. He expects thermal coal and liquefied natural gas, which make up the bulk of Japan's fuel imports from Australia, to fill the void. Japan's demand for iron ore and coking coal is also tipped to rise as reconstruction gets underway, while Japan's fresh food needs are expected to ensure continued strong markets for Australia's meat, dairy and grain exports. (david.rogers1@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0234 GMT [Dow Jones] A target of any \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  will be to push the USD/JPY above 80.0 and let it stabilize around that level, says Nomura Securities chief \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist Taisuke Tanaka. Still, large amounts of funds are necessary for \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to fulfill that because hedge-related USD/JPY selling by Japanese exporters is likely to weigh on the pair at 80.00. He also notes joint \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 , U.S. and EU is possible, given the Japan-led equities weakness around the world. He adds after that the crisis is settled, the USD/JPY may stabilize in a 80.00-85.00 band. (takashi.mochizuki@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0231 GMT [Dow Jones] Vietnam shares are down 0.4% at 461.48, with selling in select blue-chip stocks on continued concerns over the negative impact from Japan's earthquake. Dealers say sentiment is also low because a government economist predicts banks will continue to maintain high interest rates in the second quarter because inflation is likely to remain high. "Fears of a withdrawal of Japanese investors and high inflation are discouraging many players from entering the market these days," sayd a Hanoi-based brokerage trader. Among leading decliners, \uc2 Bao Viet Holdings\uc2  (BVH.VH) is down 4.9% at VND78,000, Vinpearl (VPL.VH) is down 1.6% at VND63,000 and \uc2 PetroVietnam\uc2  fertilizer (DPM.VH) is down 0.6% at VND36,300. The trader tips immediate support for the main index at 460. (phammuoi.nguyen@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0231 GMT [Dow Jones] Philippine shares are falling as investors continue to fret about the nuclear situation in Japan. The benchmark PSEi is off 1.4% at 3823.19 in light volume. Losers are swamping gainers 91 to 12. "We will probably see more of this weakness until the uncertainty in Japan recedes," says Bernard Avinante, analyst at Wealth Securities. Traders place immediate support at 3800 and resistance at 3900. Among heavyweight losers, SM Investments (SM.PH) is down 1.0% at PHP510.50, property developer Megaworld (MEG.PH) is down 1.4% at PHP2.13, PLDT (TEL.PH) is down 3.1% at PHP2,006, and Metropolitan Bank & Trust (MBT.PH) is down 0.9% to PHP61.20. (cris.larano@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0219 GMT [Dow Jones] Spot gold is at $1,389.20/oz, down $12.30 since the New York close. Continuing liquidation on Tocom after the USD/JPY lurched lower Wednesday is putting spot under pressure in a similar pattern to Tuesday's trade as Japan investors move to cash. Tocom February 2012 contract is at Y3,561 a gram, down Y85 and has fallen 6.0% since Friday's pre-earthquake levels, reflecting the stronger yen, while spot gold has fallen 1.6%. \uc2 Barclays Capital\uc2  says technically gold has important support at $1,376, a break of which would signal a deeper correction to $1,360 or even $1,325. "Strategically, we are bullish, but there is not enough in the overnight price action to buy for anything other than the short term, as we would like to keep open the option of buying a dip to $1,325," the bank says.(james.campbell@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0218 GMT [Dow Jones] China government bonds are lower after the PBOC raises the size on its routine three-month bills sold in the open market \b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2  to CNY50 billion (from CNY32 billion last week), the largest since late October. "The sizable bill sale amount indicates the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's long-term efforts to mop up flush liquidity," says a Shanghai-based trader with a local fund. Still, he reckons the unchanged yield on the three-month PBOC bills suggests that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  probably has no imminent plan to hike the country's interest rates. The Shanghai Stock Exchange government bond index is at 127.21 vs 127.22 at its last close and it's likely to stay in a 127.15-127.21 band. (ming.wang@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0215 GMT [Dow Jones] New Zealand government bond prices are lower, while interest rate swaps are higher after comments by Finance Minister Bill English on debt ahead of the weekly Debt Management Office tender, a local bond trader says. English said the government would have to borrow the majority of the money needed to rebuild Christchurch and would increase borrowing to NZ$300 million a week. The trader says the weekly bond tender was "a mixed bag" after the comments with the successful yield spread reaching 5 bps. "Since the tender they've (bond yields and swaps) all moved higher to reflect where the average was in the tender." The NZ Debt Management Office sold NZ$75 million of bonds due March 15, 2019, at an average yield of 5.22% and NZ$175 million of May 15, 2021, bonds at a average yield of 5.43%. (lucy.craymer@dowjones.com) [ 03-16-11 2252ET ]\par \par \uc2 0215 GMT [Dow Jones] The USD/JPY's decline to the 76.25 low was mainly due to a lack of JPY-selling investors, says Satoshi Tate, a senior dealer at \uc2 Mizuho Corporate Bank\uc2 . "We no longer have active Japanese long-term investors who have been aggressively buying assets overseas"; he adds "this is the main cause of the dollar falling to a surprising level of Y76.25. Improving these investors' risk-taking appetite is a must for the dollar to recover further." He says \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  MOF \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is possible, "as it makes sense that the authority provides yen-selling liquidity instead of long-term investors." But speculation is mounting over a G-7 teleconference later in the day. "Japan's MOF is unusually quite so far, and since we have the G-7 thing coming up, this could be the calm before the storm. There could be a major surprise from the G-7." He adds liquidity in the market is still thin, so the USD/JPY is likely to fall below 76.25 if there's no \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . (takashi.mochizuki@dowjones.com) [ 03-16-11 2252ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020110317e73h00024\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
