\par\par \b \uc2 WRAPUP 10-G7 cenbanks in rare  \highlight22\uc2 currency \highlight\uc2  action after yen surge\b0\par\par\uc2 1,146 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2011\par 10:23\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * G7 surprise joint \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  sends markets higher\par \par \uc2 * Yen falls across globe as central banks join action\par \par \uc2 * White House got notice of G7 plans early\par \par \uc2 * Japan mulls extreme steps to cope with nuclear crisis\par \par \uc2 (Adds details, analysis, updates market reaction)\par \par \uc2 By Leika Kihara and Kristina Cooke\par \par \uc2 TOKYO/NEW YORK, March 18 (Reuters) - A coordinated move by central banks of rich nations to stabilize the yen appeared to be working on Friday, tamping its value down after Japan's devastating earthquake and nuclear crisis triggered a yen surge and raised fears about the global economy.\par \par \uc2 The action by the Group of Seven, in which they poured billions of dollars into markets, was the first joint \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets since the G7 came to the aid of the newly launched euro in 2000.\par \par \uc2 The U.S. dollar surged two full yen to as much as 81.98 yen <JPY=> in response, up from a record low of 76.25 hit on Thursday. It dropped back to under 81 yen in early afternoon trade but remained above what some analysts suggested was a psychologically important level of 80 that could heighten chances of more \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The yen's surge in the wake of the earthquake and tsunami may have seemed counter-intuitive at first glance, but it reflected, in part, market speculation that Japanese firms and the Japanese government will repatriate money now invested overseas in order to pay insurance claims and the cost of rebuilding.\par \par \uc2 Traders on Friday estimated the Bank of Japan alone bought more than $25 billion, paying with yen to effectively weaken the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's value by boosting the supply.\par \par \uc2 Every member of the G7 was involved in Friday's market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , a massive show of unity designed to brush back speculators who might have been more willing to question the resolve of Japan if it had attempted the effort alone.\par \par \uc2 The U.S. Federal Reserve, Bank of England, \uc2 Bank of Canada\uc2  and European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which represents all 17 countries that use the euro, each separately confirmed they were \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  to keep the yen's value from climbing.\par \par \uc2 The action helped settle equity markets, which had seen heavy selling in volatile trade since the earthquake and tsunami struck Japan, the world's No. 3 economy, last Friday and triggered a crisis at nuclear power plants. Investors have struggled to get a handle on the economic fallout.\par \par \uc2 "The impact on the Japanese economy in isolation is likely to be contained, but the ripple effects could be substantial, and perhaps long-lasting," the Institute of International Finance, which represents global banks, said in a statement.\par \par \uc2 Japan's deputy finance minister, Fumihiko Igarashi, warned that \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  traders shouldn't consider the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  a one-time-only move, pledging that the G7 would "act decisively" again if speculators tried to push the yen higher.\par \par \uc2 "G7 countries agreed that if we caved in to such speculators that took advantage of people's misfortunes, the Japanese economy would be ruined and the whole world economy would be harmed," Igarashi told Reuters in an interview.\par \par \uc2 "Our stance remains unchanged that we will take decisive steps against speculators who act like sneaky thieves at a scene of a fire." [ID:nL3E7EI16G]\par \par \uc2 The G7, in its statement Thursday night announcing the decision to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 , said that "excess volatility and disorderly movements in exchange rates have adverse implications for economic and financial stability." <^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^\par \par \uc2 BreakingViews: Yen \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  may send wrong message\par \par \uc2 [ID:nLDE72H0NT]\par \par \uc2 Insider Video: \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's Vice Finance Minister on Yen\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Intervention\b0 \highlight\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://link.reuters.com/quc68r"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://link.reuters.com/quc68r}}}\par \par \uc2 Insider Video: Yen \b \highlight22\uc2 Intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  Gets Results\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://link.reuters.com/myx58r"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://link.reuters.com/myx58r}}}\uc2  ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^>\par \par \uc2 SHOW OF SOLIDARITY\par \par \uc2 No figures were available for how much the G7 -- Canada, France, Germany, Italy, the United Kingdom, the United States and Japan -- may have spent in total buying dollars, euros and pounds. But analysts said the show of unity was the point.\par \par \uc2 "It's going to have a very huge resonating effect on the market," said Kathy Lien, director of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  research at GFT in New York. "Because the only type of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  that actually works is coordinated \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and it shows the solidarity of all central banks in terms of the severity of the situation in Japan."\par \par \uc2 Japan's Nikkei share index <.N225> climbed close to 3 percent, recouping some of the week's stinging losses after the earthquake, tsunami and nuclear power plant crisis. The Dow Jones industrial average rose about 120 points in early trading but by afternoon had pared gains to trade up about 80 points.\par \par \uc2 A source told Reuters the BOJ would also leave the yen it sold in the banking system rather than mopping it up, thus adding to the vast amount of liquidity it had already provided to support its domestic markets.\par \par \uc2 Central banks will often issue bonds to soak up any extra cash in the economy that results from \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  for fear that the additional liquidity could fuel inflation.\par \par \uc2 WIDER CONCERN\par \par \uc2 G7 financial leaders may be worried that a surge in yen repatriation could create a crisis of confidence in markets already struggling in the face of Europe's debt problems and the impact of unrest in the Middle East.\par \par \uc2 Analysts were watching warily to see whether the impact of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  will be lasting, cautioning that the maximum benefit from it likely would occur on Friday. U.S. officials were maintaining a stoic silence about any further actions.\par \par \uc2 "Usually with each \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , you end up seeing a diminishing marginal return. So the first-time round usually has the biggest impact," said David Morgan, head of research for the Americas at Standard Chartered in New York.\par \par \uc2 "It'll become increasingly difficult for \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to be successful," he added.\par \par \uc2 U.S. Treasury Secretary Timothy Geithner, who heads for Brazil on Friday night with President Barack Obama, spoke with French Finance Minister Christine Lagarde, whose government currently chairs the G7, and Japanese Finance Minister Yoshihiko Noda ahead of Thursday night's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  call.\par \par \uc2 Geithner let Obama know ahead that the G7 was embarking on a coordinated \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The United States has been at pains to stress its willingness to help stricken Japan overcome the triple disaster of the earthquake, tsunami and nuclear crisis.\par \par \uc2 In a measure of the severity of the situation Tokyo now faces, Japanese engineers conceded on Friday that burying a crippled nuclear plant in sand and concrete may be a last resort to prevent a catastrophic radiation release.\par \par \uc2 That was the method used to seal huge leakages from Chernobyl in 1986 and was taken as a sign that Japan's piecemeal actions, such as dumping water from military helicopters or scrambling to restart cooling pumps, may not work.\par \par \uc2 (Additional reporting by Wayne Cole, Wanfeng Zhou, and Daniel Flynn; writing by Glenn Somerville; Editing by Leslie Adler)\par \par \uc2 GLOBAL-ECONOMY/ (WRAPUP 10, PIX)   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020110320e73h0008h\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
