\par\par \b \uc2 WRAPUP 1-G7 agrees joint  \highlight22\uc2 intervention \highlight\uc2  to restrain yen\b0\par\par\uc2 1,082 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2011\par 10:23\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * G7 surprises with joint \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to sell yen\par \par \uc2 * Yen falls, shares rally as BOJ starts to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 * UN approves no-fly zone in Lybia, oil jumps\par \par \uc2 By Wayne Cole\par \par \uc2 SYDNEY, March 18 (Reuters) - The Group of Seven rich nations on Friday agreed to join in rare concerted \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to restrain a run-away yen, hoping to calm global markets after a wild week of often panic selling.\par \par \uc2 The U.S. dollar immediately surged over a full yen to 80.73 yen , leaving behind a record low of 76.25 hit on Thursday. Japan's Nikkei share index climbed 2.5 percent, recouping some of the week's stinging losses.\par \par \uc2 The G7 agreement to jointly \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to sell yen came as a surprise to many as sources had suggested they would only give Japan a green light to go it alone.\par \par \uc2 Japan's Finance Minister Yoshihiko Noda said the Bank of Japan had begun to sell yen at 0000 GMT and other central banks from the G7 would \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  as their markets opened.\par \par \uc2 "This is the first coordinated \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  that we have seen since 2000 so it's going to have a very huge resonating effect on the market," said Kathy Lien, director of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  research at GFT in New York.\par \par \uc2 "Because the only type of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  that actually works is coordinated \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and it shows the solidarity of all central banks in terms of the severity of the situation in Japan."\par \par \uc2 On Thursday, the yen soared to a record high of 76.25 per dollar, eclipsing its historical peak of 79.75 hit in the aftermath of the Kobe earthquake.\par \par \uc2 A strong yen could make it more difficult for the heavily export-dependent Japanese economy to recover from the triple blow of last week's earthquake, tsunami and nuclear threat. The damage toll is already estimated at up to $200 billion with Japan almost certain to slip back into recession.\par \par \uc2 G7 financial leaders may also be worried that a surge in yen repatriation could unsettle global markets, creating a crisis of confidence that spreads from Asia to Europe and the United States.\par \par \uc2 Investors were also keeping a wary eye on events in Libya as the United Nations voted to impose a no-fly zone over the country and use all necessary measures to protect civilians. French diplomatic sources said military action could begin within hours of the Security Council vote.\par \par \uc2 Oil prices were up over $2 a barrel on the decision, which was seen as risking prolonging the conflict in the North African nation.\par \par \uc2 HISTORY NOT IN G7's FAVOUR\par \par \uc2 Still, if past is prologue, even massive official selling might not restrain the yen for long.\par \par \uc2 When \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  last \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in September 2010 it sold a huge 2.1 trillion yen, or around $25 billion back then, but only managed to push the dollar up from 82.85 to 85.77 yen.\par \par \uc2 The shock value quickly faded and by late October the dollar was down around 80.00.\par \par \uc2 "History isn't on the G7's side," said John Normand, a \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  analyst at \uc2 JPMorgan\uc2 , noting past acts of concerted \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  only worked when backed by \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  tightening.\par \par \uc2 In this case, there is almost no chance of the Federal Reserve tightening for months to come. The European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has signalled an intent to hike rates in April, but that might not help the dollar against the yen.\par \par \uc2 "The G7 can be a market mover initially, but it shouldn't be a trend-changer any more than the September 2010 yen \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was," argued Normand.\par \par \uc2 The G7 comprises Canada, France, Germany, Italy, Japan, the United Kingdom and United States.\par \par \uc2 HEIGHTENED ANXIETY\par \par \uc2 Late Thursday, President Barack Obama said the United States will do all it can to help Japan recover while playing down fears a drifting cloud of radiation could reach the U.S. West Coast.\par \par \uc2 Rising alarm over the unfolding disaster in the world's third-largest economy has sent shudders through markets, hitting shares and commodities, as investors sought the safe haven of government debt.\par \par \uc2 Japanese military helicopters and fire trucks doused an overheating nuclear plant in the northeast of the country with water on Thursday to try to limit the damage from the world's worst nuclear accident since Chernobyl in 1986.\par \par \uc2 The G7 call and Obama's statement suggest a heightened degree of concern among top policymakers at the threat posed by the disaster at a time when the global economy is still recovering from its worst downturn in nearly 80 years.\par \par \uc2 Europe continues to wrestle with a crippling debt crisis, and the Fed is buying up domestic government debt to safeguard a stop-start economic bounce back in the United States.\par \par \uc2 "I think the world economy is going to go right down, and it has happened at a time when financial markets are still fragile," said a G7 central banker who declined to be named.\par \par \uc2 Japan's triple disaster, unprecedented in a major developed economy, is already disrupting global manufacturing.\par \par \uc2 Makers of equipment for mobile telephones to car makers and chipmakers have warned of a squeeze on their businesses given Japan's crucial role in many supply chains that keep global commerce ticking over.\par \par \uc2 The technology sector felt an immediate impact after Friday's quake and tsunami since Japan makes around a fifth of the world's semiconductors.\par \par \uc2 Economists fear an extended slump for the world's third-biggest economy.\par \par \uc2 The sheer complexity of the damages makes it difficult to grasp the impact of the earthquake," says Kyohei Morita, an economist at \uc2 Barclays Capital\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "Indeed, an analysis of the domestic real economy alone requires an assessment not only of building damages but also lifeline disruptions, planned blackouts/voluntary energy conservation and the state of nuclear power generation."\par \par \uc2 The Nikkei newspaper on Friday reported the government was considering mandatory power usage cuts for businesses and households to avert a major blackout in greater Tokyo.\par \par \uc2 The government also announced plans to issue 10 trillion yen in emergency bonds to fund reconstruction, adding to an already massive mountain of public debt.\par \par \uc2 Still, the effect on global growth may be more limited. \uc2 BNP Paribas\uc2  estimates the disaster will shave 3 percent from Japan's projected GDP this year. That would account for just 0.2 percent of world output.\par \par \uc2 (Additional reporting by Leika Kihara, Daniel Flynn, Glenn Sommerville and Lesley Wroughton; Editing by Ed Davies)\par \par \uc2 GLOBAL-ECONOMY/ (WRAPUP 1, PIX)   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020110320e73h0008f\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
