\par\par \b \uc2 ASIAN MORNING BRIEFING: US Stocks End Higher; Eyes On G-7 Teleconference\b0\par\par\uc2 2,627 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2011\par 23:03\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par DJIA      11774.59  +161.29  +1.39% \par Nasdaq     2636.05   +19.23  +0.73% \par S&P 500    1273.72   +16.84  +1.34% \par FTSE 100   5696.11   +97.88  +1.75% \par Xetra DAX  6656.88  +143.04  +2.20% \par  \par 2Y Tsy  100 3/32  -1/32 0.581% +2.0BP \par 5Y Tsy  101 2/32  -6/32 1.897% +4.1BP \par 10Y Tsy 103 6/32 -11/32 3.244% +4.0BP \par  \par USD/JPY       78.91-95     -1.15% \par Range         79.74-77.16 \par EUR/USD       1.4022-27    +0.93% \par Range         1.4052-1.3868 \par AUD/USD       0.9801-06    -0.35% \par Range         0.9873-0.9704 \par  \par USD/JPY 1M Vol   16.90/17.50% \par EUR/USD 1M Vol   10.85/11.75% \par AUD/USD 1M Vol   14.35/14.65% \par  \par Comex Gold          $1,404.20   +$8.10 \par Comex Gold Range    $1,386.80-$1,405.40 \par Nymex Crude (NY)     $101.42   +$3.44 \par  \par  \par U.S. SUMMARY & ASIAN OUTLOOK: \par STOCKS: \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 U.S. stocks Thursday closed out their first day of gains this week as rising oil prices boosted energy companies and efforts to tame Japan's damaged nuclear-power plant eased some fears over the crisis.\f28 \par \par \uc2 After falling 3.6% in the week's first three sessions, the Dow Jones Industrial Average closed up 161.29 points, or 1.39%, to 11774.59, its biggest point and percentage gain in two weeks.\par \par \uc2 The Nasdaq Composite gained 19.23 points, or 0.73%, to 2636.05. The \uc2 Standard & Poor's\uc2  500-share index added 16.84, or 1.34%, to 1273.72, led by its energy sector.\par \par \uc2 The market's advances came as Japanese authorities claimed modest gains Thursday in their efforts to tame the Fukushima Daiichi nuclear-power complex by wetting it with some 100 tons of seawater using helicopters and fire trucks, an urgent effort to combat rising temperatures in two storage pools for spent nuclear fuel. Radiation levels at the site fell slightly and a \uc2 Tokyo Electric Power Co\uc2 . executive said \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  were "a good first step" in bringing the reactors under control.\par \par \uc2 U.S. investors said the day's gains were unsurprising after the three-day slump.\par \par \uc2 "The market was due for a bit of a bounce, given how negative the sentiment got and how violent the downdraft was," said Carlton Neel, senior managing director at Virtus Investment Advisers. "What we're starting to see is the recognition that the rebuilding effort in Japan is going to take an enormous amount of resources, both human resources and natural resources."\par \par \uc2 Market watchers said stocks were posting sharp swings as investors reacted to a flow of galvanizing headlines. Thursday's session marked the third day of triple-digit point moves.\par \par \uc2 "You're going to have continued volatility in both directions and today just happens to be on the upside," said Alan Lancz, president at Alan B. Lancz & Associates.\par \par \uc2 Energy companies gained as crude-oil prices jumped more than 3.5%. Oil-and-gas exploration and production company \uc2 Noble Energy\uc2  jumped 5.3%, while oilfield-services company Schlumberger rose 4.8% and drilling contractor Rowan gained 4.8%.\par \par \uc2 Boosting natural-gas explorers, the head of the U.S. Energy Information Administration said Japan is likely to turn to liquefied natural gas to meet its energy needs as it recovers from the earthquake. Northern Oil & Gas rose 5.4%, Pioneer Drilling added 6.4% and \uc2 Oceaneering International\uc2  gained 4.4%.\par \par \uc2 Industrials also climbed, lifted by a 3.1% jump in international package shipper \uc2 FedEx\uc2  after its fourth-quarter earnings target topped analysts' mean estimate.\par \par \uc2 Tokyo shares fell for the third time in four sessions Thursday as investors remained nervous, even as the market pared losses in the afternoon on hopes Japanese officials were making progress in averting a full-blown nuclear disaster.\par \par \uc2 Yumi Nishimura, deputy general manager of investment strategy at Daiwa Securities Capital Markets, said that expectations are growing that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  may \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, noting comments from Japanese Finance Minister Noda earlier Thursday that the Group of Seven nations will discuss via teleconference Friday morning the impact of the massive earthquake on the Japanese economy and financial markets.\par \par \uc2 "The massive trading volume seen over the past few sessions indicates the market is not lacking buyers," said Yutaka Yoshii, general manager at \uc2 Mito Securities\uc2 , adding that some domestic investors seem to be snapping up undervalued shares.\par \par \uc2 China's shares ended lower Thursday because of renewed concerns about further monetary tightening amid strong inflation pressures and uncertainties overseas due to the Japanese nuclear crisis and unrest in the Middle East.\par \par \uc2 Analysts said they expect the benchmark index to hover around the 2900 level Friday because of concerns Beijing could introduce further tightening measures during the weekend and uncertainties abroad.\par \par \uc2 "Investors would prefer to wait and see whether the government will launch any monetary tightening moves over the upcoming weekend, especially after the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  raised the yield on its one-year bills on Tuesday," said Xu Yinhui, an analyst at Guotai Junan Securities.\par \par \uc2 Zhou Lin, an analyst at Huatai Securities, said: "The market is likely to trade in a 2800-3000 range in the short run as the real scale of Japan's radiation leak remains unclear."\par \par \uc2 Hong Kong shares ended lower Thursday amid broad-based losses as investors remained concerned about the unfolding nuclear crisis in Japan.\par \par \uc2 Analysts said they expect investors to be tempted to buy again at the 22,000-point level. However they said that if the situation in Japan worsens dramatically, the index could fall to much lower levels.\par \par \uc2 Daniel So, an analyst at Sun Hung Kai Financial, said: "It's still early to tell if the nuclear plant crisis in Japan could be resolved in the near term and I think the selloff in regional markets could persist ahead."\par \par \uc2 Oil futures surged past $101 a barrel in U.S. trading Thursday as Japan claimed progress in controlling the crisis at a damaged nuclear plant and Western countries appeared closer to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Libya.\par \par \uc2 Gold ended slightly higher on the Comex division of the \uc2 New York Mercantile Exchange\uc2  Thursday alongside firmer equity markets, as traders divided attention between Japan's nuclear crisis and political instability in the Middle East.\par \par \uc2 Base metals closed higher on the London Metal Exchange Thursday as the U.S. dollar weakened and traders tipped tighter markets for the industrial commodities following last week's earthquake and tsunami in Japan.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 : \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The yen fell from a record high against the dollar Thursday, as demand for the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the wake of Japan's nuclear crisis was temporarily squelched by fears that officials may sell yen to curb its strength.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In a seemingly counterintuitive move, demand for the yen has surged as Japan's natural disaster has rapidly transformed into a nuclear crisis that has roiled markets worldwide. Thursday, the yen's broad strength hammered the dollar to a historical low at Y76.25, and sent the euro to its lowest level since September at Y106.50.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In the last week alone, the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has appreciated by more than 8% amid speculation that Japanese institutions were selling foreign assets in order to send money back to the disaster-stricken country. Thursday, those flows gave way to concerns that \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  might \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 --perhaps with the help of the world's largest industrialized nations.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Analysts, however, are sharply divided on the nature of the investment flows behind the surging yen. While most acknowledge that Japanese repatriation is supporting the yen, they add that widespread risk aversion was leading some investors to unwind "carry trades" that use the low-yielding Japanese \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to fund high-return, riskier positions elsewhere.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "There will be some repatriation but this sort of trade is overblown and ridiculous," said Jessica Hoversen, fixed income and \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  analyst at \f28 \uc2 \f2 MF Global\f28 \uc2 \f2  in Chicago.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "If [\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's nuclear crisis] stabilizes and we see coordinated \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , dollar/yen will stabilize and start to move higher," she said, adding that Japan's woeful economic fundamentals bode for a weaker \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Late Thursday, the dollar was at Y78.93 from about Y79.60 late Wednesday, according to EBS via CQG, while the euro was at Y110.58 from Y110.66. The euro was at $1.4020 from $1.3902 late Wednesday. The U.K. pound was at $1.6137 from $1.6017. The dollar was at CHF0.8987 from CHF0.9081.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The ICE Dollar Index, which tracks the U.S. dollar against a trade-weighted basket of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , was at about 75.968 from about 76.681.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japan's export-reliant economy will need a weak yen to help offset the effects of the disaster that struck last week. Accordingly, market participants are on high alert for yen-selling \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . Traders were awaiting the outcome of a teleconference among Group of Seven finance ministers Thursday for indications about Japan's intentions in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Indeed, a person familiar with the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  told Dow Jones Newswires that Japanese authorities were "prepared for battle" against emboldened traders determined to buy yen.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Analysts were struck by the swiftness of the yen's rise, which pushed the dollar down dramatically after Wednesday's trading session had drawn to a close. Once the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  pair broke below its 1995 low around Y79.75, sell orders appeared to cascade.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "It was a stop-loss fest," quipped John McCarthy, managing director of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  at ING Capital Markets in New York. But he added the move wasn't entirely unprecedented, as dramatic moves happen once or twice a year "in this twilight zone."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par BONDS: \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Treasury bonds snapped a three-day rally Thursday as fear over Japan's nuclear crisis eased, spurring a relief rally in U.S. stocks. [ 17-03-11 2203GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Demand for safe assets also wilted on U.S. data showing improvement in the labor market and strong manufacturing growth in the Middle Atlantic states. An uptick in consumer prices suggested budding risks of inflation, which is the main threat to bonds' fixed returns over time.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "The world appears to not be coming to an end today," said Alan De Rose, head Treasury trader at Oppenheimer and Co. Inc. New York.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In late afternoon trade Thursday in New York, the benchmark 10-year note was 11/32 lower to yield 3.244%. Bond prices move inversely to their yields. The 30-year bond was 22/32 lower to yield 4.422%. The two-year note was 1/32 lower to yield 0.581%.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japanese authorities claimed modest gains Thursday in their efforts to tame the Fukushima Daiichi nuclear-power complex with 100 tons of seawater using helicopters and fire trucks.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 But traders cautioned that the bond market could see two-way flows in the short term because of the uncertainty about the radiation from Japan's damaged nuclear plant and the ongoing political turmoil in the Middle East and North Africa. Besides the clashes in Bahrain, Libya warned Thursday it could target military and civilian air and sea traffic in the Mediterranean should there be a foreign military \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to aid anti-government rebels.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "It has been a mixed bag. The U.S. economic data have been decent, but there is a lot of uncertainty about Japan," said Srini Ramaswamy, head of liquid markets strategy at \f28 \uc2 \f2 J.P. Morgan\f28 \uc2 \f2  in New York. "That means the bond market will continue to see a lot of volatility in the short term."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Ramaswamy said investors likely prefer staying on the sidelines, contributing to less liquid conditions that can produce bigger swings in the market.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japanese government bond futures fell slightly in a volatile market Thursday, as the pace of falls in the Nikkei Stock Average and the dollar/yen slowed.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "As players are not sure which way they should go, amid the high uncertainty (regarding the impact of the record quake in Japan), moves tend to be one-sided" as players tend to follow each other, said Akito Fukunaga, chief rates strategist at RBS Securities Japan.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 But Fukunaga expects the JGB market to stabilize next week, with a solid bias. "If you think about fiscal and economic downside risks (from the earthquake), deterioration in the domestic economy is likely to be a more immediate concern."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Some market participants are worried about JGB supply as the government is likely to be forced to issue more debt to pay for post-quake reconstruction.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 UBS Securities strategist Atsushi Ito estimates a supplementary reconstruction budget to be around Y5.8 trillion, funded by Y3.6 trillion in over-budget JGB issuance in this fiscal year, Y1.2 trillion in reserves and Y1 trillion from reduced spending by scaling back the child allowance and shelving the plan to abolish expressway tolls.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Despite fears of oversupply, hopes for additional easing by the Bank of Japan is expected to support shorter-end JGBs, and Wednesday's 20-year sale confirmed solid demand for superlong bonds with high yields, Ito said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "We therefore do not anticipate any major rise in yields beyond current levels when the first supplementary budget is compiled," he said. But "the government will need to maintain its focus on fiscal reform by formally revising the (medium-term fiscal framework), since the current spending cap of Y71 trillion will be shattered."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China government bonds ended flat Friday after the PBOC left the yield on three-month bills in its regular open market \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operation\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  unchanged.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Both the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and bond investors think the current market rates are suitable and acceptable. I expect the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  bill yields are likely to stay firm in the coming weeks," a Shanghai-based trader with a local insurer said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Shanghai exchange government bond index is forecast to consolidate in a 127.20-127.30 band Friday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par TOP HEADLINES: \par Japan Claims Modest Gains In Bid To Cool Nuclear Plant \par US Consumer Prices Rise On Food, Gas \par Japan MoF 'Ready For Battle' On Yen \par BOJ Gov Shirakawa, Japan Fin Min Noda To Speak To Reporters After G-7 Teleconference -MOF \par G-7 Finance Chiefs To Support \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan Forex Interventions\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  - Nikkei \par Gadhafi Calls For Ceasefire Sunday \par Fed To OK Some Bank Dividend Hikes \par Prosecutors Say Rajaratnam Had Deal Info \par States To Start Talks With Mortgage Servicers \par FDIC Sues Ex-\f28 \uc2 \f2 WaMu\f28 \uc2 \f2  Execs And Their Wives \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 Nike\f28 \uc2 \f2  Results Miss Views; Margins Weak \par Japan Quake Poses Supply Issues For Apple iPad 2 \par GM To Shut Down Us Plant Due To Parts Shortage \par Obama Orders Review Of US Nuclear Safety \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 General Mills\f28 \uc2 \f2  Expected To Buy PAI's Yoplait Stake \par IMF: Japan Has Not Asked For Fund Financial Assistance \par Pimco CEO: No Asia Redemptions Since Japan Quake -Bloomberg TV \par Locke: China Must Advance On Intellectual-Property Rights \par WSJ: Japan Quake Exposes Risks In Global Supply Chain \par  \par  \par SCHEDULE OF MAIN EVENTS (times in GMT): \par 0001  UK           Feb Nationwide Consumer Confidence Index \par 0500  Japan        Jan Indexes of Business Conditions - Revision \par 0500  Japan        Feb Steel Production \par 0700  Germany      Feb PPI \par 0730  Thailand