\par\par \b \uc2 News Highlights: Top Economic Stories Of The Day\b0\par\par\uc2 782 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2011\par 22:00\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par TOP STORIES \par  \par JAPAN CLAIMS MODEST GAINS IN BID TO COOL NUCLEAR PLANT \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japanese authorities claim modest gains in efforts to tame a heavily damaged nuclear-power plant that has caused worries around the world, but increasing international skepticism and troublesome levels of radiation on the ground underscore the ongoing difficulties.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par US STOCKS END HIGHER FOR FIRST TIME IN FOUR DAYS \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 U.S. stocks close out their first day of gains this week as rising oil prices boosted energy companies and efforts to tame Japan's damaged nuclear-power plant eased some fears over the crisis. DJIA rises 161 points.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par US CONSUMER PRICES RISE ON FOOD, GAS \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Americans pay more for food and gasoline in February, but underlying inflation is tame, new data show, letting the Federal Reserve keep its easy-money policies. Idled U.S. workers also file fewer new jobless benefit claims.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par JAPAN MOF 'READY FOR BATTLE' ON YEN \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japan's Finance Ministry is being increasingly vigilant against the yen's rise ahead of a teleconference among Group of Seven finance ministers, amid threats of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to weaken the yen.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par GADHAFI CALLS FOR CEASEFIRE SUNDAY \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Libya's army says it will halt \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  starting Sunday to allow rebels to lay down their arms, softening repeated threats by Moammar Gadhafi to crush them, as world powers edges towards adopting tough measures to shut down the strongman's military machine.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par FED TO OK SOME BANK DIVIDEND HIKES \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Federal Reserve intends to inform some U.S. banks tomorrow that they passed a new round of stress tests and are free to raise dividends for first time since the financial crisis, sources tell The Wall Street Journal.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par PROSECUTORS SAY RAJARATNAM HAD DEAL INFO \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Galleon founder Raj Rajaratnam had exact details of an impending acquisition of PeopleSupport, a company where he had installed a board member, five days before the deal was announced, according to a phone conversation.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par STATES TO START TALKS WITH MORTGAGE SERVICERS \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 State attorneys general expect to soon begin negotiations aimed at hammering out a settlement that would resolve their investigation of questionable mortgage-servicing practices.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par FDIC SUES EX-WAMU EXECS AND THEIR WIVES \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 FDIC sues three former executives of the failed Washington Mutual Bank, along with two of their wives. FDIC seeks $900 million in damages for alleged gross negligence and other failures by the former executives.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 GENERAL MILLS\f28 \uc2 \f2  EXPECTED TO BUY PAI'S YOPLAIT STAKE \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 General Mills\f28 \uc2 \f2  is set to buy PAI's 51% stake in French dairy company Yoplait for EUR1.6 billion, a person familiar with the situation says. U.S. food company is one of seven bidders that remain in talks. \f28 \uc2 \f2 PAI Partners\f28 \uc2 \f2  declines to comment.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par  \par  \par    ======== DOW JONES NEWSWIRES ANALYSIS AND COMMENTARY ======== \par  \par Sports \par MOREHEAD STATE UPSETS LOUISVILLE \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Morehead State upsets Louisville, 62-61. Meanwhile, eighth-seeded Butler stages a 60-58 victory over Old Dominion, while No. 5 seed West Virginia downs Clemson 84-76 in the NCAA tournament. Kentucky, leads Princeton 34-33 at half.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par The Wall Street Journal \par JAPAN TAXPAYERS SEEN PAYING DAMAGES FROM REACTORS \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Once the crisis at Japan's crippled nuclear reactors ends and the time comes to allocate financial responsibility, there is likely to be only one party holding the bag: the Japanese taxpayer. Andrew Dowell and Erik Holm explain.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par INFLATION IS SLOWLY CREEPING BACK INTO OUTLOOK \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 While inflation is unlikely to become rampant, consumers are lifting their inflation expectations, and more businesses are marking up their selling prices to recoup input costs, writes Kathleen Madigan.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Fed Watch \par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 OPERATION\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  TWIST HINTED AT QE2'S MODEST MARKET IMPACT \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 A new look at what the Treasury and Fed achieved when they attempted to push long-term bond yields down during the Kennedy Administration helps explain why the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  doesn't appear to be getting all that much out of its current Treasury-buying effort, writes Michael Derby.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par   ============ MARKETS ACTION ============ \par  \par US STOCKS CLOSE HIGHER FOR FIRST TIME IN FOUR DAYS \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Shares close higher, with energy and industrial companies strong.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par TREASURYS SNAP 3-DAY GAINS; VANGUARD BETS BOND PRICES TO FALL \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Treasurys snap a three-day rally as fears over Japan nuclear crisis ease.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par DOLLAR MOVES HIGHER VS YEN ON REPORT G-7 WILL SUPPORT \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 JAPAN INTERVENTION\f28 \b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 \f2 Dollar moves higher against the yen after a report that G-7 leaders will support \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's possible \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par OIL SURGES AS LIBYA FLARES, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 JAPAN\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  CRISIS EBBS \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Crude oil surges past $100 as Japan claims progress in controlling nuclear crisis and Western countries appear closer to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervening\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in Libya.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par GOLD ABOVE $1,400 ON EQUITIES, MIDDLE EAST \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Comex gold edges up as traders eye Japan nuclear crisis, Mideast political instability. [ 17-03-11 2100GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 1030 1804 4180   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020110317e73h001id\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
