\par\par \b \uc2 News Highlights: Top Economic Stories Of The Day\b0\par\par\uc2 777 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2011\par 19:01\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par TOP STORIES \par  \par JAPAN PUSHES TO TAME PLANT \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Japanese government renews efforts to tame the nuclear power plant at the center of Japan's nuclear crisis after suffering some delays, even as it waited for results of its attempt to cool pools of hot nuclear waste on the scene with seawater carried by helicopter.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par US STOCKS CLIMB AS INDUSTRIALS, ENERGY REBOUND \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 U.S. stocks rebound as bets that rebuilding efforts in Japan could lift energy and industrial companies offsets anxieties around the country's nuclear power crisis. The Dow Jones Industrial Average is up more than 100 points.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par US CONSUMER PRICES RISE ON FOOD, GAS \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Americans pay more for food and gasoline in February, but underlying inflation is tame, new data show, letting the Federal Reserve keep its easy-money policies. Idled U.S. workers also file fewer new jobless benefit claims.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par JAPAN MOF 'READY FOR BATTLE' ON YEN \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japan's Finance Ministry is being increasingly vigilant against the yen's rise ahead of a teleconference among Group of Seven finance ministers, amid threats of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to weaken the yen.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par GADHAFI CALLS CEASEFIRE AS TOUGH UN ACTION LOOMS \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Libya's army says it will halt \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  starting Sunday to allow rebels to lay down their arms, softening repeated threats by Moammar Gadhafi to crush them, as world powers edges towards adopting tough measures to shut down the strongman's military machine.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 GENERAL MILLS\f28 \uc2 \f2  TO BUY PAI'S YOPLAIT STAKE FOR EUR1.6B \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 General Mills\f28 \uc2 \f2  is set to buy PAI's 51% stake in French dairy company Yoplait for EUR1.6 billion, a person familiar with the situation says. The U.S. food company is one of seven bidders that remain in talks. Shares decline 1%.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par MERKEL SIGNALS FASTER EXIT FROM NUCLEAR POWER \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The German government will present a clear timetable for a change in energy policy away from nuclear power and into renewables, Chancellor Angela Merkel says, signalling that the European powerhouse may speed up its exit from nuclear power.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par VANGUARD SIDES WITH PIMCO, AGAINST BOND PRICES \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 As many investors scoop up safe-haven U.S. Treasury bonds amid Japan's nuclear crisis, Vanguard Group, one of the world's biggest asset-management firms, is betting that these securities' prices will fall.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par CORPORATE BLACKBERRY USERS OFFERED CLOUD SERVICES \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Research In Motion will give its corporate customers an opportunity to shift back-office management of email traffic and other BlackBerry services off-site, in a move that it says will save customers money.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par CRITICS PRESS OBAMA ON CRISES \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 President Barack Obama is hearing from a growing set of critics, many of them GOP leaders, who say he is taking a hands-off approach to the spending debate in Congress and keeping a low profile on the turmoil in Libya and Bahrain.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par  \par  \par    ======== DOW JONES NEWSWIRES ANALYSIS AND COMMENTARY ======== \par  \par The Wall Street Journal \par JAPAN TAXPAYERS SEEN PAYING DAMAGES FROM REACTORS \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Once the crisis at Japan's crippled nuclear reactors ends and the time comes to allocate financial responsibility, there is likely to be only one party holding the bag: the Japanese taxpayer. Andrew Dowell and Erik Holm explain.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par INFLATION IS SLOWLY CREEPING BACK INTO OUTLOOK \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 While inflation is unlikely to become rampant, consumers are lifting their inflation expectations, and more businesses are marking up their selling prices to recoup input costs, writes Kathleen Madigan.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Fed Watch \par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 OPERATION\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  TWIST HINTED AT QE2'S MODEST MARKET IMPACT \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 A new look at what the Treasury and Fed achieved when they attempted to push long-term bond yields down during the Kennedy Administration helps explain why the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  doesn't appear to be getting all that much out of its current Treasury-buying effort, writes Michael Derby.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Sports \par CLEMSON, WEST VA KICK OFF NCAA TOURNEY \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Clemson starts strong against West Virginia, but the Mountaineers go on an 11-1 run to tie it at 40 a piece at the end of the first half. Butler and Old Dominion are locked in a tight one early.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par   ============ MARKETS ACTION ============ \par  \par US STOCKS CLIMB AS BATTERED ENERGY, INDUSTRIALS REBOUND \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Shares rebound, led higher by energy and industrial companies.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par TREASURYS SLIDE FURTHER AFTER LOFTY PHILLY FED \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Treasurys slide further after Philly Fed.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par DOLLAR MOVES HIGHER VS YEN ON REPORT G-7 WILL SUPPORT \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 JAPAN INTERVENTION\f28 \b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 \f2 Dollar moves higher against the yen after a report that G-7 leaders will support \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's possible \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par CRUDE BETWEEN CRISES IN \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 JAPAN\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , MIDDLE EAST \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Crude oil advances back above $100 as Japan fears abate.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par GOLD FLAT AS TRADERS WATCH JAPAN, EQUITIES \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Comex gold holds near steady on signs of resilience in other markets. [ 17-03-11 1801GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 1030 1804 4180   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020110317e73h001fn\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
